const LECTURAS = {
 "hf-platon": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 6",
  "title": "Plato: Republic VII",
  "html": "<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-0-el-texto-original-completo\">Part 0. The complete original\ntext</h2>\n<p><strong>Note on the translation.</strong> Our own translation from the Greek text of J. Burnet (Platonis Opera, Oxford, 1902), checked against that of Patricio de Azcárate (Madrid, 1872). Text of chapters\nI-V, XIII and XIV, the ones set as required reading.</p>\n<h3 id=\"i\">I.</h3>\n<p>—After this —I said— compare our nature, as regards education and the lack of it, with a situation like this. Picture some men in an underground dwelling in the form of a cave, whose entrance, a long one, opens to the light and runs the whole length of the cave. They have been there since childhood, bound by chains on their legs and necks, forced to stay still and to look only straight ahead, unable to turn their heads because of the bonds. Behind them, high up and far off, burns the light of a fire; and between the fire and the prisoners a raised path, along which imagine a low wall built, like the screen that puppeteers set in front of the audience to show their marvels above it.</p>\n<p>—I see it —he said.</p>\n<p>—Now look, along that wall, at some men carrying all sorts of objects that stick up above it: figures of men and of animals, made of stone, of wood and of the most varied materials. Naturally, among those carrying them some are talking and others are silent.</p>\n<p>—A strange image you describe —he said— and strange prisoners.</p>\n<p>—Like ourselves —I said—. And, to begin with, do you think that men like these have seen, of themselves and of their companions, anything other than the shadows that the fire casts on the wall of the cave in front of them?</p>\n<p>—How could they see it —he said—, if all their lives they are forced to keep their heads still?</p>\n<p>—And of the objects that pass by? Is it not the same?</p>\n<p>—What else could it be?</p>\n<p>—And if they could talk to one another, do you not think they would take those shadows they saw passing for real things?</p>\n<p>—They could not but do so.</p>\n<p>—And what if the prison also had an echo coming from the wall in front? Each time one of those passing by spoke, do you think they would attribute the voice to anything other than the shadow they see passing?</p>\n<p>—No, by Zeus! —he said.</p>\n<p>—Then, beyond any doubt —I said—, men like these would hold nothing to be true but the shadows of the manufactured objects.</p>\n<p>—That is wholly inevitable —he said.</p>\n<p>—Now consider —I said— what would become of them if they were freed from the chains and cured of their folly, supposing that, by nature, the following were to happen to them. When one of them was released and suddenly forced to stand up, to turn his neck, to walk and to look towards the light, in doing all this he would feel pain and, dazzled, would be unable to make out those objects whose shadows he had seen before. And if someone told him that before he saw nothing but trifles, and that now, closer to what is real and turned towards more real things, he sees more rightly; and if, pointing to each of the objects passing by, that person forced him to answer what each one is, what do you think he would say? Do you not think he would be at a loss and would hold what he saw before to be truer than what is now shown to him?</p>\n<p>—Much truer —he said.</p><h3 id=\"ii\">II.</h3>\n<p>—And if he were forced to look at the light itself? Would his eyes not hurt, and would he not flee, turning back towards those things he can make out, convinced that these are indeed clearer than what is shown to him?</p>\n<p>—So it is —he said.</p>\n<p>—And if from there —I went on— someone dragged him by force up the rough, steep slope, and did not let go of him until he had pulled him out into the light of the sun, would he not suffer and be vexed as he was dragged, and, on reaching the light, with his eyes blinded by the glare, would he not be unable to make out any of what we now hold to be true?</p>\n<p>—No, at least not all at once —he said.</p>\n<p>—He would need to grow used to it, I think, in order to be able to look at the things above. At first he would make out the shadows more easily; then the figures of men and of other things reflected in the water; later, the things themselves. And after that, it would be easier for him to contemplate by night what is in the sky and the sky itself, looking at the light of the stars and the moon, than to see by day the sun and its light.</p>\n<p>—How could it be otherwise?</p>\n<p>—And finally, I believe, he would be able to look at the sun —not its reflections in the water nor anywhere else foreign to it, but the sun itself, in its own place— and contemplate it as it is.</p>\n<p>—Necessarily —he said.</p>\n<p>—And next he would conclude about it that it is what produces the seasons and the years, what governs everything in the visible region and, in a certain way, the cause of all that they used to see.</p>\n<p>—It is plain —he said— that, after that, he would come to this.</p>\n<p>—And then? When he remembered his first dwelling, the kind of knowledge proper to that place and those who shared the prison with him, do you not think he would count himself fortunate at the change and would pity them?</p>\n<p>—Very much so.</p>\n<p>—And if among them honours, praises and rewards were then granted to whoever most sharply made out the passing shadows, best remembered which ones used to go in front, which behind and which together, and was thus the most able to foretell what was to come, do you think our man would feel any longing for that and would envy those who there received honours and power? Or would he not rather feel what Homer says, and prefer with all his might ‘to till the soil in the service of another, of a man without estate’, and to endure anything rather than return to those opinions and to that life?</p>\n<p>—So I believe —he said—: he would agree to suffer anything rather than live in that way.</p>\n<p>—And now note this —I said—: if that man went down again and sat in his old seat, would his eyes not fill with darkness on coming suddenly out of the sun?</p>\n<p>—Certainly —he said.</p>\n<p>—And if he had to go back to judging those shadows, competing with those who remain in chains, while his sight is still poor because his eyes have not readjusted —and the time this took him would not be short—, would he not provoke laughter, and would they not say of him that, for having gone up, he has come back with his sight ruined, and that it is not even worth trying to make the ascent? And anyone who tried to release them and lead them upward, would they not kill him, if they could lay hands on him and do it?</p>\n<p>—Beyond any doubt —he said.</p><h3 id=\"iii\">III.</h3>\n<p>—Well then, my dear Glaucon —I said—, this image must be applied in its entirety to what was said before, likening the region that sight reveals to us to the underground dwelling, and the fire that burns in it to the power of the sun. And if you take the ascent to the world above and the contemplation of what is there as the rising of the soul to the intelligible region, you will not be far from my own hope, since it is that you wish to hear; though only the divine knows whether it happens to be true. In any case, this is how it appears to me: in the intelligible, the last thing to be seen, and not without effort, is the idea of the good. And once it has been perceived, we are bound to infer that it is, for everything, the cause of all that is right and beautiful: for in the visible it has given birth to the light and to its lord, while in the intelligible it is itself sovereign and provides truth and understanding. And that anyone who means to act sensibly, whether in private or in public, must see it.</p>\n<p>—I too believe it —he said—, so far as I can.</p>\n<p>—Well then —I said—, agree to this as well, and do not be surprised that those who have come this far are unwilling to busy themselves with human affairs, but that their souls long always to remain on high; it is only natural, at least if the matter fits the image we have described.</p>\n<p>—It is natural indeed —he said.</p>\n<p>—And what of this? Do you think it strange —I said— that someone who comes down from divine contemplations to the miseries of men should cut a poor figure and look quite ridiculous when, while still seeing badly and before growing properly used to the surrounding darkness, he is forced to plead, in the law courts or anywhere else, about the shadows of the just or about the figures from which those shadows come, and to contend about how those who have never beheld justice itself conceive of them?</p>\n<p>—There is nothing strange in it —he said.</p>\n<p>—On the other hand —I said—, anyone with sense will remember that the eyes are troubled in two ways and from two causes: on passing from light to darkness and from darkness to light. And, thinking that the same happens to the soul, when he sees one troubled and unable to make anything out, he will not laugh without reason, but will examine whether, coming from a more luminous life, it finds itself in the dark through being unaccustomed, or whether, coming from a deeper ignorance towards something more luminous, it is dazzled by so much brightness. The first he will count happy in its experience and its life; the second he will pity, and, if he wished to laugh at it, his laughter would be less ridiculous than that directed at the soul coming down from the light.</p>\n<p>—You speak sensibly —he said.</p><h3 id=\"iv\">IV.</h3>\n<p>—It is necessary, then —I said—, if this is true, to think the following about it: that education is not as some present it when they announce what they claim to do. They say, in effect, that they put knowledge into a soul that does not have it, as if they were instilling sight into blind eyes.</p>\n<p>—They do say so, yes —he agreed.</p>\n<p>—Well then, our present reasoning —I said— shows that this faculty, which resides in the soul of each one, together with the organ by which each learns, must be turned, with the whole soul, away from what comes to be —just as the eye cannot turn from the dark to the bright except with the whole body—, until it is able to sustain the contemplation of being and of the most luminous part of being, which is what we call the good. Is it not so?</p>\n<p>—Yes.</p>\n<p>—Of this, then —I said—, there could be an art: of the turning, of how to make the organ turn most easily and effectively; not of implanting vision in it, since it already has it, but of setting it straight, since it is not oriented towards where it should be nor looks at what it ought.</p>\n<p>—So it seems —he said.</p>\n<p>—As for the other virtues, those said to be of the soul, they are perhaps close to those of the body, for, even if they did not exist before, they can be produced afterwards by habit and exercise. But the virtue of knowledge seems to belong to something more divine, which never loses its power and which, according to where it is turned, proves useful and beneficial, or else useless and harmful. Or have you not noticed, in those said to be wicked but clever, how sharply their mean little soul sees and how keenly it makes out that towards which it is turned? It is not that it has poor sight, but that it is forced to serve wickedness, so that, the more sharply it sees, the more evils it causes.</p>\n<p>—That is exactly so —he said.</p>\n<p>—Well then —I said—, if from childhood such a being had had those leaden weights, so to speak, cut away —weights akin to coming-to-be, which, fastened on by feasting and by such pleasures and dainties, turn the soul's gaze downwards; if, freed from them, it had been turned towards the true, that same soul, in those same men, would see with just as keen a sight as it now sees that towards which it is turned.</p>\n<p>—That is natural —he said.</p>\n<p>—And what of this? Is it not natural, and does it not follow of necessity from what has been said —I went on—, that neither those who lack education and have no experience of the truth could ever govern a city well, nor those who have been allowed to spend their time in study to the very end? The former, because they have no single goal in life to aim at in everything they do, in public and in private; the latter, because they will not willingly act, believing themselves to have passed already, while still alive, to the islands of the blessed.</p>\n<p>—It is true —he said.</p>\n<p>—Ours, then —I said—, is the task, the task of the founders: to compel the best natures to attain that knowledge we earlier said was the highest, to see the good and to undertake that ascent; and, once they have gone up and seen enough, not to allow them what is now allowed them.</p>\n<p>—What is that?</p>\n<p>—To stay up there —I said— and refuse to go down again to those captives, nor to share their toils and honours, be these of little or of great worth.</p>\n<p>—But then —he said—, shall we be unjust to them and make them live worse, when they could live better?</p><h3 id=\"v\">V.</h3>\n<p>—You forget again, my friend —I said—, that the law does not care that one single class of the city should be especially happy, but seeks that the whole city should be, fitting the citizens together by persuasion and by force, making them share with one another the benefit that each can bring to the common good. And it is the law that rears men of such a temper in the city, not to let each one turn wherever he likes, but to make use of them with a view to the cohesion of the city.</p>\n<p>—It is true —he said—; I had forgotten.</p>\n<p>—Well then, consider, Glaucon —I said—, that we shall not be unjust either to the philosophers who arise among us, but shall speak to them with reason when we compel them to care for the others and to protect them. We shall tell them that it is natural that, in other cities, those who come to be such do not share in the labours of the community, for there they spring up of their own accord, against the will of each regime; and whoever grows up by himself, owing his upbringing to no one, has reason not to trouble to repay it to anyone. ‘You, on the other hand, we have begotten for yourselves and for the whole city, as leaders and kings of the hive, educated with more care and fullness than those others, and better able to share in both lives. So each of you must go down, in turn, to the common dwelling of the rest and grow used to seeing in the dark: once accustomed, you will see a thousand times better than those there and will make out each image, knowing what it is and of what thing it is an image, for you will have contemplated the truth about the beautiful, the just and the good.’ And so the city, yours and ours, will live awake and not in dreams, as most cities live today, governed by those who quarrel with one another over empty shadows and fight over rule as if it were a great good. The truth, on the contrary, is this: the city in which those who are to rule least desire to rule is necessarily the best governed and the freest from faction; and the city that has rulers of a different kind, the reverse.</p>\n<p>—That is so, indeed —he said.</p>\n<p>—Do you think, then —I said—, that our pupils will disobey us on hearing this and refuse to work, each in turn, in the city, spending the rest of the time, which is the longer part, dwelling together in the region of the pure?</p>\n<p>—Impossible —he said—, for they are just things that we shall be ordering just men to do. What is certain, though, is that each of them will come to government as to something unavoidable, unlike those who rule in each city today.</p>\n<p>—That is so, my friend —I said—. If you can find, for those who are to rule, a life better than ruling itself, you can have a well-governed city; for only in it will the truly rich rule: rich not in gold, but in what the happy man must have in abundance, a good and sensible life. On the other hand, where those who come to public affairs are poor and hungry for goods of their own, convinced that the good must be snatched from there, it is not possible; because, once rule becomes a thing fought over, that war, domestic and internal, ruins both them and the rest of the city.</p>\n<p>—Nothing could be truer —he said.</p>\n<p>—Do you know, then —I said—, any other life that scorns political office, except that of true philosophy?</p>\n<p>—No, by Zeus! —he said.</p>\n<p>—And it is fitting that they come to power not as lovers of it; otherwise other rivals who also love it will contend against them.</p>\n<p>—How could it be otherwise?</p>\n<p>—Whom else, then, will you compel to watch over the city but those most versed in that by which a city is best governed, and who enjoy besides other honours and a life preferable to that of the politician?</p>\n<p>—No one else —he said.</p><p><em>Chapters VI to XII, omitted: Plato runs through all the\ndisciplines that must be taught to the guardians and philosophers:\ngymnastics, music, arithmetic, geometry, astronomy.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"xiii\">XIII.</h3>\n<p>—And I think —I said— that the study of all these disciplines we have run through, if it comes to show the community and kinship there is among them, and one reasons out in what sense they are akin to one another, will contribute something to what we are seeking, and our effort will not be in vain; but otherwise it will be.</p>\n<p>—I too sense it will be so —he said—. But it is an immense labour you speak of, Socrates.</p>\n<p>—The prelude, do you say —I replied—, or what? Do we not know that all this is no more than the prelude to the melody itself that must be learned? For I do not think that the experts in these matters strike you as dialecticians.</p>\n<p>—No, by Zeus! —he said—, except a very few of those I have known.</p>\n<p>—And then —I said—, those who cannot give or receive an account of things, will they ever know any of what we say must be known?</p>\n<p>—That neither —he said.</p>\n<p>—Then, Glaucon —I said—, is this not the melody that dialectic performs, already before us? Though intelligible, it is mimicked by the power of sight, which, we said, already sought to look at the animals themselves, at the stars themselves and, finally, at the sun itself. In the same way, when someone, by means of dialectic, tries to make his way —without recourse to any sense, by reason alone— towards what each thing is in itself, and does not give up until he grasps with the understanding alone what the good is in itself, he reaches then the very summit of the intelligible, just as that man reached the limit of the visible.</p>\n<p>—Exactly —he said.</p>\n<p>—Well then, do you not give this course the name of dialectic?</p>\n<p>—How could I not?</p>\n<p>—And the loosing from the chains —I said—, the passing from the shadows to the images and to the light, the ascent from the underground to the sun, and there the inability as yet to look at the animals, the plants and the light of the sun, but only at the divine reflections in the water and the shadows of real beings —no longer the shadows of figures cast by a light that, set against the sun, is itself like them—: all this work of the sciences we have run through has the power to raise the noblest part of the soul up to the vision of the most excellent of beings, just as before it raised the keenest organ of the body towards the sight of what is most luminous in the corporeal and visible region.</p>\n<p>—For my part —he said— I accept it so, though it strikes me as something very hard to accept and, on the other hand, hard also to reject. All the same —and since these are not things to be heard only now, but ones to which we shall have to return many times—, let us suppose that it is as is now said and go on at once to the melody itself, running through it as we ran through the prelude. Tell me, then, what the manner of the dialectical faculty is, into what kinds it divides and which are its paths; for these, it seems, would be the ones that lead to that point, on reaching which there would be as it were a rest from the road and the journey's end.</p>\n<p>—You will no longer be able to follow me, my dear Glaucon —I said—, though for my part the effort would not be lacking; and then you would no longer behold an image of what we have been saying, but the truth in person, at least as it appears to me. Whether it is really so or not is no longer worth contending; but that there is something of the kind that must be seen, that much must be maintained. Is it not so?</p>\n<p>—How could it not be?</p>\n<p>—And is it not also true that only the dialectical faculty can reveal it to one who has experience of what we were just now running through, and that by no other means is it possible?</p>\n<p>—This too deserves to be maintained —he said.</p>\n<p>—At least this —I said— no one will dispute with us when we affirm that no other method attempts to grasp systematically, with respect to each thing, what each one is in itself. For the other arts all concern themselves either with the opinions and desires of men, or with coming-to-be and manufacture, or with the care of what is born and made. And the rest, which we said grasped something of being —geometry and those that follow it—, we see dream about being, but find it impossible to behold it awake so long as, making use of hypotheses, they leave these untouched, unable to give an account of them. For when the starting point is something not known, and the conclusion and what lies between are interwoven with what is not known, what device could make such a concatenation become knowledge?</p>\n<p>—None —he said.</p><h3 id=\"xiv\">XIV.</h3>\n<p>—Then —I said— the dialectical method is the only one that proceeds thus, doing away with the hypotheses, up to the first principle itself, so as to make itself secure in it; and, when the eye of the soul is truly sunk in a coarse mire, it draws it out little by little and raises it on high, making use, as helpers in that turning, of the arts we have run through. By habit we have often called them sciences, but they need another name, clearer than opinion and darker than knowledge: in an earlier passage we fixed it as ‘thought’. But it is not, I think, a matter of disputing over a name when we have before us the examination of things so important.</p>\n<p>—No, indeed —he said.</p>\n<p>—Will it be enough to use the name that, spoken in the soul, makes clear the state in question?</p>\n<p>—It will be enough.</p>\n<p>—It will be enough, then —I said—, to give, as we did before, to the first part the name of knowledge; to the second, that of thought; to the third, that of belief; and to the fourth, that of imagination. We shall call the pair of the last two taken together opinion, and that of the first two, intelligence. Opinion is about coming-to-be; intelligence, about essence. And what essence is in relation to coming-to-be, intelligence is in relation to opinion; and what intelligence is in relation to opinion, that is knowledge in relation to belief and thought in relation to imagination. But the correspondence between the realities to which all this refers, and the dividing into two of each region —the opinable and the intelligible—, let us leave aside, Glaucon, so that it does not entangle us in discussions many times longer than those already past.</p>\n<p>—For my part —he said—, so far as I can follow you, in this too I agree.</p>\n<p>—And do you call dialectician the one who grasps, of each thing, the account of its essence? And of the one who does not have it, will you not say that, in so far as he cannot give an account of it to himself and to another, in just that measure he lacks understanding of it?</p>\n<p>—How should I not say it? —he replied.</p>\n<p>—Well, the same may be said of the good. Of the one who is not able to define the idea of the good by reason, setting it apart from everything else, nor to make his way, as in a battle, through all the objections raised against him, striving to refute in accordance with essence and not with appearance, nor to pass through all of it with a reasoning that does not stumble, will you not say that he knows neither the good itself nor any other good, but that, if he touches some image of the good, he touches it by opinion and not by knowledge, and that he passes this life dreaming and drowsing, until, before waking here, he reaches Hades to sleep a complete sleep?</p>\n<p>—Yes, by Zeus! —he exclaimed—; all that I shall affirm with all my might.</p>\n<p>—Well then, if ever you came actually to educate these children of yours whom you now rear and educate in word, you would not allow them, I think, being as they are irrational like incommensurable lines, to govern the city or to be masters of the most important things in it.</p>\n<p>—No, indeed —he said.</p>\n<p>—Will you prescribe to them, then, that they apply themselves above all to that teaching which makes them able to ask and answer with the greatest knowledge possible?</p>\n<p>—I shall prescribe it —he said—, but together with you.</p>\n<p>—And does it not seem to you —I said— that dialectic stands over the studies as their coping-stone, and that no other discipline could rightly be placed above it, but that with this what concerns the studies is now brought to completion?</p>\n<p>—So I believe —he said. [...]</p><p><em>(Our own translation from the Greek text of J. Burnet, Platonis Opera, Oxford, 1902, checked against that of Patricio de Azcárate, Madrid, 1872.)</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2\nid=\"parte-1-guía-de-lectura-simplificada-república-vii-capítulos-i-v-xiii-y-xiv\">Part\n1. Simplified reading guide: <em>The Republic</em> VII, chapters I-V,\nXIII and XIV</h2>\n<h3 id=\"hoja-para-el-alumnado\">Student sheet</h3>\n<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> <em>The Republic</em> is a dialogue\nwritten by Plato (Athens, 427-347 BC). The one who speaks in the first\nperson is Socrates, his teacher; his interlocutor in book VII is\nGlaucon, Plato's brother. Socrates tells an\n<strong>allegory</strong> (a story in which each concrete element\nstands for an abstract idea) to explain \"the state our nature is in\nwith respect to education or the lack of it\". The\nstory is known as <strong>the allegory of the cave</strong>.</p>\n<p>The chapters are numbered with Roman numerals according to the\ndepartment's edition (translation by José Manuel Pabón and Manuel\nFernández-Galiano, Alianza, 2003), for continuity with the classes; the text reproduced here is our own translation. The numbers with a letter (514a, 517b)\nare the <strong>Stephanus numbering</strong>, the universal reference to\nthe pages of the 1578 edition, which is the one used by the exams and the\nphilosophy textbooks. Chapters I-V occupy 514a-521b; chapters\nXIII-XIV, 531c-535a. Within each chapter, the limits we give are\napproximate: the department's source marks only the start of each\npage (514a, 516a, 517a, 518a, 519a, 520a, 521a, 532a, 533a, 534a).</p>\n<p><strong>How to use this guide.</strong> First read the chapter in the\ntext; then read \"What happens\" to check that you have understood it;\nanswer the two questions in your notebook (one or two sentences each); and\ncopy down the vocabulary you did not know.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"capítulo-i-514a-515d-la-caverna-y-los-prisioneros\">Chapter I\n(514a-515d). The cave and the prisoners</h4>\n<p><strong>What happens.</strong> Socrates asks Glaucon to imagine an\nunderground dwelling with an entrance open to the light. Inside there are\nmen chained since childhood by the legs and the neck: they cannot\nmove or turn their heads, so they see only the back wall. Behind\nthem a fire burns and, between the fire and the prisoners, there is a\nraised path with a low wall (a \"little screen\", like that of the\npuppeteers). Along that path pass people carrying objects and\nstatues of stone and wood; some speak and others are silent. The\nprisoners see only the <strong>shadows</strong> of those objects\ncast by the fire and hear the <strong>echo</strong> of the voices.\nAs they have never seen anything else, they believe the shadows are the only\nreality. Glaucon says the scene is strange; Socrates replies:\n\"like ourselves\". At the end of the chapter, one of the prisoners is\nunchained and forced to stand up, turn his neck and look towards the light.\nIt hurts, he sees \"spots\" and cannot make out the objects. If someone tells him\nthat he now sees more real things, he does not believe it: the shadows of\nbefore seem truer to him.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> We are used to taking appearances (the\nshadows: what we are told, what we see on a screen, what \"everyone\nsays\") for reality. Education begins with a forced and\npainful turning: at first, the truth seems less real than habit.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why do the prisoners believe the shadows are reality?\nPoint out two reasons the text gives.</li>\n<li>What happens to the first prisoner when he is unchained, and what does he think\nof what he sees?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Allegory</em>: a story in which each concrete element symbolises\nan abstract idea.</li>\n<li><em>Bonds</em>: cords or chains that tie.</li>\n<li><em>Little screen / low partition</em>: a small, low wall.</li>\n<li><em>Puppeteers</em>: people who move puppets (dolls) above\na screen so that the audience sees only the puppets.</li>\n<li><em>Spots</em>: sparks or flashes seen when the light\ndazzles.</li>\n<li><em>Empty shadows</em>: shadows that are hollow, worthless.</li>\n<li><em>At a loss</em>: confused, not knowing what to think.</li>\n<li><em>In accordance with nature</em>: just as happens in a\nnatural way.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"capítulo-ii-515e-517a-la-subida-el-sol-y-el-regreso\">Chapter II\n(515e-517a). The ascent, the sun and the return</h4>\n<p><strong>What happens.</strong> If the prisoner is forced to look at the fire,\nhis eyes hurt and he wants to go back to the shadows, which seem clearer\nto him. Then he is dragged by force up an \"rough and\nsteep\" ascent to the light of the sun. On arriving, he can see nothing: he needs\nto <strong>grow accustomed</strong>. He does so by stages: first he sees the\nshadows, then the reflections of people and things in the water,\nafter that the things themselves, later the night sky (stars and moon)\nand, finally, the <strong>sun</strong> \"as it is\".\nThen he deduces that the sun produces the seasons and the years and governs\neverything visible: it is the cause of everything they used to see in the cave. He\nremembers his companions and counts himself happy to have changed; he\npities them. He does not envy the honours given in the cave to whoever\nbest foretold the order of the shadows: he prefers, as Homer says,\nto \"work the land in the service of another, of a man without means\" rather than\nlive in \"those opinions and that life\". But if he goes back down and\nsits in his place, his eyes fill with darkness; the others laugh\nat him, say that going up ruins the eyes and, if they could,\nwould <strong>kill</strong> anyone who tried to unchain them and make them\ngo up.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Knowing is a gradual, strenuous ascent,\nagainst one's own will: no one leaves the cave alone or all at once.\nWhoever has seen the truth and comes back to free the others runs a\ndanger. Plato is thinking of the death sentence of Socrates (399\nBC).</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Put in order the five things the prisoner sees in turn outside the\ncave, from the easiest to the most difficult.</li>\n<li>Why do the prisoners mock the one who returns? What would they do with\nanyone who tried to free them?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Rough and steep</em>: difficult and very sloping (the\nascent).</li>\n<li><em>Infer</em>: deduce, draw a conclusion.</li>\n<li><em>Nostalgia</em>: sorrow for what has been left behind.</li>\n<li><em>Honours</em>: prizes and social recognition.</li>\n<li><em>The opinable</em>: that which admits only of opinions, not of sure\nknowledge.</li>\n<li><em>Without means</em>: without wealth.</li>\n<li><em>Darkness</em>: total blackness.</li>\n<li><em>Ascent</em>: a going up.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4\nid=\"capítulo-iii-517a-518b-interpretación-de-la-imagen-el-bien-es-como-el-sol\">Chapter\nIII (517a-518b). Interpretation of the image: the Good is like the\nsun</h4>\n<p><strong>What happens.</strong> Socrates explains the allegory. The cave\n(\"the dwelling-prison\") is the <strong>visible world</strong>; the fire\nis the power of the sun; the ascent and the contemplation of the things above\nare the <strong>rising of the soul to the intelligible world</strong>. In that\nworld, the last thing to be perceived, \"and not without effort\", is the <strong>idea of the\ngood</strong>; once seen, it is understood to be the cause of all that is\nright and beautiful, that in the visible world it has produced the light and the sun,\nand that in the intelligible it produces truth and knowledge. Whoever\nwishes to \"act sensibly, both in private and in public\" has to\nsee it. That is why it is no surprise that those who have reached the top do not\nwant to busy themselves with human affairs, nor that they seem clumsy and ridiculous\nwhen, still dazzled, they have to argue in the law courts\nabout \"the shadows of the just\" with people who have never seen justice\nitself. Socrates adds that the eyes are troubled for two causes:\non passing from light to darkness and on passing from darkness to light. A\nsensible person does not laugh at the soul that sees badly: he asks whether it comes from the light or\ngoes towards it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> There are two worlds: the visible (sensible) and\nthe intelligible. The Idea of the Good is to the intelligible world what the sun\nis to the visible: the cause that things exist and that we can\nknow them. Knowing the Good is the condition for living well and for governing\nwell.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Complete the correspondence Socrates makes: cave = ____; fire\n= ____; ascent = ____; sun = ____.</li>\n<li>What are the two ways of \"troubling the eyes\"? To what\nsituation in life does each correspond?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Visible / sensible world</em>: the one we grasp with the\nsenses.</li>\n<li><em>Intelligible world</em>: the one grasped only with the intelligence\n(reason).</li>\n<li><em>Glimpse</em>: an intuition, what one believes one half-sees without seeing it\nfully.</li>\n<li><em>Sovereign</em>: the one that rules, the one that governs.</li>\n<li><em>To be dazzled</em>: to be left blinded or bedazzled; to become confused.</li>\n<li><em>The just in itself</em>: justice itself, the Idea of justice, not\na concrete case of justice.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4\nid=\"capítulo-iv-518b-519d-la-educación-es-girar-el-alma-hay-que-obligar-a-subir-y-a-bajar\">Chapter\nIV (518b-519d). Education is turning the soul; one must compel it to go up and\nto come down</h4>\n<p><strong>What happens.</strong> Socrates draws a conclusion about\n<strong>education</strong>: it is not as \"some\" say (Plato is thinking of\nthe Sophists), who claim to put knowledge into the soul as if they were giving\nsight to blind eyes. The soul of each one already has the faculty of\nlearning; what must be done is to <strong>turn it whole</strong>,\naway from \"what comes to be\" (what changes), towards being and towards \"the\nbrightest part of being\", the good; just as the eye cannot turn\ntowards the light without turning the whole body. There can be an art of making\nthat organ turn, not of giving it vision. The other virtues are acquired\nby habit and exercise; that of knowledge is \"something more divine\" that\nis never lost and that proves useful or harmful according to where it looks (that is\nwhy there are people who are \"wicked, but clever\"). If from childhood the\nweights that drag the soul downwards (gluttony, pleasures) were pruned away, that same\nsoul would see the true. From this it follows that neither the\n<strong>uneducated</strong> can govern (they have no goal in life) nor\nthose who stay <strong>studying to the end</strong> (they believe themselves already in\n\"the islands of the blessed\" and do not want to act). The founders\nof the city must compel the best natures to go up to the\ngood and, afterwards, not allow them to stay up there. Glaucon protests: do we not\nharm them, making them live worse?</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> To educate is not to fill an empty head,\nbut to reorient the gaze of the soul towards the truth (Plato explains it\nwith <strong>recollection</strong>: to learn is to remember what the\nsoul once knew). Knowledge creates an obligation: whoever has seen the good\nmust return.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What do \"some\" say education is, and what does Plato reply?</li>\n<li>Why can neither the uneducated nor those who go on\nstudying \"to the end\" govern?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Instil</em>: to put inside, to give from outside.</li>\n<li><em>What comes to be</em>: what is generated and changes, the sensible\nworld.</li>\n<li><em>Virtue</em>: the capacity or excellence of a person (in Greek,\n<em>areté</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Leaden excrescences</em>: weights of lead that grow fastened to the\nsoul (the appetites) and drag it downwards.</li>\n<li><em>Gluttony</em>: excess in food and drink.</li>\n<li><em>Islands of the blessed</em>: in Greek mythology, the\nparadise where the heroes live after death.</li>\n<li><em>Founders</em>: Socrates and Glaucon, who are \"founding\" the\nideal city with words.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4\nid=\"capítulo-v-519e-521b-la-ley-busca-el-bien-de-la-ciudad-entera\">Chapter\nV (519e-521b). The law seeks the good of the whole city</h4>\n<p><strong>What happens.</strong> Socrates answers Glaucon: the\n<strong>law</strong> is not interested in one class being especially\nhappy, but in the whole city being so. For this it creates\n<strong>harmony</strong> among the citizens \"by means of\npersuasion or of force\", makes each one contribute what they can to the\ncommunity and forms citizens so as \"to make use of them itself\" with a view\nto the unity of the State. It is not unjust to compel the philosophers to\ngovern: in other cities the philosophers have formed themselves and owe\nnothing to anyone, but in the ideal city the city has reared them,\n\"as leaders and kings of the hive\", and so they must go down in turns to the\ndwelling of the others. Once accustomed to the darkness, they will see\n\"a thousand times better\" than those there, because they know the truth about the\nbeautiful, the just and the good. So the city will live \"in the light of day and not\namong dreams\". The best-governed city is the one in which the\nrulers <strong>least desire to govern</strong>: they are \"the true\nrich\", rich in a good life; where it is \"beggars and\nthose hungry for personal goods\" who go into politics, the struggle\nfor power produces civil war. Only the life of the true philosopher scorns office; that is why\nthe rulers must be compelled to govern, those who know how the State is best\nrun and who have a better life than that of the politician.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> The city stands above the individual:\nthe philosopher’s education is not for his own enjoyment, but for the service\nof all. The philosopher governs out of duty (he gives back what the city has\ngiven him), not out of ambition; and precisely for that reason he governs well.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What argument does Socrates use to justify that it is just to compel the\nphilosophers to govern?</li>\n<li>Why does he say that the best city is the one in which the\nrulers do not want to govern?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Law</em>: here, the set of norms that organises the ideal\ncity.</li>\n<li><em>Harmony</em>: agreement and balance between different parts.</li>\n<li><em>Persuasion</em>: convincing with words.</li>\n<li><em>Kings of the hives</em>: the queen bees; the city has\nreared its rulers with special care.</li>\n<li><em>Dissensions / internecine war</em>: internal clashes,\ncivil war.</li>\n<li><em>Pupils</em>: students, the young people the city educates.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Chapters VI-XII (removed in the department’s\nedition).</strong> Plato runs through the disciplines the future\nrulers must study: gymnastics, music, arithmetic,\ngeometry (plane and solid), astronomy and harmony. It is\nmathematics that accustoms the soul to thinking without the senses.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4\nid=\"capítulo-xiii-531c-533c-las-ciencias-son-el-preludio-la-dialéctica-es-la-melodía\">Chapter\nXIII (531c-533c). The sciences are the prelude; dialectic is the\nmelody</h4>\n<p><strong>What happens.</strong> Socrates says that studying all those\nsciences will only be useful if the affinity between them is discovered.\nGlaucon complains of the enormous labour, and Socrates answers that all that\n\"is no more than the <strong>prelude</strong> of the melody that must be\nlearned\": the experts in mathematics are not yet\n<strong>dialecticians</strong>, because they do not know how to \"give or receive an account\"\n(explain with reasons) of things. Dialectic is the journey that\nreason makes, \"without resorting to any sense\", towards \"what each\nthing is in itself\" until reaching \"what the good is in itself\", just as the\nprisoner reached the sun. Socrates returns to the cave: freeing oneself\nfrom the chains, looking at the fire, climbing up and seeing the reflections in the water is\nthe effect of the sciences he has listed, which raise up \"the best part\nof the soul\". Glaucon asks him to explain what dialectic is; Socrates\ndoubts he can follow him, but states that it is the only method that\ntries to grasp systematically what each thing is: the other arts\ndeal with opinions, desires or fabrications, and geometry and its\nsisters \"dream\" about what exists because they start from\n<strong>hypotheses</strong> that they do not justify.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Mathematics is a bridge towards the\nintelligible, but it does not reach the end because it takes its\nprinciples for granted. Only dialectic, which asks \"what is\" each thing up to the\nGood, is full knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why does Socrates call the mathematical sciences a \"prelude\" and\ndialectic a \"melody\"?</li>\n<li>What does geometry lack in order to be true knowledge?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Prelude / proem</em>: a musical or literary introduction that\nprecedes the main piece.</li>\n<li><em>Melody</em>: the main piece; here, dialectic.</li>\n<li><em>Dialectic</em>: a rational method that ascends from idea to idea\nup to the Idea of the Good; also, the knowledge reached by it.</li>\n<li><em>To give an account</em>: to explain with reasons (in Greek,\n<em>logos</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Hypothesis</em>: a supposition accepted without proving it so as\nto be able to start reasoning.</li>\n<li><em>In itself</em>: the thing itself, its essence, not a concrete\nexample.</li>\n<li><em>To apprehend</em>: to grasp with the mind.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4\nid=\"capítulo-xiv-533c-535a-los-cuatro-grados-del-conocimiento-la-dialéctica-como-remate\">Chapter\nXIV (533c-535a). The four degrees of knowledge; dialectic as the\ncrowning</h4>\n<p><strong>What happens.</strong> The dialectical method is the only one that,\n\"doing away with the hypotheses\", reaches \"the first principle itself\" and treads\nfirm ground; it draws \"the eye of the soul\" out of the \"coarse mire\" and raises it\nwith the help of the mathematical sciences, which do not deserve the name of\nknowledge but another: <strong>thought</strong>, \"clearer than\nopinion, but more obscure than knowledge\". Socrates fixes the four\nnames: <strong>knowledge</strong> (the first part),\n<strong>thought</strong> (the second), <strong>belief</strong>\n(the third) and <strong>imagination</strong> (the fourth). The first two\ntogether are <strong>intelligence</strong>, which refers to\nessence; the last two together are <strong>opinion</strong>, which\nrefers to generation (what is born and changes). The relation is\nproportional: what essence is to generation, so is\nintelligence to opinion. The dialectician is the one who grasps the\nessence of each thing and can define with reasons the idea of the good and\ndefend it \"as in a battle\" against all criticisms; whoever\ncannot do so reaches only an image of the good by opinion and \"does\nnothing but dream\". That is why anyone \"deprived\nof reason like irrational lines\" will not be allowed to govern. Dialectic is \"the crowning of the\nother teachings\": there is no discipline higher than it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> It is the passage that fixes the <strong>line\nof knowledge</strong>: two degrees of opinion (imagination,\nbelief) for the sensible world and two of intelligence (thought,\nknowledge) for the intelligible world. Dialectic, the summit of\nknowledge, is the condition for governing.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Write the four degrees of knowledge from lowest to highest and\ngroup them into opinion and intelligence.</li>\n<li>What must whoever truly knows the good be able to do?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>First principle</em>: the starting point that depends on no\nhypothesis (the Idea of the Good).</li>\n<li><em>Quagmire</em>: ground full of mud; here, the sensible world in\nwhich the soul is sunk.</li>\n<li><em>Essence</em>: what a thing truly is, what\nremains.</li>\n<li><em>Generation</em>: what is born, changes and dies.</li>\n<li><em>Belief</em>: sensible knowledge of concrete things (in\nGreek, <em>pistis</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Imagination</em>: knowledge of images and appearances (in\nGreek, <em>eikasía</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Irrational lines</em>: in mathematics, magnitudes that cannot\nbe expressed as a ratio (quotient) of two numbers; Plato plays on\nthe word \"reason\".</li>\n<li><em>Crowning</em>: the final part that crowns something.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-2-tabla-de-la-caverna-y-la-línea\">Part 2. Table of the\ncave and the line</h2>\n<h3 id=\"24-versión-con-huecos-hoja-para-el-alumnado\">2.4 Version with\ngaps (student sheet)</h3>\n<p><strong>Instructions.</strong> Complete the table with the help of\nthe text (chs. I–III and XIV) and the word bank. Each word or expression\nin the bank is used only once; some cells need more than one.\nAfterwards we shall correct in class with the complete table.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element of the myth</th>\n<th>What it symbolises</th>\n<th>Degree of knowledge</th>\n<th>Degree of reality</th>\n<th>What happens to the prisoner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chains on legs and neck</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shadows on the wall and echoes</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statues and objects being carried</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carriers of objects and wall</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rough and steep ascent, by force</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reflections in the water and shadows of the things above</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The things above and the night sky</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The sun</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The return to the cave</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n<td>____</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Word bank (column ‘What it symbolises’).</strong> The\nbody and its desires · Appearances and what others tell us · Material\nthings, copies of the Forms · The sun of the visible world ·\nThose who manufacture appearances (according to the department, the Sophists) ·\nEducation as a painful process; ascending dialectic · Mathematical\nobjects · The Forms · The Idea of the Good · The philosopher’s political\nduty; descending dialectic.</p>\n<p><strong>Word bank (column ‘Degree of knowledge’).</strong>\nImagination (eikasía) · Belief (pístis) · Discursive thought\n(diánoia) · Knowledge or intuitive intelligence (nóesis) · Nóesis at\nits peak · Passage from opinion to science · Application of knowledge to\nthe shadows of justice · None (obstacle) · None (condition of\nvision) · None.</p>\n<p><strong>Word bank (column ‘Degree of reality’).</strong>\nImages and copies · Sensible things · Source of light of the sensible world ·\nBorder between images and things · Passage from the sensible to the intelligible\n· Intelligible world, lower level · Intelligible world, the authentic\nreality · Foundation of all reality · Return to the sensible world ·\nWhat binds the soul to the sensible world.</p>\n<p><strong>Word bank (column ‘What happens to the\nprisoner’).</strong> He has been motionless since childhood · He takes them for the only\nreality and competes to guess them · Pain, flashes, perplexity · His\neyes hurt and he wants to go back to the shadows · He never sees them · He suffers and\nis dragged · He begins to reason without the senses · He contemplates what is\ntrue · He feels happy and pities the others · They laugh at him and\nwould kill him.</p>\n<p><strong>Closing questions (to discuss in pairs).</strong> In\nwhich row would you place social media? And a textbook? And a\nmathematical proof? Justify your answer with the table.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2\nid=\"anexo-rúbricas-oficiales-del-ejercicio-1-pau-2026-texto-literal-de-rubrica_pau-2026docx\">Annex.\nOfficial rubrics for Exercise 1 (PAU 2026)</h2>\n<p><strong>SPELLING CORRECTION:</strong> Each spelling error will be\npenalised by 0,1 points, up to a maximum of 1 point.</p>\n<p><strong>QUESTION 1.1. 1 POINT. Specific Competences to assess: 1\nand 7.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>If the student writes an explanation longer than the text, it will not\nbe accepted as a summary.</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No important ideas are mentioned, or secondary ideas are mentioned.\nInadequate structure. Comprehension is poor. The writing is very\ndeficient, with grammatical errors and badly constructed sentences.</td>\n<td>0 - 0,25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Some important ideas are mentioned, but they are not sufficiently\nclear. The structure is basic and has small flaws.\nComprehension is sufficient, but the grammar and expression are\npoor.</td>\n<td>0,25 - 0,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Most of the ideas are clear. Adequate comprehension. Grammar\ncorrect, but simple sentences and little elaboration in general. It lacks\nprecision in some details.</td>\n<td>0,5 - 0,75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All the important ideas are expressed clearly. Exact\ncomprehension of the text. Excellent writing, correct and elaborate grammar,\nwell-structured sentences and a notable syntactic and semantic richness.</td>\n<td>0,75 - 1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>QUESTION 1.2. OPTION A. 3 POINTS. Specific Competences to\nassess: 1 and 7.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>There is neither comprehension nor comparison. It is a text with no coherence, no\nstructure and no development. It lacks philosophical vocabulary or it is\nwholly mistaken. Very confused grammar, with meaningless sentences.</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very deficient comprehension. Comparison almost non-existent or very\nmistaken. There is no argumentative structure. Philosophical language inadequate\nor banal. Very poor grammar, with numerous errors and sentences that\nhinder comprehension.</td>\n<td>0 - 0,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insufficient comprehension, with important errors. Comparison\ninadequate or incoherent. There is barely any argumentative structure. Poor use or\nlack of philosophical vocabulary. Scant grammar, hardly\nunderstandable.</td>\n<td>0,5 - 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partial comprehension of the text or thesis. Superficial comparison, with no\ndeep criticism. Limited philosophical vocabulary. Weak and\nlittle-argued structure. Understandable grammar, but with evident errors or\nan obscure style.</td>\n<td>1 - 1,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sufficient but limited comprehension. Partial and little-\ndeveloped comparison. Inexact philosophical vocabulary. Basic argumentation,\nlittle cohesion. Acceptable grammar, but with errors or deficient\nexpression.</td>\n<td>1,5 - 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good comprehension of the text and of the question. Apt comparison,\neven if nuances are missing. Adequate philosophical vocabulary, but with\nsmall errors. Logical but not always effective structuring.\nCorrect grammar, with simple sentences.</td>\n<td>2 - 2,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deep and precise comprehension of the text and of the question.\nCritical, nuanced and philosophically significant comparison.\nPrecise and well-integrated philosophical vocabulary. Structured\nand clear arguments. Excellent grammar and elaborate sentences.</td>\n<td>2,5 - 3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>QUESTION 1.2. OPTION B. 3 POINTS. Specific Competences to\nassess: 1 and 7.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Insufficient comprehension and explanation. A text with no coherence, no\nstructure and no development. It lacks philosophical vocabulary or it is\nwholly mistaken. Very confused grammar, with meaningless sentences.</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very weak comprehension. Explanation of the sentence almost non-existent or\nvery mistaken. No argumentative structure. Philosophical language\ninadequate or banal. Very poor grammar, with numerous errors and sentences\nthat hinder comprehension.</td>\n<td>0 - 0,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scant comprehension, with important errors. Inadequate\nor incoherent explanation of the sentence. There is barely any argumentative structure. Poor use\nor lack of philosophical vocabulary. Scant grammar, hardly\nunderstandable.</td>\n<td>0,5 - 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partial comprehension. Superficial explanation of the sentence, with no\ndeep argumentative development. Limited philosophical vocabulary.\nWeak and little-argued structure. Understandable grammar, but with\nevident errors or an obscure style.</td>\n<td>1 - 1,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sufficient but limited comprehension. Partial and little-\ndeveloped explanation of the sentence. Inaccuracies in the philosophical vocabulary.\nBasic argumentation, with little cohesion. Acceptable grammar, but with\nerrors or deficient expression.</td>\n<td>1,5 - 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good comprehension. Good explanation of the sentence, even if\nsome nuances are missing. Adequate philosophical vocabulary, but with small\nerrors. Logical but not always effective argumentative structure.\nCorrect grammar, with simple sentences.</td>\n<td>2 - 2,5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deep and precise comprehension. Critical, nuanced and\nphilosophically significant explanation of the sentence. Philosophical vocabulary\nprecise and well integrated. Structured and clear argumentation. Excellent\ngrammar and elaborate sentences.</td>\n<td>2,5 - 3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"
 },
 "hf-paquete-descartes": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 14",
  "title": "Descartes: Discourse on Method II–IV",
  "html": "<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-0-el-texto-original-completo\">Part\n0. The complete original text</h2>\n<p><strong>Note on the translation.</strong> Translation\nby Manuel García Morente (public domain). Text\nof parts II, III and IV, the compulsory reading.</p>\n<h3 id=\"segunda-parte\">Part Two</h3>\n<p>I was at that time in Germany, where the occasion of some wars that\nhave not yet ended had called me; and returning from the coronation\nof the emperor towards the army, the beginning of winter caught me\nin a place where, finding no conversation that diverted me and having,\nfortunately, no cares or passions to disturb my mind, I remained\nthe whole day alone and shut up beside a stove, with all the tranquillity\nnecessary to give myself over to my thoughts. Among which, one\nof the first was that it occurred to me to consider that it often happens\nthat there is not as much perfection in works composed of several\npieces and made by the hands of many masters as in those on which\nonly one person has worked. Thus we see that buildings which a single\narchitect has begun and finished are usually more beautiful and\nbetter ordered than those which several have tried to put together and arrange,\nusing old walls built for other purposes. Those old cities, which were\nat first only villages and which in the course of time have become great\ntowns, are, for the most part, very badly laid out and proportioned, if we\ncompare them with those regular squares that an engineer draws on a plain\naccording to his fancy; and although, considering their buildings one by\none, we shall often find in them as much art as in those of these latter new\ncities, or more, nevertheless, seeing how they are arranged, a large one\nhere, a small one there, and how they make the streets crooked and uneven,\none would say that it is rather fortune than the will of some men endowed\nwith reason that has disposed them in that way. And if it is considered\nthat, nevertheless, there have always been certain officials charged with\nseeing that the buildings of private individuals serve public ornament, it\nwill be well recognised how difficult it is to do things completely when working\non what has been made by others. Thus, I also imagined that those peoples\nwho were formerly half savage and have gradually become civilised,\nmaking their laws as the inconvenience of crimes and quarrels forced them,\ncannot be as well constituted\nas those which, since they\ncame together, have</p>\n<p>been observing the constitutions of some prudent legislator. As\nit is also very certain that the state of the true religion, whose\nordinances God alone has instituted, must be incomparably better\narranged than all the others. And to speak of other human things,\nI believe that if Sparta was formerly very flourishing, it was\nnot because of the goodness of each of its laws in particular, for\nsome were very strange and even contrary to good customs, but because,\nhaving been invented by one man alone, they all tended to\nthe same end. And so I thought that the sciences of books, at least\nthose whose reasons are only probable and lack demonstrations,\nhaving been composed and gradually enlarged by the opinions of various\ndifferent people, are not as close to the truth as the simple\nreasonings that a man of good sense can naturally make about the\nthings that present themselves. And I also thought that, since\nwe have all been children before being men and have had to let ourselves\nbe ruled for a long time by our appetites and our tutors, who were often\ncontrary to one another, and neither of whom perhaps always advised\nus for the best, it is almost impossible that our judgements should\nbe as pure and as solid as they would be if, from the moment of birth,\nwe had had the full use of our reason and had never been directed by anything\nbut it. It is true that we do not see all the houses of a town\npulled down for the sole purpose of rebuilding them differently and making\nthe streets more beautiful; but we do see that many private individuals\nhave their houses torn down in order to rebuild them, and are often\nforced to do so when the buildings are in danger of falling because\ntheir foundations are no longer very firm. By whose example I came to\npersuade myself that it would not in truth be sensible for a private\nindividual to set out to reform a State by changing everything from the\nfoundations, and pulling it down in order to set it straight; nor even\nto reform the body of the sciences or the order established in the schools\nfor teaching them; but that as regards the opinions which up to\nthen given my credit, I could do nothing better than undertake once and\nfor all the task of removing them, in order afterwards to replace them\nwith better ones or with the same ones, once I had adjusted them to\nthe level of reason. And I held firmly for certain that, by this means,\nI would succeed in directing my life much better than if I were content\nto build on old foundations and to rely only on the principles I had\nlearned when young, without ever having examined whether or not they\nwere true. For although in this undertaking I saw various difficulties,\nthey were not, however, of the kind that have no remedy, nor can they\nbe compared with those found in the reform of the smallest things that\nconcern the public. These great political bodies are very hard to raise\nup once they have been thrown down, or even to hold upright when\nthey totter, and their falls are necessarily very hard. Moreover, as regards\ntheir imperfections, if they have any—and the mere diversity that\nexists among them is enough to assure us that several have—, custom\nhas undoubtedly softened them a great deal, and has even avoided or imperceptibly\ncorrected not a few of them, which prudence would not have\nbeen able to remedy so effectively; and finally, they are almost\nalways more bearable than changing them would be, like the highways\nthat wind through the mountains, which little by little become\nso smooth and comfortable through much traffic that it is much\nbetter to follow them than to set about taking short cuts,</p>\n<p>leaping over rocks and descending to the bottom of chasms. For\nall this, I can in no way applaud those men of restless and hasty\ntemper who, though called neither by birth nor by fortune to the\nmanagement of public affairs, never cease to make, in idea, some\nnew reform; and if I thought there was in this writing the slightest\nthing that could make me suspected of such folly, I would not\nhave consented to its publication. My designs have never been\nother than to try to reform my own thoughts and to build on ground\nthat belongs to me alone. If, having been rather pleased with my\nwork, I show you the model of it here, this does not mean that\nI wish to advise anyone to imitate me. Those who have received from\nGod better and more abundant gifts will doubtless have loftier\npurposes, but I very much fear that even this one of mine may already\nbe too bold for some people. The mere resolution to rid oneself of\nall previously received opinions is not an example that everyone should\nfollow. And the world is made up almost entirely of only two kinds\nof minds, for whom this example is in no way suitable, namely: of\nthose who, believing themselves cleverer than they are, cannot restrain\nthe haste of their judgements nor keep enough patience to conduct\nall their thoughts in an orderly way; so that, if once they had taken\nthe liberty of doubting the principles they have received and of departing\nfrom the common road, they could never keep to the path that\nmust be followed to go more directly, and would remain astray all\ntheir lives; and of others who, possessing enough reason or modesty to\njudge that they are less capable of distinguishing the true from the\nfalse than other people from whom they can receive instruction, ought\nrather to be content with following the opinions of those people\nthan to seek better ones for themselves. And I would doubtless have\nbeen of this latter kind of minds, if I had had in my life only one\nteacher or had not known how different the opinions of the most learned\nhave been at all times. But having learned at school that nothing can\nbe imagined, however strange and incredible, that has not been said\nby one of the philosophers, and having seen afterwards, in my travels,\nthat not all those who think differently from us are for that reason\nbarbarians and savages, but that many make as much use of reason as we\ndo, or more; and having considered that one and the same man, with\nthe same wit, if he has been brought up from childhood among French or\nGermans, becomes very different from what he would be if he had always\nlived among Chinese or cannibals, and that even in the fashions of our\nclothes, what pleased us ten years ago, and may perhaps please us\nagain ten years hence, seems to us today extravagant and ridiculous, so\nthat it is custom and example that persuade us rather than any certain\nknowledge; and that, nevertheless, a majority of votes is no proof\nof any value for truths that are somewhat difficult to discover, because\nit is more likely that one man alone should come upon them than a\nwhole people, I could not choose a person whose opinions seemed to me\npreferable to those of others, and I found myself as if obliged\nto undertake by myself the task of guiding myself.</p>\n<p>But like a man who has to walk alone and in the dark, I resolved to go\nso slowly and to use so much circumspection in everything that, at the\nprice of advancing little, I would at least take very good care not to\nstumble and fall. And I did not even wish to begin to get rid completely\nof any of the opinions that might formerly have slipped into my belief\nwithout having been introduced by reason, until after spending a good\ntime on the plan of the work I was about to undertake, seeking the true\nmethod for arriving at the knowledge of all the things of which my\nmind was capable. I had studied a little, when I was younger, among the\nparts of philosophy, logic, and among those of mathematics, the analysis\nof the geometers and algebra, three arts or sciences which, it seemed,\nshould contribute something to my purpose. But when I examined them,\nI had to note that, as regards logic, its syllogisms and most of the other\ninstructions it gives serve rather to explain to others things already\nknown or even, like the art of Lull, to speak without judgement of\nthings unknown, than to learn them. And although it contains, in truth,\nmany good and true precepts, there are nevertheless mixed with them so\nmany others that are harmful or superfluous that separating them is almost\nas difficult as drawing a Diana or a Minerva out of a block of unhewn\nmarble. Then, as regards the analysis of the ancients and the algebra\nof the moderns, apart from the fact that they deal only with very abstract\nmatters which do not seem to be of any use, the former is always\nso constrained to consider figures that it cannot exercise the understanding\nwithout greatly tiring the imagination; and in the latter, its practitioners\nhave so subjected themselves to certain rules and certain\nsymbols that they have made of it a confused and obscure art, good for\nentangling the wit, instead of a science that cultivates it. For all of\nwhich, I thought that some other method had to be sought that would combine\nthe advantages of these three, excluding their defects. And as a\nmultitude of laws very often serves as an excuse for vices, a State being\nmuch better governed when there are few, but very strictly observed,\nso also, in place of the great number of precepts that logic contains,\nI believed that the following four would suffice for me, provided that\nI took a firm and constant resolution not to fail to observe them\neven once. The first was never to accept anything as true unless I\nknew with evidence that it was so; that is, carefully to avoid precipitancy\nand prejudice, and to include in my judgements nothing more\nthan what presented itself so clearly and distinctly to my mind that\nthere would be no occasion to put it in doubt. The second, to divide\neach of the difficulties I examined into as many parts as possible\nand as were required for its best solution. The third, to conduct\nmy thoughts in an orderly way, beginning with the simplest objects\nand those easiest to know, in order to ascend little by little, by\ndegrees, to the knowledge of the more composite, and even supposing\nan order among those that do not naturally precede one another.</p>\n<p>Raymond Lull had written an Ars magna, in which he set out a kind of\nintellectual mechanism, a sort of algebra of thought. A method consisting\nin referring a given proposition to another, simpler one, already known\nto be true, so that afterwards, starting from this, the former can be\ndeduced. It is the procedure used to solve problems of geometry,\nsupposing them already solved, and showing that the consequences\nderived from this proposition are known theorems. Plato\nis held to be the inventor of geometrical analysis.</p>\n<p>And the last, to make everywhere enumerations so complete and reviews\nso general that I might be sure of omitting nothing. Those long chains\nof reasoning, very simple and easy, which geometers are accustomed\nto use in order to arrive at their most difficult demonstrations,\nhad given me occasion to imagine that all things which can come within\nhuman knowledge follow one another in the same way, and that, provided\nonly one abstains from accepting as true anything that is not, and\nalways keeps the order necessary for deducing one from another, there\ncan be none so remote that it cannot be reached, or so hidden that\nit cannot be discovered. And I did not tire myself much in seeking\nwith which ones it was necessary to begin, for I already knew that it\nwas with the simplest and easiest to know; and considering that, among\nall those who have hitherto sought truth in the sciences, only the\nmathematicians have been able to find some demonstrations, that is, some certain\nand evident reasons, I did not doubt that I had to begin with the\nsame ones that they have examined, even though I did not expect to draw from\nthis any other benefit than to accustom my mind to feed on truths and\nnot to be content with false reasons. Yet I did not therefore conceive the\nintention of trying to learn all the particular sciences commonly called\nmathematics, and seeing that, although their objects are different, they\nall nevertheless agree in considering nothing but the various relations or\nproportions found in such objects, I thought it would be better to limit\nmyself to examining these proportions in general, supposing them only in\nthose subjects that would serve to make their knowledge easier for me, and\neven not tying them to those subjects in any way, so as to be able afterwards\nto apply them all the more freely to all the others to which they might\nbe suited 16. Then I noticed that, in order to know them, I would sometimes\nneed to consider each of them in particular, and at other times only\nto retain or understand several together, and I thought that, in order\nto consider them better in particular, I ought to suppose them as lines,\nbecause I found nothing simpler, nor anything I could represent more distinctly;\nand to retain or understand several together, it was necessary\nto explain them in some symbols, the shortest possible; and that,\nby this means, I was taking the best there is in geometrical analysis\nand in algebra, and thus correcting all the defects of the one by the\nother. And, in fact, I venture to say that the exact observance of\nthe few precepts I had chosen gave me such facility in unravelling all\nthe questions treated by these two sciences that, in the two or three\nmonths I spent in examining them, having begun with the simplest and\nmost general, and each truth I found being a rule that served me afterwards\nto find others, I not only managed to solve several questions\nthat I had previously considered very difficult, but it even seemed to\nme towards the end that, even in those I did not know, I could determine\nby what means and how far it was possible to solve them. In which,\nperhaps, you will not accuse me of excessive vanity if you consider\nthat, since there is only one truth in each thing, whoever finds it\nknows all that can be known of it; and that, for example, a child who\nknows arithmetic and does a sum according\nto the rules can be sure of having</p>\n<p>found, concerning the sum he was examining, all that the human mind\ncan find; because, after all, the method that teaches how to follow\nthe true order and to enumerate exactly all the circumstances of what\nis sought contains everything that gives certainty to the rules of\narithmetic. But what pleased me most in this method was that, with\nit, I was assured of using my reason in everything, if not perfectly,\nat least as well as lay in my power. Not to mention that, in applying\nit, I felt my mind gradually becoming accustomed to conceive objects\nmore clearly and distinctly, and that, not having subjected it to\nany particular matter, I promised myself to apply it with equal fruit\nto the difficulties of the other sciences, as I had done to those of\nalgebra. Not for this reason did I dare to begin straight away to examine\nall those that presented themselves, for that itself would have\nbeen contrary to the order that the method prescribes; but having\nnoticed that the principles of the sciences had all to be taken from\nphilosophy, in which I still found none that was certain, I thought that\nabove all it was necessary to try to establish some of this kind, and,\nthis being the most important thing in the world and the one in which\nprecipitancy and prejudice are most to be feared, I believed I should\nnot undertake the enterprise before reaching a more mature age than that\nof twenty-three, which I then was, and before having devoted a good space\nof time to preparing myself, uprooting from my mind all the bad opinions\nto which I had given entry before that time, also gathering a store\nof various experiences, which would afterwards be the material of my\nreasonings, and finally, exercising myself ceaselessly in the method\nI had prescribed for myself, in order to fix it better in my mind.</p>\n<h3 id=\"tercera-parte\">Part Three</h3>\n<p>Finally, just as, to begin rebuilding the lodging in which one\nlives, it is not enough to have pulled it down and to have gathered\nmaterials and architects, or to have trained oneself in architecture\nand moreover to have carefully drawn the plan of the new building,\nbut one must also provide oneself with some other dwelling,\nin which to spend comfortably the time the work will last; so, in order\nnot to remain irresolute in my actions while reason obliged me\nto be so in my judgements, and not to fail to live, in the meantime,\nas happily as I could, I arranged for myself a provisional morality,\nconsisting of only three or four maxims, which I shall gladly\ncommunicate to you. The first was to obey the laws and customs of my\ncountry, holding constantly to the religion in which, by God's grace,\nI had been instructed from childhood, and governing myself in\neverything else by the most moderate opinions, the furthest from all\nexcess, that were commonly accepted in practice by the wisest of\nthose with whom I would have to live. For since I had already begun\nto count my own opinions for nothing, because I intended to submit\nthem all to a new examination, I was sure I could do nothing</p>\n<p>better than follow those of the wisest. And even though among the Persians\nand the Chinese there are perhaps men as wise as among us, it seemed to me that\nthe most useful thing was to adapt myself to those with whom I would have\nto live; and that to know what their true opinions were, I should pay\nattention rather to what they did than to what they said, not only\nbecause, given the corruption of our customs, there are few people\nwilling to say what they believe, but also because many do not know\nit, since the act of thought by which one believes a thing is different\nfrom that by which one knows that one believes it, and so the\none is often found without the other. And among several opinions\nequally accepted, I chose the most moderate, not only because they\nare always the most convenient in practice, and probably the best,\nsince all excess tends to be bad, but also in order to stray less\nfrom the true path, in case of error, if, having chosen one of the\nextremes, it were the other that ought to have been followed. And in\nparticular I considered as an excess every promise by which a part\nof one's own liberty is given away: not that I disapproved of the\nlaws which, to remedy the inconstancy of weak minds, allow one, when\none has some good design, or even for the security of commerce in\nindifferent designs, to make vows or contracts binding oneself to\nperseverance: but since I saw nothing in the world that remained always\nin the same state, and since, as far as I was concerned, I hoped to\nperfect my judgements more and more, not to make them worse, I would\nhave thought I was committing a grave fault against good sense if,\nmerely because I approved something at that time, I were obliged to hold\nit good later too, when perhaps it had ceased to be so, or when\nI had ceased to esteem it as such. My second maxim was to be as firm\nand resolute in my actions as I could, and to follow with as much constancy\nthe most doubtful opinions, once I had determined upon them, as\nif they were most certain, imitating in this travellers who, lost in\nsome forest, must not wander about turning this way and that, still\nless stop in one place, but walk always as straight as they can towards\na fixed point without changing direction for slight reasons, even\nthough at the beginning it was only chance that determined them to\nchoose that course, for in this way, if they do not arrive precisely\nwhere they wish to go, they will at least end by arriving somewhere,\nwhere it is to be thought they will be better off than in the middle\nof the forest. And so, since the actions of life often admit of\nno delay, it is a very certain truth that if it is not in our\npower to discern the best opinions, we must follow the most probable;\nand even if we find no more probability in some than in\nothers, we must nevertheless decide on some and consider them\nafterwards, no longer as doubtful, insofar as they relate to\npractice, but as very true and very certain, because the reason\nthat determined us is so. And this was enough to free me from\nthen on from all the regrets and remorse that usually agitate\nthe consciences of those weak and wavering minds who, without\nconstancy, let themselves be drawn into practising as good things\nthat they afterwards judge bad. My third maxim was always\nto try to conquer myself rather than fortune, and to change my\ndesires rather than the order of the world, and generally</p>\n<p>to accustom myself to believing that there is nothing entirely\nin our power except our own thoughts, so that after we\nhave done our best in regard to external things, everything\nthat fails to succeed is, for us, absolutely impossible.\nAnd this alone seemed to me enough to keep me in future from desiring\nanything without obtaining it and thus to keep me content; for since\nour will is not naturally determined to desire anything but what\nour understanding in some way represents to it as possible, it is\nclear that if we consider all the goods that are outside us as equally\nout of reach of our power, we shall feel no sorrow at lacking\nthose that seem due to our birth, when we see ourselves deprived of\nthem through no fault of our own, any more than we feel it at not\nbeing masters of the kingdoms of China or Mexico; and making, as the\nsaying goes, a virtue of necessity, we shall no more desire to be\nhealthy when ill, or to be free when imprisoned, than we now desire\nto possess bodies made of a matter as incorruptible as diamond or\nwings to fly like birds. But I confess that long exercises and repeated\nmeditations are needed to accustom oneself to looking at all\nthings from that angle; and I believe that it was in this chiefly\nthat the secret of those philosophers consisted who were able in former\ntimes to withdraw from the empire of fortune, and in spite of\nsuffering and poverty, to compete in happiness with the gods themselves.\nFor, occupied without rest in considering the limits prescribed by nature,\nthey were so perfectly persuaded that nothing was in their power\nexcept their own thoughts, that this alone was enough to prevent them\nfrom feeling affection for other things; and they disposed of those thoughts\nso absolutely that they had in this some reason to esteem themselves\nricher and more powerful and freer and more blessed than any other\nmen, who, not having this philosophy, can never, however much nature\nand fortune may have favoured them, dispose of all they want as those philosophers\ndid. Finally, as a conclusion to this morality, it occurred\nto me to consider, one by one, the different occupations to which men\ndevote their lives, in order to try to choose the best; and without wishing\nto say anything of those of others, I thought I could do nothing\nbetter than continue in the very one I had; that is, to apply my whole\nlife to the cultivation of my reason and to advance as far as I could\nin the knowledge of truth, according to the method I had prescribed for\nmyself. I had already felt such extreme contentment since I began to\nuse that method that I did not think a gentler and more innocent one\ncould be received in this life; and discovering every day, with its\nhelp, some truths which seemed to me quite important and generally\nunknown to other men, the satisfaction I experienced filled my mind\nso completely that everything else was indifferent to me. Besides,\nthe three preceding maxims were founded only on the purpose I harboured\nof continuing to instruct myself; for since God has given each\nman some light with which to discern the true from the false, I\nwould not have believed that I should be content for a single moment\nwith the opinions of others, had I not proposed to use my own judgement\nto examine them when the time came; and I could not have freed\nmyself from scruples in following them, had I not hoped to take\nadvantage of every occasion to find better ones, if there were</p>\n<p>any; and, finally, I would not have known how to limit my desires\nand be content, had I not followed a path by which, at the same time\nas assuring myself of the acquisition of all the knowledge I could,\nI also thought in the same way to come to acquire all the true\ngoods that were in my power; for since our will is determined to\npursue or avoid anything only because our understanding represents\nit to it as good or bad, it suffices to judge well in order to\nact well 22, and to judge as well as one can in order to act as\nwell as one can; that is, to acquire all the virtues and with them\nall the goods that can be obtained; and when one has the certainty\nthat this is so, one cannot but be content. Having, then, established\nmyself in these maxims, which I set aside together with\nthe truths of faith, which have always been first in my belief,\nI thought that I could freely begin to rid myself of all my other\nopinions; and as I hoped to achieve this better by conversing\nwith men than by remaining longer shut up in the room where I had\nmeditated all these thoughts, I continued my journey before the\nwinter was altogether over. And in the nine years that followed,\nI did nothing but go hither and thither about the world, trying\nto be rather a spectator than an actor in the comedies that are\nplayed in it; and making particular reflections on every matter\non whatever could make it suspect and give occasion for mistakes, I managed\nto root out of my mind, in all that time, whatever errors could previously have\nslipped in. Not that I imitated the sceptics, who doubt merely for the sake\nof doubting and always pose as irresolute; on the contrary, my purpose was\nnone other than to make myself sure of the truth, casting aside the shifting\nearth and sand, so as to come upon the bedrock or clay. Which, in my opinion,\nI achieved quite well; so much so that, trying to discover the falsity\nor uncertainty of the propositions I examined, not by feeble conjectures but\nby clear and secure reasonings, I found none so doubtful that I could not\ndraw from it some conclusion sufficiently certain, even if only that it contained\nnothing certain. And just as in pulling down an old house the materials\nare usually kept to rebuild the new one, so too in destroying all those\nopinions of mine that I judged ill-founded, I made various observations and\nacquired experiences that have since served me to establish other, more certain\nones. And in addition I continued to exercise myself in the method I had\nprescribed for myself; for besides taking good care to conduct my thoughts\nin general according to the aforesaid rules, I set aside from time to time\nsome hours to practising them, particularly on difficulties\nof mathematics, or also on some others which I could\nmake almost similar to those of mathematics, detaching\nthem from the principles of the other sciences, which\ndid not seem to me firm enough; all this can be seen\nin various questions which are explained in this same\nvolume 24. And so, living in appearance like those who\nhave no other occupation than to lead a gentle and innocent\nlife and who contrive to separate pleasures from\nvices, and who, in order to enjoy their leisure without\ntedium, make use of all the honest diversions within\ntheir reach, I did not fail to persevere in my purpose\nand to profit for the knowledge of truth, more,</p>\n<p>perhaps, than if I had been content with reading books or frequenting literary\ngatherings. Nevertheless, those nine years passed without my having\ncome to any decision concerning the difficulties over which the learned usually\ndispute, and without having begun to seek the foundations of a philosophy\nmore certain than the vulgar one. And the example of several excellent\nminds who have tried to do so without, in my opinion, succeeding, led\nme to imagine so much difficulty in it that I would perhaps not\nhave dared to undertake it so soon had I not seen that some were\nspreading the rumour that I had carried it out. I cannot say what\ngrounds they could have had for expressing such an opinion; and if\nI have contributed anything to it by my words, it must have been by\nhaving confessed my ignorance with more candour than those who have\nstudied a little usually do, and perhaps also by having made known\nthe reasons I had for doubting many things that others consider\ncertain, but not because I have prided myself on possessing any\ndoctrine. But since I have a heart good enough not to want to be taken\nfor other than I am, I thought it necessary to try by all means\nto make myself worthy of the reputation they gave me; and precisely\neight years ago, that desire decided me to withdraw from all the\nplaces where I might have acquaintances, and to retire here 25,\nto a country where the long duration of the war has caused such orders\nto be established that the armies kept up seem to serve only\nso that men may enjoy the fruits of peace with all the greater\nsecurity, and where, in the midst of the crowd of a great and very\nactive people, more attentive to their own business than curious\nabout that of others, I have been able, without lacking\nany of the comforts to be found in the more frequented cities,\nto live as solitary and retired as in the remotest desert.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cuarta-parte\">Part Four</h3>\n<p>I do not know whether I should speak to you of the first meditations I\nmade there, for they are so metaphysical and so out of the common that perhaps\nthey will not please everyone 26 Nevertheless, so that it may be judged\nwhether the foundations I have taken are firm enough, I find myself\nin a way obliged to say something of those reflections. I had noticed long\nago that, as regards conduct, it is sometimes necessary to follow opinions\nthat we know to be very uncertain as if they were beyond doubt, and\nthis has already been said in the preceding part; but since on this occasion\nI wished to concern myself solely with the search for truth, I thought\nI should do the contrary and reject as absolutely false everything in which\nI could imagine the slightest doubt, in order to see whether, after\nthis, there would remain in my belief something that was entirely\nindubitable. Thus, since the senses sometimes deceive us, I wished\nto suppose that there is nothing that is just as they present\nit to us in the imagination; and since there are men who err in\nreasoning, even about the simplest matters of geometry, and commit\nparalogisms, I judged that I was as liable to error as anyone\nelse, and rejected as false all the reasons that I had previously\ntaken as demonstrative; and finally, considering that all</p>\n<p>the thoughts that come to us when awake can also occur to us in sleep,\nwithout any of them then being true, I resolved to pretend that\nall the things that had hitherto entered my mind were no more true\nthan the illusions of my dreams. But I noticed immediately that, while\nI wished thus to think that everything is false, it was necessary\nthat I, who thought it, should be something; and observing that this\ntruth: I think, therefore I am, was so firm and secure that the most\nextravagant suppositions of the sceptics were not capable of shaking\nit, I judged that I could accept it, without scruple, as the first\nprinciple of the philosophy I was seeking. I then examined attentively\nwhat I was, and seeing that I could pretend that I had no body and that\nthere was no world nor any place in which I found myself, but that I\ncould not pretend for that reason that I was not, but on the contrary,\nfrom the very fact that I thought of doubting the truth of other things,\nit followed most certainly and evidently that I was, whereas, if I\nmerely ceased to think, even if everything else that I had imagined were\ntrue, I had no longer any reason to believe that I was, I thereby\nknew that I was a substance whose whole essence and nature is to think,\nand which, in order to be, needs no place and depends on nothing material;\nso that this ‘I’, that is, the soul by which I am what I am, is\nentirely distinct from the body and even easier to know than the body,\nand even if the body were not, the soul would not cease to be all that\nit is. After this, I considered in general what is required in a proposition\nin order for it to be true and certain, for since I had just found\none that I knew to be so, I thought I ought also to know in what\nthat certainty consists. And having noticed that in the proposition ‘I\nthink, therefore I am’, there is nothing that assures me that I speak\nthe truth, except that I see very clearly that in order to think it is\nnecessary to be, I judged that I could adopt this general rule: that\nthe things we conceive very clearly and distinctly are all true, but\nthat there is only some difficulty in noting which are the ones we conceive\ndistinctly. After which, I had to reflect that, since I doubted,\nmy being was not entirely perfect, for I saw clearly that there is\nmore perfection in knowing than in doubting; and it then occurred to\nme to inquire from where I had learned to think of something more perfect\nthan myself; and I knew evidently that it must be from some nature\nthat was in fact more perfect. As regards the thoughts that were in\nme of various things outside me, such as the sky, the earth, light,\nheat and many others, I was not much concerned to know where they came\nfrom, because, seeing nothing in these thoughts that seemed to make\nthem superior to me, I could believe that, if they were true, they were\ndependencies of my nature, insofar as it possesses some perfection,\nand if they were not, they came from nothing, that is, they were in\nme because there is a defect in me. But the same could not happen with\nthe idea of a being more perfect than my being, for it was manifestly\nimpossible that such an idea should come from nothing; and since\nthere is no less repugnance in thinking that the more perfect should\nbe a consequence and dependency of the less perfect than in thinking\nthat something should come from nothing, it could not come from myself\neither; so that it only remained that it had been put in me by a\nnature truly more perfect than I am, and possessing even all the</p>\n<p>perfections of which I could have an idea; that is, to explain it\nin one word, by God. To this I added that, since I knew of some perfections\nthat I lacked, I was not the only being that existed (here,\nif you will permit me, I shall make free use of the terms of the\nschool), but that it was absolutely necessary that there be some other\nmore perfect being on whom I depended and from whom I had acquired\nall that I possessed; for if I were alone and independent of any\nother being, in such a way that the little in which I participated\nin the perfect Being came from myself, I could have had from myself\nalso, for the same reason, everything else that I knew I lacked, and so\nbe myself infinite, eternal, immutable, omniscient, omnipotent and,\nin short, possess all the perfections that I could perceive in God. For\nby virtue of the reasonings I have just made, in order to know the\nnature of God, as far as mine is capable of knowing it, it was enough\nfor me to consider all the things of which I found in myself some idea\nand see whether or not it was a perfection to possess them, and I was\nsure that none of those that indicated some imperfection is in God,\nbut all the others are in Him; thus I saw that doubt, inconstancy, sadness\nand other similar things cannot be in God, since I myself would be\nvery glad to be free of them. Moreover, I had ideas of various sensible\nand corporeal things, for even supposing that I dreamed and that\neverything I saw and imagined was false, I could not deny, nevertheless,\nthat those ideas were truly in my thought. But having already known\nvery clearly in myself that intelligent nature is distinct from corporeal\nnature, and considering that all composition denotes dependence,\nand that dependence is manifestly a defect, I judged from this that it\ncould not be a perfection in God to be composed of those two natures,\nand that, consequently, God was not composed; whereas, if there were in\nthe world bodies, or some intelligences or other natures that were not\nwholly perfect, their being must depend on the divine power, to the\npoint of not being able to subsist without it for a single instant. I\nthen wished to inquire into other truths; and having proposed to myself\nthe object of the geometers, which I conceived as a continuous body\nor a space infinitely extended in length, breadth and height or depth,\ndivisible into various parts that can have various figures and magnitudes\nand be moved or transposed in all directions, for geometers suppose\nall that in their object, I went over some of their simplest demonstrations,\nand having noticed that the great certainty that everyone attributes\nto these demonstrations is founded only on their being conceived\nwith evidence, according to the rule stated above, I also noticed that\nthere was nothing in them that assured me of the existence of their\nobject, for, for example, I saw well that, if we suppose a triangle,\nit is necessary that its three angles be equal to two right angles; but\nI saw nothing that assured me that there is any triangle in the world;\nwhereas, if I returned to examine the idea I had of a perfect being,\nI found that existence is included in it in the same way as in\nthe idea of a triangle it is included that its angles are equal to two\nright angles, or in that of a sphere that all its parts are equally\ndistant from the centre, and even with more evidence still; and that,\nconsequently, it is at least as certain that God, who is that perfect\nbeing, is or exists, as any demonstration of geometry can be. But\nif there are some who are persuaded that it is difficult to know\nwhat God is, and even what the soul is, it is because they never</p>\n<p>raise their minds above sensible things and are so accustomed to\nconsidering everything with the imagination—which is a way of thinking\nparticular to material things—that what is not imaginable seems\nto them not to be intelligible. Which is quite evident in the maxim\nthat the philosophers themselves admit as true in the schools, and\nwhich says that there is nothing in the understanding that has not\nbeen previously in the senses 27, in which, however, it is certain that the\nideas of God and of the soul have never been; and it seems to me that those\nwho wish to make use of their imagination to understand those ideas are\nlike those who, in order to hear sounds or smell odours, wished to use\ntheir eyes; and there is still this difference between the former and the\nlatter: that the sense of sight does not assure us any less of the truth\nof its objects than smell and hearing do of theirs, whereas neither the imagination\nnor the senses can ever assure us of anything unless the understanding\nintervenes. Finally, if there are still men who have not been sufficiently\nconvinced by the reasons I have presented of the existence of God\nand of the soul, I want them to know that all the other things that perhaps\nthey think more certain, such as that they have a body, that there are\nstars, and an earth, and the like, are nevertheless less certain; for although\nwe have a moral assurance of these things, so great that it seems that,\nunless one is extravagant, no one can put them in doubt, nevertheless,\nwhen it is a question of metaphysical certainty, it cannot be denied, unless\none is out of one's mind, that it is sufficient reason for not being\ntotally sure to have noticed that we can in the same way imagine in dreams\nthat we have another body and that we see other stars and another\nearth, without it being so. For how shall we know that the thoughts\nthat occur to us during sleep are false, and that those we have when\nawake are not, if it often happens that the former are no less vivid\nand distinct than the latter? And however much the best minds may\nstudy, I do not believe they can give any reason sufficient to remove\nthat doubt, unless they presuppose the existence of God. For in the\nfirst place, that very rule that I took before, namely, that the\nthings we conceive very clearly and distinctly are all true, that very\nrule receives its certainty only from the fact that God is or exists,\nand that he is a perfect being, and that everything that is in us\ncomes from him; from which it follows that, our ideas or notions, when\nthey are clear and distinct, being real things and coming from God,\ncannot but be true in that respect too. So that if we quite often\nhave ideas that contain falsity, it is because there is something confused\nand obscure in them, and in this respect they participate in\nnothingness; that is, if they are thus confused in us, it is because we\nare not totally perfect. And it is evident that there is no less repugnance\nin admitting that falsity or imperfection as such proceeds from\nGod himself than in admitting that truth or perfection proceeds from\nnothing. But if we did not know that everything in us that is real and\ntrue comes from a perfect and infinite being, then, however clear and\ndistinct our ideas might be, there would be no reason whatever to assure\nus that they have the perfection of being true. Thus, the knowledge\nof God and of the soul having attested to us the certainty of that\nrule, it is quite easy to see that the dreams that we imagine when</p>\n<p>asleep must in no way make us doubt the truth of the thoughts we\nhave when awake. For if it should happen that in sleep a person had\na very clear and distinct idea, as, for example, if a geometer\ninvented a new demonstration, that would not be a reason for preventing\nit from being true; and as for the commonest error in many dreams,\nwhich consists in representing various objects to us in the\nsame way as the external senses represent them to us, it must not\nmatter to us that it gives us occasion to distrust the truth of such\nideas, because they can also often deceive us while awake, as\nthose who have jaundice see everything yellow, or as the stars and\nother very distant bodies seem to us much smaller than they are.\nFor, in the last resort, awake or asleep, we must never let ourselves\nbe persuaded except by the evidence of reason. And note well\nthat I say of reason, not of the imagination or of the senses; just\nas, because we see the Sun very clearly, we must not for that\nreason judge it to be of the size we see it; and we can very well\nimagine distinctly a lion's head stuck on the body of a goat, without\nhaving to conclude from this that the chimera exists in the world,\nfor reason does not tell us that what we thus see or imagine\nis true, but it does tell us that all our ideas or notions must\nhave some foundation of truth; for it would not be possible that\nGod, who is wholly perfect and true, should have put them in us without\nit; and since our reasonings are never so evident and so complete\nwhen we dream as when we are awake, although sometimes our\nimaginations are as vivid and expressive, and even more so,\nin sleep than in waking, reason tells us that, since all\nour thoughts cannot be true, because we are not totally\nperfect, truth must infallibly be found rather in those\nwe think when awake than in those we have in dreams.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-1-guía-de-lectura-simplificada-discurso-del-método-partes-ii-iii-y-iv\">Part\n1.\nSimplified reading guide: <em>Discourse\non Method</em>, parts II, III and IV</h2>\n<h3 id=\"hoja-para-el-alumnado\">Student sheet</h3>\n<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> The <em>Discourse on Method</em>\n(1637) is the work in which René Descartes (La Haye, 1596 –\nStockholm, 1650) tells, in the first person and in French (not\nin Latin, so as to reach more people), how he searched for secure\nknowledge. The compulsory reading parts are II (the method and\nits four rules), III (the provisional morality) and IV (methodical\ndoubt and ‘I think, therefore I am’). Part I (criticism of the\neducation received) and the preface are read as context.</p>\n<p><strong>How to use this guide.</strong> First read the passage in the\ntext; then read “What it says” to check that you have understood;\nanswer the two questions in your notebook (one or two sentences each); and\ncopy down the vocabulary you did not know.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"parte-ii-el-método-y-sus-cuatro-reglas\">Part II. The method and\nits four rules</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> Descartes is alone, in winter, in a\nroom with a stove, and decides to rebuild all his knowledge from\nscratch. He compares knowledge accumulated by tradition to an old city that\nhas grown without a plan: a city designed by a single\nengineer is more orderly. That is why he wants to build his knowledge on a foundation that\nis his alone. He reviews the sciences he studied: logic has good\nprecepts mixed with useless ones; geometrical analysis tires the imagination;\nalgebra is confusing. He needs a new method\nthat keeps the good of all three and avoids their defects. He sums it up in\n<strong>four rules</strong>: (1) <em>evidence</em>: accept nothing\nthat is not presented to the mind clearly and distinctly, avoiding\nhaste and prejudice; (2) <em>analysis</em>: divide each\ndifficulty into as many parts as possible; (3) <em>synthesis</em>:\nconduct one’s thoughts in order, from the simplest to the most complex;\n(4) <em>enumeration</em>: make reviews and checks so complete that\nnothing is overlooked. The model is mathematics, the only knowledge that gives\ncertainty.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Reason is one and the same in all\nhuman beings; error does not come from some being cleverer than others, but from\nnot using a method. The method copies the way mathematicians work: start from what is evident and deduce in an orderly way.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Write down the four rules of the method, summing up each\none in a single word.</li>\n<li>Why does Descartes take mathematics as his model rather than the logic or\nthe philosophy of his time?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Evidence</em>: what is presented to the mind so clearly\nand distinctly that it leaves no room for doubt.</li>\n<li><em>Clarity and distinctness</em>: an idea is clear when it is perceived\ndirectly; distinct when it is not confused with any other.</li>\n<li><em>Haste</em>: accepting something as true before having\nevidence (out of hurry).</li>\n<li><em>Prejudice</em>: refusing to accept an evident truth because\nof preconceptions.</li>\n<li><em>Analysis</em>: breaking a problem down into its simplest\nparts.</li>\n<li><em>Synthesis</em>: putting knowledge back together, going from the simple to the\ncomplex.</li>\n<li><em>Enumeration</em>: counting and reviewing so as not to omit anything.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"parte-iii-la-moral-provisional\">Part III. The provisional\nmorality</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> While he rebuilds his knowledge, Descartes\ncannot be left without criteria for living: it would be like pulling down the house\nwithout having anywhere to live during the works. That is why he gives himself a\n<strong>provisional morality</strong> of three or four maxims. First:\nobey the laws and customs of his country and keep his religion,\nfollowing the most moderate opinions. Second: be firm and resolute in\nhis actions, like a traveller lost in a forest who always walks\nstraight towards a fixed point instead of going round in circles. Third: conquer himself\nrather than fortune, changing his own desires rather than the\norder of the world (only our thoughts are within our power). And\nthe conclusion: devote his life to cultivating reason and seeking the truth.\nFrom this he draws his <strong>moral intellectualism</strong>: since the will\nonly follows what the understanding presents to it as good, “it is enough to judge\nwell in order to act well”.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Doubt is only for knowledge\n(theoretical), not for practical life: in order to act one has to decide, even if\nthere is no certainty. And good judgement is the condition of good\nconduct.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why does Descartes need a “provisional” morality while he seeks\nthe truth?</li>\n<li>Explain in your own words the phrase “it is enough to judge well in order to act\nwell”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Provisional morality</em>: a set of practical rules for living\nwhile knowledge is being rebuilt.</li>\n<li><em>Maxim</em>: a rule or principle of conduct.</li>\n<li><em>Fortune</em>: here, what does not depend on us (wealth,\nhealth, honours).</li>\n<li><em>Moral intellectualism</em>: the thesis that knowledge of the\ngood is a condition of good action (an inheritance from Socrates).</li>\n<li><em>Understanding</em>: the faculty of knowing; <em>will</em>:\nthe faculty of choosing.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"parte-iv-la-duda-metódica-y-el-pienso-luego-existo\">Part IV. Methodical\ndoubt and “I think, therefore I am”</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> In order to seek the truth, Descartes does the\nopposite of what he does in morality: he rejects as false everything that admits the\nslightest doubt, to see whether anything indubitable remains. He doubts in three steps: (1) the\n<strong>senses</strong>, because they sometimes deceive us; (2) <strong>reasoning</strong>, because people make mistakes even\nin geometry (paralogisms); (3) the <strong>whole of reality</strong>,\nbecause there is no sure sign that distinguishes waking from dreaming. But in the\nvery act of doubting he discovers a truth that withstands everything: in order to doubt,\nto think, it is necessary to exist. “<strong>I think, therefore I\nam</strong>” (<em>cogito, ergo sum</em>) is the first principle of\nhis philosophy. On examining what this “I” is, he concludes that it is a\n<strong>substance whose essence is to think</strong>, distinct from the body and\neasier to know than it. And from why that truth is certain he draws his\n<strong>general criterion of truth</strong>: whatever we conceive clearly\nand distinctly is true.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Doubt is not an end (as it is for the\nsceptics), but an instrument for demolishing what is false and finding a firm\nrock. That rock is the thinking subject: the “I” becomes the\nfoundation of all knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Put the three steps of doubt in order and say what each one\ncalls into question.</li>\n<li>Why does “I think, therefore I am” withstand even the dream\nhypothesis?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Methodical doubt</em>: suspending judgement on everything that is not\nindubitable, as a path (method) for seeking the truth.</li>\n<li><em>Paralogism</em>: a false piece of reasoning made in good faith.</li>\n<li><em>Cogito, ergo sum</em>: “I think, therefore I am”, the first certainty\nof the system.</li>\n<li><em>Substance</em>: that which exists without needing anything else in order to\nexist.</li>\n<li><em>Res cogitans</em>: thinking substance (the soul); <em>res\nextensa</em>: extended substance (matter).</li>\n<li><em>Criterion of truth</em>: a rule for recognising what is true\n(clarity and distinctness).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-2-los-cinco-fragmentos-de-la-selección-pau\">Part 2. The\nfive fragments of the PAU selection</h2>\n<p><strong>Note on the translation.</strong> The reference translation for the exam is that of Manuel García Morente. The fragments\nthat follow come from the AGORA selection, in a very close modern\ntranslation; differences of nuance are indicated where they matter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"fragmento-1--parte-ii-las-cuatro-reglas-del-método\">Fragment 1\n· Part II: the four rules of the method</h3>\n<p>Because of this, I thought that another method had to be sought that kept the\nadvantages of the three while avoiding their defects. And since the abundance of\nlaws often serves as an excuse for vices—a State being much\nbetter regulated when, though it has very few laws, they are strictly\nobserved—I thought that in place of the large number of rules that make up\nlogic, the following four would be enough for me, provided that I took\nthe firm and lasting decision to observe them always:</p>\n<p>The first was never to accept anything as true unless I knew\nevidently that it was so: that is, to avoid carefully\nhaste and prejudice, and to include in my judgements nothing more than\nwhat presented itself to my understanding so clearly and distinctly that it left\nno room for any doubt.</p>\n<p>The second was to divide each of the difficulties I examined into\nas many parts as possible and necessary to solve the\nproblem.</p>\n<p>The third was to conduct my thoughts in an orderly way, beginning\nwith the simplest objects, the easiest to know, in order to rise little by little,\nby degrees, to the knowledge of the most complex, assuming an\norder even among those that do not naturally precede one\nanother.</p>\n<p>And the last was to make, in every case, enumerations so complete and\nreviews so general that I could be sure of leaving nothing out.</p>\n<p>Those long chains of reasoning, so simple and easy (which\ngeometers usually use to arrive at their most difficult demonstrations),\ngave me occasion to imagine that all the things that can\nfall within human knowledge follow one another in the same\nway. […] I did not doubt that I ought to begin with those same things [mathematics],\nthough I expected from it no other benefit than to accustom\nmy understanding to feed on truths and not to be satisfied with false\nreasons. (Part II).</p>\n<h3\nid=\"fragmento-2--parte-ii-la-universalidad-del-método-y-la-espera-de-la-madurez\">Fragment\n2 · Part II: the universality of the method and waiting for maturity</h3>\n<p>But what pleased me most about this method was that, through it,\nI was sure of using my reason in all things, if not with complete\nperfection, at least as well as lay in my power. […]\nas I had not limited this method to any particular subject, I promised myself to\napply it to the other sciences with the same benefit with which I had\napplied it to the difficulties of algebra in mathematics. But I did not for that\nreason dare to undertake the study of all existing sciences,\nfor that would be contrary to the order the method prescribes. Realising\nthat all the principles of the sciences had to be taken from\nphilosophy, and finding in it as yet none that was certain,\nI thought that, before anything else, I had to establish those principles. […] I thought\nmoreover that I should not undertake that task until I had reached a more\nmature age than the twenty-three years I then had, and had devoted much time\nto preparing myself, both by wiping from my mind all the bad opinions\nI had previously received, and by accumulating many experiences that would later\nbe the subject of my reasonings. (Part II).</p>\n<h3\nid=\"fragmento-3--parte-iii-dedicar-la-vida-a-la-razón-el-intelectualismo-moral\">Fragment\n3 · Part III: devoting one’s life to reason; moral intellectualism</h3>\n<p>For this reason, I thought the best thing I could do was to persist in the\ntask before me, that is, to devote my whole life to cultivating\nreason and to advancing as far as possible in the knowledge of the truth,\nfollowing the method I had set myself. […] since God has given all of us\nsome light to distinguish between the true and the false, it would never\nhave seemed to me that I should be content with the opinions of others unless I\nhad first decided that I would use my own judgement to examine them\nwhen I had the time. […] For our will neither pursues nor avoids anything\nthat our understanding does not present to it as good or bad, and so\nit is enough to judge well in order to act well, and to judge as well as possible in order to act\nas well as possible, that is, to acquire all the virtues and goods\nthat can be attained. And when we are sure that this is so,\nwe shall always be content. (Part III).</p>\n<h3 id=\"fragmento-4--parte-iv-la-duda-metódica-y-el-cogito\">Fragment 4\n· Part IV: methodical doubt and the cogito</h3>\n<p>[…] as at that moment I wished to devote myself solely to seeking the\ntruth, I thought it necessary to do the very opposite and to reject as\nabsolutely false everything that could give rise to the slightest\ndoubt, to see whether after that anything remained in my belief that was\ntotally indubitable. Thus, since our senses sometimes\ndeceive us, I chose to suppose that nothing was as\nwe imagine it through them. And since there are men who make mistakes and\nparalogisms in reasoning (even on the simplest matters of\ngeometry), I thought that I too could err like anyone else, and\nI rejected as false all the reasons I had formerly taken for\ndemonstrations. And finally, considering that all those same\nthoughts that we have when awake can also come to us\nwhen asleep (without any of them being true), I resolved to\nimagine the following: that all the things that had ever entered\nmy mind were no more than illusions of my dreams.</p>\n<p>But, immediately, I noticed that while I wished to think that\neverything was false, it was necessary that I, who was thinking it, be something.\nAnd I noticed that this truth: I think, therefore I am, was so solid\nand certain that not even the most extravagant suppositions of the sceptics\ncould shake it, and I judged that I could accept it without misgiving as\nthe first principle of the philosophy I was seeking. (Part IV).</p>\n<h3\nid=\"fragmento-5--parte-iv-qué-soy-dualismo-y-el-criterio-de-verdad\">Fragment\n5 · Part IV: what I am (dualism) and the criterion of truth</h3>\n<p>After this, I examined attentively what I was, and I saw that I could imagine\nthat I had no body at all and that there was no world or place where I\nwas, but that I could not for that reason imagine that I was not; and I saw,\nlikewise, that from the very fact of thinking of doubting the truth of\nother things it followed, evidently and certainly, that I was something, whereas, on the\ncontrary, if I merely ceased to think I would have no reason\nto believe that I was. Thus I knew that I was a substance whose\nwhole essence or nature is to think, which needs no place in order\nto be and depends on no material thing. So this “I”, that is,\nthe soul (by which I am what I am), is entirely distinct from the\nbody, and even easier to know than the body, and even if the body\ndid not exist, the soul would still be all that it is.</p>\n<p>Next, I considered in general what a proposition needs\nin order to be true and certain; […] having noticed that in the\nproposition “I think, therefore I am” there was nothing that assured me\nthat it spoke the truth—except that I saw very clearly that in order to think\nit is necessary to be—I judged that I could take as a general rule the following:\nthat the things we conceive very clearly and distinctly are all\ntrue, but that the difficulty lies in noticing which are those\npropositions that we conceive distinctly. (Part IV).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-4-conceptos-clave-para-el-examen-diccionario\">Part 4.\nKey concepts for the exam (dictionary)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition for the exam</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Method</td>\n<td>A set of certain and easy rules which, if properly observed, prevent\ntaking the false for the true and make it possible to attain all possible\nknowledge.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>The property of what is presented to the mind so clearly and\ndistinctly that it cannot be doubted; it is attained by intuition, not\nthrough the senses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarity and distinctness</td>\n<td>The two conditions of evidence: an idea is clear if it is perceived\ndirectly, and distinct if it is not confused with another.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis and synthesis</td>\n<td>The two movements of the method: breaking the complex down into the simple\n(analysis) and putting it back together in an orderly way (synthesis).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Methodical doubt</td>\n<td>Suspending judgement on everything not indubitable, as a path to\nseeking the truth (not as a final position, unlike\nscepticism).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cogito, ergo sum</td>\n<td>“I think, therefore I am”: the first certainty, discovered by intuition,\nwhich expresses the union between thinking and existing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substance</td>\n<td>That which exists without needing anything else in order to exist. Descartes\ndistinguishes the thinking (soul), the extended (matter) and the infinite\n(God).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anthropological dualism</td>\n<td>A conception of the human being as the union of two distinct substances:\nsoul (thought) and body (extension).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criterion of truth</td>\n<td>A general rule drawn from the cogito: whatever is conceived clearly and distinctly is true.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Moral intellectualism</td>\n<td>The thesis that knowledge of the good is a condition of good\naction: “it is enough to judge well in order to act well”.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Provisional morality</td>\n<td>Practical rules (obedience, firmness, self-mastery) for living\nwhile knowledge is being rebuilt.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mathesis universalis</td>\n<td>The idea that all the sciences are one and the same human wisdom and\nshare a single method.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"
 },
 "hf-paquete-kant": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 18",
  "title": "Kant: What Is Enlightenment?",
  "html": "<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-0-el-texto-original-completo\">Part 0. The complete original\ntext</h2>\n<h3>Respuesta a la pregunta: ¿Qué es la Ilustración?</h3>\n<p><strong>Immanuel Kant</strong> (1784)</p>\n<p><strong>Original:</strong> «Beantwortung der Frage: Was ist Aufklärung?», <em>Berlinische Monatsschrift</em> 4 (diciembre de 1784), pp. 481-494. <strong>Edición:</strong> <em>Kant’s gesammelte Schriften</em>, ed. de la Academia Prusiana de las Ciencias (Akademie-Ausgabe, AA), tomo VIII, Berlín, 1912/1923, pp. 33-42. Entre corchetes, la página de la AA donde empieza el texto que sigue.</p>\n<p><strong>Traducción propia (2026)</strong>, del alemán. Texto completo. Licencia de la web: <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.es\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>.</p>\n<p>Los cuatro fragmentos que entran en la PAU se señalan así: <strong>[▶ Fragmento PAU n]</strong> … <strong>[Fin del fragmento PAU n ◀]</strong>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 35]</span> <strong class=\"pau\" id=\"pau1\">[▶ Fragmento PAU 1]</strong> La Ilustración (<em>Aufklärung</em>) es la salida del ser humano de una minoría de edad (<em>Unmündigkeit</em>) de la que él mismo tiene la culpa. La minoría de edad consiste en no ser capaz de usar el propio entendimiento sin que otro lo dirija. Y esa minoría de edad es culpa de uno mismo cuando no se debe a falta de entendimiento, sino a falta de decisión y de valor para usarlo sin que otro lo dirija. <em>Sapere aude!</em> ¡Atrévete a usar tu propio entendimiento! Tal es, pues, la divisa de la Ilustración.</p>\n<p>Pereza y cobardía: esas son las causas por las que tantísimos seres humanos, a pesar de que la naturaleza los declaró hace mucho libres de toda dirección ajena (<em>naturaliter maiorennes</em>, ‘mayores de edad por naturaleza’), siguen, y de buena gana, siendo menores de edad toda la vida; y por las que a otros les resulta tan fácil erigirse en sus tutores (<em>Vormünder</em>). ¡Qué cómodo es ser menor de edad! Si tengo un libro que tiene entendimiento por mí, un director espiritual que tiene conciencia por mí, un médico que me juzga la dieta, etc., no necesito esforzarme yo. Mientras pueda pagar, no me hace falta pensar: otros cargarán ya en mi lugar con ese engorroso trabajo. Que la gran mayoría de las personas (y con ella todo el bello sexo) tenga el paso a la mayoría de edad no solo por penoso, sino también por muy peligroso, de eso ya se encargan aquellos tutores que, con suma amabilidad, han asumido la vigilancia suprema sobre ellos. Primero entontecen a su ganado doméstico y se cuidan mucho de que esas criaturas mansas no osen dar ni un paso fuera del andador en que las han metido; luego les enseñan el peligro que las amenaza si intentan andar solas. Ese peligro, en realidad, no es tan grande, porque tras <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 36]</span> unas cuantas caídas acabarían aprendiendo a andar; pero un ejemplo así asusta y, por lo general, quita las ganas de cualquier intento posterior. <strong class=\"pau\">[Fin del fragmento PAU 1 ◀]</strong></p>\n<p>Por eso a cada individuo, por separado, le cuesta salir trabajosamente de la minoría de edad, convertida para él casi en naturaleza. Hasta le ha cogido cariño, y por ahora es de verdad incapaz de usar su propio entendimiento, porque nunca le dejaron probarlo. Reglamentos y fórmulas, esas herramientas mecánicas del uso racional —o, mejor dicho, del mal uso— de sus dotes naturales, son los grilletes de una minoría de edad perpetua. Aunque alguien se los quitara, daría un salto inseguro incluso sobre la zanja más estrecha, porque no está habituado a moverse con tanta libertad. Por eso son pocos los que, cultivando su propio espíritu, han logrado desenredarse de la minoría de edad y andar, con todo, con paso firme.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"pau\" id=\"pau2\">[▶ Fragmento PAU 2]</strong> En cambio, es más posible que un público se ilustre por sí solo; es más: si se le deja libertad, resulta casi inevitable. Porque siempre habrá algunos que piensan por su cuenta, incluso entre los tutores que se han puesto al frente de la gran masa; y estos, una vez que se hayan quitado ellos mismos de encima el yugo de la minoría de edad, irán extendiendo a su alrededor el espíritu de una valoración racional del valor propio y de la vocación de cada ser humano a pensar por sí mismo. Lo curioso es que el público al que esos tutores habían uncido antes a tal yugo los obliga después a ellos mismos a quedarse debajo, cuando lo azuzan a ello algunos de sus tutores que son incapaces de toda ilustración. Tan dañino es plantar prejuicios: al final se vengan de quienes los crearon o de sus predecesores. Por eso un público solo puede llegar despacio a la ilustración. Una revolución acaso derribe un despotismo personal y una opresión movida por la codicia o la ambición de mando, pero nunca producirá una reforma verdadera del modo de pensar (<em>Denkungsart</em>); al contrario, nuevos prejuicios servirán, igual que los viejos, de andaderas a la gran masa que no piensa.</p>\n<p>Ahora bien, para esta ilustración no se necesita más que libertad; y, en concreto, la más inofensiva de cuanto pueda llamarse libertad, a saber: la de hacer en todos los terrenos un uso público de la razón propia. Pero oigo gritar desde todas partes: ¡no razonéis (<em>räsonniert nicht</em>)! <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 37]</span> El oficial dice: ¡no razonéis, haced la instrucción! El consejero de Hacienda: ¡no razonéis, pagad! El clérigo: ¡no razonéis, creed! (En el mundo, un único señor dice: ¡razonad cuanto os plazca y sobre lo que os plazca; pero obedeced!) <strong class=\"pau\">[Fin del fragmento PAU 2 ◀]</strong> Aquí hay limitación de la libertad por todas partes. <strong class=\"pau\" id=\"pau3\">[▶ Fragmento PAU 3]</strong> Pero ¿qué limitación estorba a la ilustración, y cuál no la estorba, sino que hasta la favorece? Respondo: el uso público de la propia razón tiene que ser libre siempre, y es el único que puede traer la ilustración a los seres humanos; el uso privado, en cambio, puede a menudo restringirse muchísimo sin que la ilustración vea por ello especialmente frenado su avance. Por uso público de la razón propia (<em>öffentlicher Gebrauch der Vernunft</em>) entiendo el que alguien hace de ella como docto (<em>Gelehrter</em>) ante todo el público del mundo lector. Llamo uso privado (<em>Privatgebrauch</em>) al que le está permitido hacer de su razón en un determinado puesto o cargo civil que se le ha confiado. Para muchos asuntos que tocan al interés de la comunidad (<em>gemeines Wesen</em>) es necesario cierto mecanismo mediante el cual algunos de sus miembros han de mantenerse en una actitud meramente pasiva, para que el gobierno, gracias a una unanimidad artificial, los oriente hacia fines públicos o, al menos, les impida destruir esos fines. Aquí, desde luego, no está permitido razonar: hay que obedecer. Pero en cuanto esta pieza de la máquina se ve a sí misma, a la vez, como miembro de toda una comunidad, e incluso de la sociedad de los ciudadanos del mundo (<em>Weltbürgergesellschaft</em>), y por tanto en calidad de docto que se dirige por escrito a un público en sentido estricto, entonces sí puede razonar, sin que con ello sufran los asuntos a los que está destinado en parte como miembro pasivo. Así, sería muy pernicioso que un oficial al que sus superiores ordenan algo se pusiera, estando de servicio, a discutir en voz alta si esa orden es adecuada o útil; debe obedecer. Sin embargo, no sería justo impedirle que, en tanto que docto, haga observaciones sobre los fallos del servicio de guerra y las presente al juicio de su público. Un ciudadano no puede rehusar el pago de los tributos que le imponen; incluso una censura impertinente de esos gravámenes, cuando es él quien debe pagarlos, puede castigarse como escándalo (que podría dar pie a desobediencias generales). Y, sin embargo, ese mismo ciudadano no falta a su deber de ciudadano si, como docto, expresa en público lo que piensa contra lo <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 38]</span> improcedente o incluso injusto de tales cargas. Del mismo modo, un clérigo está obligado a enseñar a sus alumnos de catecismo y a su parroquia de acuerdo con el símbolo (<em>Symbol</em>, la confesión de fe) de la Iglesia a la que sirve, pues lo admitieron con esa condición. Pero como docto tiene plena libertad, y aun la misión, de comunicar al público todas sus ideas, examinadas con cuidado y bienintencionadas, sobre lo que en ese símbolo es erróneo, junto con propuestas para ordenar mejor la religión y la Iglesia. <strong class=\"pau\">[Fin del fragmento PAU 3 ◀]</strong> Y no hay en ello nada que pueda cargarle la conciencia. Porque lo que enseña en virtud de su cargo, como funcionario de la Iglesia, lo presenta como algo sobre lo que no tiene libre potestad para enseñar a su antojo, sino que se le ha contratado para exponerlo según lo prescrito, por encargo y en nombre ajeno. Dirá: «Nuestra Iglesia enseña tal o cual cosa; estos son los argumentos de que se vale». Saca entonces para su parroquia todo el provecho práctico de unos preceptos que él no firmaría con plena convicción, pero cuya exposición puede comprometerse a hacer, puesto que no cabe descartar del todo que encierren alguna verdad y, en todo caso, al menos no se encuentra en ellos nada contrario a la religión interior. Pues si creyera encontrar en ellos eso último, no podría ejercer su cargo con buena conciencia: tendría que dejarlo. Así pues, el uso que un maestro contratado hace de su razón ante su parroquia es solo un uso privado, porque esta, por numerosa que sea, nunca deja de ser una reunión doméstica; y en ese respecto él, como sacerdote, no es libre ni debe serlo, porque cumple un encargo ajeno. En cambio, como docto que habla por escrito al público propiamente dicho, es decir, al mundo, el clérigo, en el uso público que hace de su razón, goza de una libertad sin límites para valerse de su razón propia y expresarse en nombre propio. Pues que los tutores del pueblo (en lo espiritual) sean a su vez menores de edad es un despropósito que termina eternizando los despropósitos.</p>\n<p>Pero ¿no debería tener derecho una asociación de clérigos —un sínodo, por ejemplo, o una venerable <em>classis</em> (como se llama a sí misma entre los holandeses)— a obligarse unos a otros bajo juramento a un determinado símbolo inmutable, para ejercer así una tutela suprema e incesante sobre todos y cada uno de sus miembros y, por medio de ellos, sobre el <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 39]</span> pueblo, e incluso para hacerla eterna? Digo que eso es del todo imposible. Un contrato semejante, firmado para mantener al género humano apartado para siempre de toda ilustración ulterior, es completamente nulo y sin efecto, aunque lo confirmaran el poder supremo, las dietas imperiales y los tratados de paz más solemnes. Una época no puede coaligarse y juramentarse para poner a la siguiente en una situación en la que le sea imposible ampliar sus conocimientos (sobre todo los que tanto le importan), limpiarlos de errores y, en general, seguir avanzando en la ilustración. Eso sería un delito contra la naturaleza humana, cuyo fin originario consiste justamente en ese progreso; y los descendientes tienen, por tanto, pleno derecho a rechazar tales acuerdos por haberse tomado sin competencia y de forma sacrílega. La piedra de toque de todo lo que se pueda decidir como ley sobre un pueblo está en esta pregunta: ¿podría un pueblo darse a sí mismo semejante ley? Pues bien, eso sería posible durante un tiempo determinado y corto, como a la espera de algo mejor, para introducir cierto orden; a condición de dejar a la vez libre a cada ciudadano, y en especial al clérigo, para que en calidad de docto, públicamente, es decir, por escrito, haga sus observaciones acerca de lo defectuoso de la organización vigente, mientras el orden introducido sigue en pie; y ello hasta que la comprensión pública de la naturaleza de estos asuntos haya llegado tan lejos y se haya acreditado tanto que, uniendo sus voces (aunque no las de todos), pudiera llevarse ante el trono una propuesta para amparar a aquellas comunidades que, conforme a su idea de una comprensión mejor, se hubieran puesto de acuerdo en una organización religiosa distinta, sin impedírselo a quienes quisieran quedarse con lo de siempre. Pero ponerse de acuerdo en una constitución religiosa permanente, que nadie pueda poner en duda públicamente, aunque solo sea durante lo que dura una vida humana, y con ello anular, por así decirlo, un tramo del camino de la humanidad hacia su mejora y volverlo estéril, y hasta dañino para la posteridad, es algo absolutamente ilícito. <strong class=\"pau\" id=\"pau4\">[▶ Fragmento PAU 4]</strong> Cierto que una persona, en lo que a ella misma toca, y aun entonces solo por algún tiempo, puede aplazar la ilustración en aquello que le corresponde saber; pero renunciar a ella, sea para sí, y más todavía para la posteridad, equivale a violar los sagrados derechos de la humanidad y pisotearlos. Y lo que ni siquiera un pueblo puede decidir sobre sí mismo, <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 40]</span> menos aún puede decidirlo sobre él un monarca, ya que su autoridad de legislador se basa precisamente en que reúne en su voluntad la voluntad de todo el pueblo. Con tal de que vigile que toda mejora, verdadera o supuesta, sea compatible con el orden civil, por lo demás puede dejar que sus súbditos hagan por su cuenta lo que juzguen preciso para la salvación de sus almas. Eso no le incumbe; lo que sí le incumbe es impedir que uno estorbe con violencia a otro en su esfuerzo por definir y promover esa salvación con todas sus fuerzas. Incluso rebaja su majestad si se mete en esto y somete a la inspección de su gobierno los escritos con que sus súbditos intentan aclarar sus ideas: tanto si lo hace desde su propio y superior saber, con lo que se expone al reproche <em>Caesar non est supra grammaticos</em> (‘el César no está por encima de los gramáticos’), como, y todavía mucho más, si degrada su poder supremo hasta el punto de apoyar el despotismo espiritual de unos cuantos tiranos de su Estado contra el resto de sus súbditos.</p>\n<p>Si se pregunta, pues: ¿vivimos ahora en una época ilustrada?, la respuesta es: no; vivimos, eso sí, en una época de ilustración. Hoy por hoy, tal y como andan las cosas, a los seres humanos, tomados en conjunto, les queda aún mucho para ser ya capaces —o para que puedan siquiera llegar a serlo— de usar en asuntos de religión su propio entendimiento con seguridad y acierto, sin la dirección de otro. Pero sí tenemos señales claras de que ahora se les abre el terreno para avanzar libremente hacia ello, y de que van disminuyendo poco a poco los obstáculos a la ilustración general, es decir, a la salida de la minoría de edad de la que ellos mismos tienen la culpa. En este sentido, nuestra época es la de la Ilustración: el siglo de Federico. <strong class=\"pau\">[Fin del fragmento PAU 4 ◀]</strong></p>\n<p>Un príncipe que no encuentra indigno de él decir que tiene por deber no prescribir nada a los hombres en materia de religión, sino dejarles en ella completa libertad, y que por eso mismo rechaza hasta el altanero nombre de tolerancia, es él mismo ilustrado, y es digno de que el mundo de hoy y el de mañana, agradecidos, lo alaben como el primero que liberó de la minoría de edad al género humano, al menos por parte del gobierno, y dejó a cada cual en libertad de usar su propia razón en todo cuanto sea cuestión de conciencia. Bajo su reinado, clérigos respetabilísimos pueden, sin menoscabo de su deber oficial, someter al examen del mundo, libre y públicamente, <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 41]</span> en calidad de doctos, juicios y puntos de vista suyos que se apartan aquí o allá del símbolo aceptado; y con más razón cualquier otro a quien no limite ningún deber de cargo. Este espíritu de libertad se extiende también fuera de sus fronteras, incluso allí donde tiene que pelear con los obstáculos exteriores de un gobierno que se entiende mal a sí mismo. Porque a ese gobierno le pone ante los ojos un ejemplo de que con libertad no hay nada que temer por la tranquilidad pública ni por la concordia de la comunidad. Los seres humanos salen por sí mismos, poco a poco, de la rudeza, con tal de que nadie urda artificios a propósito para tenerlos en ella.</p>\n<p>He puesto el punto central de la ilustración —es decir, de la salida de los seres humanos de su minoría de edad culpable— sobre todo en los asuntos de religión, porque, en cuanto a las artes y las ciencias, quienes nos gobiernan no tienen interés alguno en hacer de tutores de sus súbditos; y porque, encima, esa minoría de edad es tanto la más dañina como la más deshonrosa de todas. Ahora bien, la mentalidad de un jefe de Estado que fomenta la primera llega todavía más lejos, y comprende que tampoco hay peligro, ni siquiera en lo que respecta a su legislación, en permitir a sus súbditos que usen públicamente su propia razón y que expongan públicamente al mundo sus ideas sobre una mejor redacción de las leyes, incluso con una crítica franca de las ya promulgadas; de esto tenemos un ejemplo brillante, en el que ningún monarca se ha adelantado aún a aquel a quien veneramos.</p>\n<p>Pero solo quien, ilustrado él mismo, no tiene miedo a las sombras y, al mismo tiempo, dispone de un ejército numeroso y bien disciplinado que garantice la tranquilidad pública, puede decir lo que no se atrevería a decir una república (<em>Freistaat</em>): ¡razonad cuanto os plazca y sobre lo que os plazca; pero obedeced! Así se muestra aquí una marcha extraña e inesperada de las cosas humanas; como sucede también en otros casos, si se la mira en conjunto, casi todo en ella es paradójico. Un grado mayor de libertad civil parece ventajoso para la libertad de espíritu del pueblo, y sin embargo le pone barreras insuperables; un grado menor de aquella, por el contrario, le da a esta espacio para desplegarse con todas sus fuerzas. Pues bien, cuando la naturaleza ha hecho brotar, bajo esta dura corteza, el germen que cuida con más ternura, a saber, la inclinación y la vocación a pensar libremente, ese germen repercute poco a poco en la mentalidad del pueblo (que así se va haciendo cada vez más capaz de la libertad de obrar) y, por último, incluso en los principios del gobierno, que encuentra beneficioso para sí mismo <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 42]</span> tratar al ser humano, que ya es más que una máquina, de acuerdo con su dignidad.*</p>\n<p>Königsberg (Prusia), 30 de septiembre de 1784.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>* <em>Nota de Kant.</em> En las <em>Noticias semanales</em> de Büsching del 13 de septiembre leo hoy, día 30 del mismo mes, el anuncio de la <em>Berlinische Monatsschrift</em> de este mes, donde se menciona la respuesta del señor Mendelssohn a esta misma pregunta. Aún no ha llegado a mis manos; si no, me habría hecho guardar la presente, que ahora puede quedar aquí solo como prueba de hasta qué punto el azar es capaz de hacer coincidir los pensamientos.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2\nid=\"parte-1-guía-de-lectura-simplificada-qué-es-la-ilustración\">Part 1.\nSimplified reading guide: <em>What Is Enlightenment?</em></h2>\n<h3 id=\"hoja-para-el-alumnado\">Student sheet</h3>\n<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> <em>An Answer to the Question: What Is\nEnlightenment?</em> is a short article that Immanuel Kant (Königsberg,\n1724-1804) published in 1784 in a journal. It answers the question that a\npastor had posed a year earlier. It is an optimistic text, typical of the\nEnlightenment atmosphere: it argues that humanity is emerging from its\nimmaturity and that for this it needs only freedom. Kant writes in\nthe Prussia of Frederick II, the “philosopher king”.</p>\n<p><strong>How to use this guide.</strong> First read the fragment in the\ntext; then read “What it says” to check that you have understood;\nanswer the two questions in your notebook (one or two sentences each); and\ncopy down the vocabulary you did not know.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"1-la-definición-de-ilustración-y-la-minoría-de-edad-culpable\">1.\nThe definition of Enlightenment and culpable immaturity</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> Kant defines Enlightenment as “the\nliberation of man from his culpable incapacity”. The incapacity is not being\nable to use one’s own intelligence without the guidance of another. It is\n<em>culpable</em> because its cause is not a lack of intelligence, but\na lack of decision and courage to think for oneself. That is why the\nmotto is “<strong>Sapere aude!</strong>: have the courage to use your\nown reason!”. Laziness and cowardice keep many people in immaturity:\nit is comfortable to have a book that thinks for me, a priest who has a\nconscience for me, a doctor who prescribes my diet. If I can pay,\nI do not need to think. The guardians, moreover, frighten their “domestic\nanimals” by showing them the dangers of walking alone, even though those\ndangers are not so serious.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Enlightenment is not a lack of\nintelligence, but a lack of courage: it is an attitude, a command that each\nperson gives himself. No one can enlighten another from outside; each person\nhas to dare to think.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why does Kant say that immaturity is “culpable” and not\nsimply a misfortune?</li>\n<li>What does “Sapere aude” mean and why is it the motto of the\nEnlightenment?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Enlightenment</em>: the human being’s emergence from culpable\nimmaturity.</li>\n<li><em>Immaturity</em>: the inability to use one’s own\nunderstanding without the guidance of another.</li>\n<li><em>Guardians</em>: those who think, decide or believe in place of\nothers.</li>\n<li><em>Sapere aude</em>: “dare to know” (in Latin), dare to\nthink for yourself.</li>\n<li><em>Emancipation</em>: liberation, the step into maturity.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"2-la-libertad-de-hacer-uso-público-de-la-razón\">2. The freedom\nto make public use of reason</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> To become enlightened only one\nthing is needed: <strong>freedom</strong>, and the most harmless kind of all, that of\nmaking <strong>public use</strong> of reason. Kant distinguishes two uses\nwith a sense different from the present-day one. The <strong>public use</strong> is\nthat which someone makes as a <em>scholar</em> (a learned man) before the reading public,\nin writing. The <strong>private use</strong> is that which he makes in his\n<em>post</em> within an institution (civil servant, officer,\nclergyman). In the private use one must obey: an officer may not\nquestion an order in the barracks, the citizen may not refuse to pay\ntaxes, the clergyman must preach according to the creed of his Church. But\nthose same people, as <em>experts</em> writing for the\npublic, can and should freely criticise what they consider\ndefective. The formula is: “argue as much as you like and about what\nyou like, but obey”.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Obedience in one’s post ensures that\ninstitutions function; public criticism allows society\nto improve. They are two different planes that do not contradict each other: one obeys as a\ncivil servant and argues as a scholar.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Explain with an example the difference between the public and the private use\nof reason.</li>\n<li>Why does Kant say that the freedom of public use is “the most\nharmless” of freedoms?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Public use of reason</em>: that which is made as a scholar before\nthe reading public.</li>\n<li><em>Private use of reason</em>: that which is made in a post or\nfunction within an institution.</li>\n<li><em>Scholar / expert</em>: someone who writes and argues before the\npublic.</li>\n<li><em>Mechanism</em>: the mechanical functioning of institutions,\nwhich demands obedience.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4\nid=\"3-la-revolución-no-basta-el-paternalismo-es-el-peor-despotismo\">3.\nRevolution is not enough; paternalism is the worst despotism</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> A <strong>revolution</strong> can\noverthrow a personal despotism, but it never achieves “a true\nreform in ways of thinking”: new prejudices will replace the\nold ones. Enlightenment is a slow process, not a coup. Kant also\nrejects <strong>paternalism</strong>: a government that set out to\nmake its people happy, treating them as a father treats children who are\nminors, would be “the greatest despotism imaginable”. The sovereign should not\nconcern himself with the salvation of his subjects’ souls (that is no business of his),\nbut only prevent some from hindering others in seeking their own\nhappiness. The mission of the State is to guarantee a framework for coexistence,\nnot to provide happiness. In religious matters, the enlightened ruler\ndoes not “tolerate” (like someone putting up with an evil), but recognises the right of\neveryone to run their life according to their own criteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Enlightenment is not imposed from above\nor by force: it is a change of mentality that each person makes for\nhimself. The State must limit itself to guaranteeing freedom, not to\ndeciding how citizens ought to be happy.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why is a revolution not enough to enlighten a people?</li>\n<li>Why does Kant call a paternalistic government “the greatest despotism\nimaginable”?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Revolution</em>: an abrupt change of political power, which does not change\nby itself the way of thinking.</li>\n<li><em>Paternalism</em>: a government that treats citizens as\nminors.</li>\n<li><em>Despotism</em>: absolute and arbitrary power.</li>\n<li><em>Toleration</em>: here, the mere “putting up with” of the different; Kant\ngoes beyond it with the recognition of the other’s right.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"4-época-de-ilustración-no-época-ilustrada\">4. An age of\nEnlightenment, not an enlightened age</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> At the end, Kant asks whether we live in\nan <em>enlightened</em> age. He answers no, but we do live in an\n<strong>age of Enlightenment</strong>: the field has been opened for\nmen to work freely towards their emancipation, and the obstacles are\ndiminishing. It is the “age of Frederick” (Frederick II of Prussia), the\nmonarch who allows free thinking. A long road remains: men\nare not yet in a position to use their own\nreason with confidence, above all in matters of religion. Enlightenment is a task\nin progress, not a result already achieved.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Kant distinguishes the process (an age\n<em>of</em> Enlightenment, in which we are living) from the result (an\n<em>enlightened</em> age, which has not yet been reached). Enlightenment is a\nhorizon, not a point of arrival.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What is the difference between an “enlightened age” and an “age of\nEnlightenment”?</li>\n<li>In what matter does Kant say that men are furthest from\nusing their own reason?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Enlightened age</em>: one in which Enlightenment has already been\nachieved.</li>\n<li><em>Age of Enlightenment</em>: one in which people are working\nto achieve it.</li>\n<li><em>Age of Frederick</em>: the Prussia of Frederick II, an example of an\nenlightened monarch.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-2-los-cuatro-fragmentos-de-la-selección-pau\">Part 2. The\nfour fragments of the PAU selection</h2>\n<p><strong>Traducción propia (2026)</strong>, del alemán. Texto completo. Licencia de la web: <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.es\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>.</p>\n<h3\nid=\"fragmento-1--definición-de-ilustración-pereza-y-cobardía\">Fragment\n1 · Definition of Enlightenment; laziness and cowardice</h3>\n<p>La Ilustración (<em>Aufklärung</em>) es la salida del ser humano de una minoría de edad (<em>Unmündigkeit</em>) de la que él mismo tiene la culpa. La minoría de edad consiste en no ser capaz de usar el propio entendimiento sin que otro lo dirija. Y esa minoría de edad es culpa de uno mismo cuando no se debe a falta de entendimiento, sino a falta de decisión y de valor para usarlo sin que otro lo dirija. <em>Sapere aude!</em> ¡Atrévete a usar tu propio entendimiento! Tal es, pues, la divisa de la Ilustración.</p>\n<p>Pereza y cobardía: esas son las causas por las que tantísimos seres humanos, a pesar de que la naturaleza los declaró hace mucho libres de toda dirección ajena (<em>naturaliter maiorennes</em>, ‘mayores de edad por naturaleza’), siguen, y de buena gana, siendo menores de edad toda la vida; y por las que a otros les resulta tan fácil erigirse en sus tutores (<em>Vormünder</em>). ¡Qué cómodo es ser menor de edad! Si tengo un libro que tiene entendimiento por mí, un director espiritual que tiene conciencia por mí, un médico que me juzga la dieta, etc., no necesito esforzarme yo. Mientras pueda pagar, no me hace falta pensar: otros cargarán ya en mi lugar con ese engorroso trabajo. Que la gran mayoría de las personas (y con ella todo el bello sexo) tenga el paso a la mayoría de edad no solo por penoso, sino también por muy peligroso, de eso ya se encargan aquellos tutores que, con suma amabilidad, han asumido la vigilancia suprema sobre ellos. Primero entontecen a su ganado doméstico y se cuidan mucho de que esas criaturas mansas no osen dar ni un paso fuera del andador en que las han metido; luego les enseñan el peligro que las amenaza si intentan andar solas. Ese peligro, en realidad, no es tan grande, porque tras <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 36]</span> unas cuantas caídas acabarían aprendiendo a andar; pero un ejemplo así asusta y, por lo general, quita las ganas de cualquier intento posterior.</p>\n<h3\nid=\"fragmento-2--el-público-se-ilustra-a-sí-mismo-la-revolución-no-basta\">Fragment\n2 · The public enlightens itself; revolution is not enough</h3>\n<p>En cambio, es más posible que un público se ilustre por sí solo; es más: si se le deja libertad, resulta casi inevitable. Porque siempre habrá algunos que piensan por su cuenta, incluso entre los tutores que se han puesto al frente de la gran masa; y estos, una vez que se hayan quitado ellos mismos de encima el yugo de la minoría de edad, irán extendiendo a su alrededor el espíritu de una valoración racional del valor propio y de la vocación de cada ser humano a pensar por sí mismo. Lo curioso es que el público al que esos tutores habían uncido antes a tal yugo los obliga después a ellos mismos a quedarse debajo, cuando lo azuzan a ello algunos de sus tutores que son incapaces de toda ilustración. Tan dañino es plantar prejuicios: al final se vengan de quienes los crearon o de sus predecesores. Por eso un público solo puede llegar despacio a la ilustración. Una revolución acaso derribe un despotismo personal y una opresión movida por la codicia o la ambición de mando, pero nunca producirá una reforma verdadera del modo de pensar (<em>Denkungsart</em>); al contrario, nuevos prejuicios servirán, igual que los viejos, de andaderas a la gran masa que no piensa.</p>\n<p>Ahora bien, para esta ilustración no se necesita más que libertad; y, en concreto, la más inofensiva de cuanto pueda llamarse libertad, a saber: la de hacer en todos los terrenos un uso público de la razón propia. Pero oigo gritar desde todas partes: ¡no razonéis (<em>räsonniert nicht</em>)! <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 37]</span> El oficial dice: ¡no razonéis, haced la instrucción! El consejero de Hacienda: ¡no razonéis, pagad! El clérigo: ¡no razonéis, creed! (En el mundo, un único señor dice: ¡razonad cuanto os plazca y sobre lo que os plazca; pero obedeced!)</p>\n<h3 id=\"fragmento-3--uso-público-y-uso-privado-de-la-razón\">Fragment 3\n· Public and private use of reason</h3>\n<p>Pero ¿qué limitación estorba a la ilustración, y cuál no la estorba, sino que hasta la favorece? Respondo: el uso público de la propia razón tiene que ser libre siempre, y es el único que puede traer la ilustración a los seres humanos; el uso privado, en cambio, puede a menudo restringirse muchísimo sin que la ilustración vea por ello especialmente frenado su avance. Por uso público de la razón propia (<em>öffentlicher Gebrauch der Vernunft</em>) entiendo el que alguien hace de ella como docto (<em>Gelehrter</em>) ante todo el público del mundo lector. Llamo uso privado (<em>Privatgebrauch</em>) al que le está permitido hacer de su razón en un determinado puesto o cargo civil que se le ha confiado. Para muchos asuntos que tocan al interés de la comunidad (<em>gemeines Wesen</em>) es necesario cierto mecanismo mediante el cual algunos de sus miembros han de mantenerse en una actitud meramente pasiva, para que el gobierno, gracias a una unanimidad artificial, los oriente hacia fines públicos o, al menos, les impida destruir esos fines. Aquí, desde luego, no está permitido razonar: hay que obedecer. Pero en cuanto esta pieza de la máquina se ve a sí misma, a la vez, como miembro de toda una comunidad, e incluso de la sociedad de los ciudadanos del mundo (<em>Weltbürgergesellschaft</em>), y por tanto en calidad de docto que se dirige por escrito a un público en sentido estricto, entonces sí puede razonar, sin que con ello sufran los asuntos a los que está destinado en parte como miembro pasivo. Así, sería muy pernicioso que un oficial al que sus superiores ordenan algo se pusiera, estando de servicio, a discutir en voz alta si esa orden es adecuada o útil; debe obedecer. Sin embargo, no sería justo impedirle que, en tanto que docto, haga observaciones sobre los fallos del servicio de guerra y las presente al juicio de su público. Un ciudadano no puede rehusar el pago de los tributos que le imponen; incluso una censura impertinente de esos gravámenes, cuando es él quien debe pagarlos, puede castigarse como escándalo (que podría dar pie a desobediencias generales). Y, sin embargo, ese mismo ciudadano no falta a su deber de ciudadano si, como docto, expresa en público lo que piensa contra lo <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 38]</span> improcedente o incluso injusto de tales cargas. Del mismo modo, un clérigo está obligado a enseñar a sus alumnos de catecismo y a su parroquia de acuerdo con el símbolo (<em>Symbol</em>, la confesión de fe) de la Iglesia a la que sirve, pues lo admitieron con esa condición. Pero como docto tiene plena libertad, y aun la misión, de comunicar al público todas sus ideas, examinadas con cuidado y bienintencionadas, sobre lo que en ese símbolo es erróneo, junto con propuestas para ordenar mejor la religión y la Iglesia.</p>\n<h3\nid=\"fragmento-4--la-renuncia-a-la-ilustración-viola-los-derechos-del-hombre-época-de-federico\">Fragment\n4 · Renouncing Enlightenment violates the rights of man; the age of\nFrederick</h3>\n<p>Cierto que una persona, en lo que a ella misma toca, y aun entonces solo por algún tiempo, puede aplazar la ilustración en aquello que le corresponde saber; pero renunciar a ella, sea para sí, y más todavía para la posteridad, equivale a violar los sagrados derechos de la humanidad y pisotearlos. Y lo que ni siquiera un pueblo puede decidir sobre sí mismo, <span class=\"aa\">[AA VIII 40]</span> menos aún puede decidirlo sobre él un monarca, ya que su autoridad de legislador se basa precisamente en que reúne en su voluntad la voluntad de todo el pueblo. Con tal de que vigile que toda mejora, verdadera o supuesta, sea compatible con el orden civil, por lo demás puede dejar que sus súbditos hagan por su cuenta lo que juzguen preciso para la salvación de sus almas. Eso no le incumbe; lo que sí le incumbe es impedir que uno estorbe con violencia a otro en su esfuerzo por definir y promover esa salvación con todas sus fuerzas. Incluso rebaja su majestad si se mete en esto y somete a la inspección de su gobierno los escritos con que sus súbditos intentan aclarar sus ideas: tanto si lo hace desde su propio y superior saber, con lo que se expone al reproche <em>Caesar non est supra grammaticos</em> (‘el César no está por encima de los gramáticos’), como, y todavía mucho más, si degrada su poder supremo hasta el punto de apoyar el despotismo espiritual de unos cuantos tiranos de su Estado contra el resto de sus súbditos.</p>\n<p>Si se pregunta, pues: ¿vivimos ahora en una época ilustrada?, la respuesta es: no; vivimos, eso sí, en una época de ilustración. Hoy por hoy, tal y como andan las cosas, a los seres humanos, tomados en conjunto, les queda aún mucho para ser ya capaces —o para que puedan siquiera llegar a serlo— de usar en asuntos de religión su propio entendimiento con seguridad y acierto, sin la dirección de otro. Pero sí tenemos señales claras de que ahora se les abre el terreno para avanzar libremente hacia ello, y de que van disminuyendo poco a poco los obstáculos a la ilustración general, es decir, a la salida de la minoría de edad de la que ellos mismos tienen la culpa. En este sentido, nuestra época es la de la Ilustración: el siglo de Federico.</p>\n<h2 id=\"parte-4-conceptos-clave-para-el-examen-diccionario\">Part 4.\nKey concepts for the exam (dictionary)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition for the exam</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Enlightenment</td>\n<td>The human being’s emergence from culpable immaturity: daring to\nuse one’s own understanding without the guidance of another.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Immaturity</td>\n<td>The inability to use one’s own understanding without the direction of\nanother; it is culpable when it is due to a lack of courage, not of\nintelligence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sapere aude</td>\n<td>“Dare to know”: the motto of the Enlightenment, which demands courage to\nthink for oneself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Autonomy</td>\n<td>The capacity to give oneself one’s own law; to think and decide without\nguardians.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public use of reason</td>\n<td>That which is made as a scholar before the reading public, in writing;\nit must always be free.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private use of reason</td>\n<td>That which is made in a post or function within an institution;\nit may be restricted and demands obedience.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guardians</td>\n<td>Those who think, believe or decide in place of others, keeping them\nin immaturity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paternalism</td>\n<td>A government that treats citizens as minors, deciding\nfor them how to be happy; for Kant, the greatest despotism.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enlightened age / age of Enlightenment</td>\n<td>The first is the one already achieved; the second, the process under way (our\nown, the “age of Frederick”).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Toleration</td>\n<td>The mere “putting up with” of the different; Kant goes beyond it with the recognition\nof everyone’s right to run their own life.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"
 },
 "hf-paquete-nietzsche": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 21",
  "title": "Nietzsche: Ecce Homo, ‘Why I Am a Destiny’",
  "html": "<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-0-el-texto-original-completo\">Part 0. The complete original\ntext</h2>\n<p><strong>Friedrich Nietzsche</strong>, <em>Ecce homo. Wie man wird, was man ist</em> (escrito en 1888; publicado en 1908), capítulo «Warum ich ein Schicksal bin», §§ 1-9.</p>\n<p>Original: edición crítica de G. Colli y M. Montinari, <em>Sämtliche Werke. Kritische Studienausgabe</em> (KSA), tomo 6, pp. 365-374 (Berlín / Múnich, De Gruyter / dtv, 1980). Texto alemán de dominio público.</p>\n<p>Traducción propia (2026), del alemán. Licencia de la web: <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.es\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>.</p>\n<p>Las marcas <span class=\"pau\">[PAU n ▸]</span> y <span class=\"pau\">[◂ PAU n]</span> indican dónde empieza y dónde termina cada uno de los cuatro pasajes que entran en la PAU.</p>\n<h3>Por qué soy un destino</h3>\n<h3>1</h3>\n<p>Conozco mi suerte (<em>Loos</em>). Algún día mi nombre quedará asociado a la memoria de algo descomunal: a una crisis como no la ha habido nunca en la tierra, al más hondo choque de conciencias, a una decisión conjurada contra todo lo que hasta entonces se había creído, exigido, santificado. <span class=\"pau\">[PAU 1 ▸]</span> No soy un hombre, soy dinamita. — Pese a todo ello, no tengo nada de fundador de religiones: las religiones son cosa de la plebe; después de tocar a personas religiosas necesito lavarme las manos… No quiero «creyentes»; pienso que tengo demasiada malicia incluso para creer en mí mismo; jamás hablo a las masas… Me da un miedo atroz que un día me declaren santo: ya se adivinará por qué saco este libro antes; ha de impedir que se hagan tropelías conmigo… No quiero ser un santo; antes prefiero ser un bufón… Quizá soy un bufón… Y aun así —mejor dicho, no «aun así», porque hasta hoy nada ha habido más embustero que los santos— es la verdad la que habla por mi boca. — Pero mi verdad es terrible: porque hasta hoy a la mentira se la llamaba verdad. — Transvaloración de todos los valores (<em>Umwertung aller Werte</em>): esa es mi fórmula para un acto en el que la humanidad toma conciencia de sí misma en el grado más alto, y que en mi persona se hizo carne y genio. Es mi suerte la que dispone que yo haya de ser el primer hombre decente, que me sepa enfrentado a la mendacidad de milenios… Yo he sido el primero en descubrir la verdad, porque fui el primero en percibir la mentira como mentira: en olerla… Mi genio está en mi olfato… Contradigo como nadie ha contradicho nunca y, sin embargo, soy lo opuesto de un espíritu que dice no. Soy un mensajero de buenas noticias como no ha existido otro; conozco tareas de tal altura que hasta hoy no había ni siquiera un concepto para ellas; solo a partir de mí vuelve a haber esperanzas. <span class=\"pau\">[◂ PAU 1]</span> Con todo ello soy también, por necesidad, el hombre de la fatalidad (<em>Verhängnis</em>). Porque, cuando la verdad entre en combate con la mentira milenaria, habrá sacudidas, una convulsión de terremotos, un desplazamiento de montes y valles como jamás se ha soñado nada semejante. El concepto de política quedará entonces disuelto por entero en una guerra entre espíritus; cuantas construcciones de poder tenía la vieja sociedad saltarán por los aires —todas descansan sobre la mentira—: habrá guerras como nunca las ha habido en la tierra. Solo a partir de mí hay en la tierra gran política.</p>\n<h3>2</h3>\n<p>¿Se busca una fórmula para semejante destino, que se hace hombre? — La hallará el lector en mi <em>Zaratustra</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>«—y el que quiera ser creador en lo bueno y en lo malo, ese tiene que ser primero un destructor y quebrar valores.</p>\n<p>Así pues, el mal supremo forma parte de la bondad suprema: pero esta es la creadora».</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"pau\">[PAU 2 ▸]</span> Soy, con muchísima diferencia, el hombre más temible que haya habido hasta hoy; esto no quita que vaya a ser el más bienhechor. Conozco el gusto por destruir en un grado que se corresponde con mi fuerza para destruir; en ambas cosas obedezco a mi naturaleza dionisíaca, que no sabe separar el hacer que no del decir sí. Soy el primer inmoralista (<em>Immoralist</em>): con ello soy el destructor por excelencia. —</p>\n<h3>3</h3>\n<p>No me han preguntado, aunque deberían haberlo hecho, qué quiere decir justamente en mi boca —la del primer inmoralista— el nombre de Zaratustra: porque lo que hace de aquel persa algo tan inmensamente singular en la historia es exactamente lo contrario. Zaratustra fue el primero que vio en la lucha del bien y del mal la verdadera rueda que mueve el engranaje de las cosas; trasladar la moral al plano metafísico —como fuerza, causa y fin en sí— fue obra suya. Pero en el fondo la pregunta contendría ya la respuesta. Fue Zaratustra quien creó este error, el más funesto de todos, la moral: por consiguiente, tiene que ser también el primero en reconocerlo. Y no solo porque en este asunto tiene una experiencia más larga y más amplia que cualquier otro pensador —la historia entera no es sino la refutación experimental de la tesis del llamado «orden moral del mundo»—; lo más importante es que Zaratustra es más veraz que cualquier otro pensador. Su doctrina, y únicamente ella, tiene la veracidad (<em>Wahrhaftigkeit</em>) como virtud suprema; es decir, lo opuesto a la cobardía del «idealista», que ante la realidad se da a la fuga. Zaratustra tiene más valor en el cuerpo que todos los pensadores juntos. <span class=\"pau\">[◂ PAU 2]</span> Decir la verdad y tirar bien con el arco: esa, la virtud persa. — ¿Me entienden?… Que la moral se supere a sí misma por veracidad, que el moralista se supere a sí mismo convirtiéndose en su contrario —en mí—: eso significa en mi boca el nombre de Zaratustra.</p>\n<h3>4</h3>\n<p><span class=\"pau\">[PAU 3 ▸]</span> En el fondo, mi palabra «inmoralista» encierra dos negaciones. Niego, de un lado, un tipo de hombre que hasta hoy pasaba por el más alto: los buenos, los benévolos, los bienhechores; niego, por otro, cierta clase de moral que ha logrado vigencia y dominio como moral en sí, es decir, la moral de la <em>décadence</em> o, dicho de forma más tangible, la moral cristiana. Cabría considerar más decisiva la segunda contradicción, puesto que el sobreestimar la bondad y la benevolencia lo cuento ya, en líneas generales, entre las consecuencias de la décadence, como síntoma de debilidad, como algo inconciliable con una vida que asciende y dice sí: en el decir sí, negar y destruir son condición. — Por ahora me quedo con la psicología del hombre bueno. Si se quiere tasar cuánto vale un tipo de hombre, hay que calcular el precio que cuesta mantenerlo, hay que saber de qué condiciones depende su existencia. Lo que hace posible que existan los buenos es la mentira; con otras palabras: negarse a ver, a toda costa, cómo está hecha, en el fondo, la realidad: que no es, en efecto, de tal naturaleza que despierte a cada momento instintos de benevolencia, y mucho menos de tal naturaleza que tolere a cada momento la intromisión de unas manos bonachonas y miopes. Considerar las situaciones de penuria de todo tipo como una objeción, como algo que hay que abolir, es la <em>niaiserie</em> (la necedad) por excelencia; en conjunto, una auténtica calamidad por sus efectos, una fatalidad de estupidez, casi tan necio como lo sería querer abolir el mal tiempo, por compasión, pongamos, con los pobres… <span class=\"pau\">[◂ PAU 3]</span> En la gran economía del todo, lo que la realidad tiene de terrible (en los afectos, en los deseos, en la voluntad de poder, <em>Wille zur Macht</em>) es necesario en una medida incalculablemente mayor que esa forma de pequeña felicidad a la que llaman «bondad»; hasta hay que ser indulgente para concederle a esta, ya que tiene su condición en la mendacidad de los instintos, siquiera un sitio. Ya tendré ocasión, y grande, de probar los efectos desmedidamente siniestros que ha tenido para toda la historia el optimismo, ese aborto de los <em>homines optimi</em> («los mejores»). Zaratustra, el primero que comprendió que el optimista es <em>décadent</em> lo mismo que el pesimista, y acaso más dañino, dice: <em>los hombres buenos nunca dicen la verdad. Los buenos os enseñaron costas falsas y falsas seguridades; nacisteis entre mentiras de los buenos y en ellas os guarecisteis. Todo lo han falseado y retorcido los buenos hasta la raíz.</em> Por suerte, el mundo no está construido sobre unos instintos tales que justamente el bonachón ganado de rebaño halle allí su angosta felicidad; pedir que todo se haga «hombre bueno», animal de rebaño, de ojos azules, benévolo, «alma bella» o, como desea el señor Herbert Spencer, altruista, equivaldría a quitarle a la existencia su gran carácter, equivaldría a castrar a la humanidad y a rebajarla a una mísera chinería. — ¡Y eso se ha intentado!… Eso, precisamente, se llamó moral… Por eso Zaratustra da a los buenos el nombre ya de «los últimos hombres», ya de «el principio del fin»; ante todo los percibe como la especie de hombre más dañina, porque imponen su existencia a costa de la verdad no menos que a costa del futuro.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>«Los buenos: esos no saben crear, esos son siempre el principio del fin…</p>\n<p>…crucifican a quien escribe valores nuevos sobre tablas nuevas, sacrifican para sí el futuro, ¡crucifican el futuro entero del hombre!</p>\n<p>Los buenos: esos fueron siempre el principio del fin…</p>\n<p>Y hagan el daño que hagan los calumniadores del mundo: el daño que hacen los buenos es el más dañino de los daños».</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>5</h3>\n<p>Zaratustra, primer psicólogo que han tenido los buenos, es —por tanto— amigo de los malvados. Cuando una especie de hombre de la décadence ha subido al rango de especie suprema, eso solo pudo suceder a costa de su especie contraria, la especie de hombre fuerte y segura de la vida. Si el animal de rebaño reluce con el resplandor de la más pura virtud, el hombre excepcional tiene que haber sido rebajado de valor hasta pasar por malvado. Si la mendacidad a toda costa reclama la palabra «verdad» para su óptica, entonces al auténticamente veraz habrá que encontrarlo con los peores nombres. Zaratustra no deja ninguna duda en esto: dice que fue precisamente conocer a los buenos, a los «mejores», lo que le produjo horror ante el hombre en general; que de esa aversión le nacieron alas «para alejarse flotando hacia futuros lejanos»; no esconde que el tipo de hombre que él propone, relativamente sobrehumano, lo es precisamente respecto de los buenos, y que los buenos y los justos llamarían diablo a su superhombre (<em>Übermensch</em>)…</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>«Vosotros, los hombres más altos con que se encontraron mis ojos, esta es mi duda sobre vosotros y mi risa secreta: ¡adivino que a mi superhombre lo llamaríais… diablo!</p>\n<p>Tan extraños sois en vuestra alma a lo grande que el superhombre os resultaría terrible en su bondad…».</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Es aquí, y en ningún otro sitio, donde hay que tomar impulso para comprender lo que quiere Zaratustra: la especie de hombre que concibe él concibe la realidad como es; es lo bastante fuerte para ello; no le es ajena ni vive apartada de ella: es la realidad misma, lleva todavía en sí todo lo que esta tiene de terrible y de dudoso; solo así cabe grandeza en el hombre…</p>\n<h3>6</h3>\n<p>— Pero he escogido la palabra «inmoralista» como emblema, como distintivo de honor, también en otro sentido; estoy orgulloso de tener esta palabra que me separa de toda la humanidad. Nadie hasta ahora ha sentido la moral cristiana por debajo de sí: para eso hacía falta una altura, una amplitud de mirada, una hondura y una abismalidad psicológicas hasta hoy totalmente inauditas. La moral cristiana ha sido hasta hoy la Circe de todos los pensadores: todos ellos la servían. — ¿Quién descendió antes que yo a las cuevas de las que mana el aliento venenoso de esta clase de ideal —¡la calumnia del mundo!—? ¿Quién se atrevió ni siquiera a sospechar que son cuevas? ¿Quién, entre los filósofos, fue psicólogo antes de mí, y no más bien su contrario, «estafador superior», «idealista»? Antes de mí no había psicología en absoluto. — Ser aquí el primero puede ser una maldición; en cualquier caso, es un destino: porque también se desprecia siendo el primero… Mi peligro es el asco ante el hombre…</p>\n<h3>7</h3>\n<p>¿Me han entendido? — Lo que me delimita, lo que me pone aparte frente al resto entero de la humanidad, es que he descubierto la moral cristiana. De ahí que necesitase una palabra que llevara el sentido de un reto a todo el mundo. No haber abierto antes los ojos en esto lo considero la mayor inmundicia que la humanidad lleva en la conciencia: autoengaño hecho instinto, una voluntad de principio de no ver ningún acontecer, ninguna causalidad, ninguna realidad; una falsificación de moneda <em>in psychologicis</em> (en materia psicológica) que llega hasta el crimen. La ceguera ante el cristianismo es el crimen por excelencia: el crimen cometido contra la vida… Milenios, pueblos, primeros y últimos, filósofos y viejecitas —descontados cinco o seis instantes de la historia, y yo como séptimo—: en este punto todos se merecen unos a otros. El cristiano ha sido hasta hoy el «ser moral», un caso curioso sin parangón; y como «ser moral», más absurdo, más mentiroso, más vanidoso, más frívolo, más dañino para sí mismo de lo que podría soñar el mayor despreciador del género humano. La moral cristiana: la más maligna forma de la voluntad de mentir, la Circe propiamente dicha de la humanidad, aquello que la ha estragado. No es el error como error lo que me horroriza a la vista de esto, no es la carencia milenaria de «buena voluntad», de disciplina, de decoro, de valor en lo espiritual, que se delata en su victoria: es la carencia de naturaleza, es el hecho, absolutamente estremecedor, de que la contranaturaleza misma haya recibido como moral los máximos honores y haya quedado colgada, como ley, como imperativo categórico, sobre la humanidad… ¡Equivocarse de este modo, no un individuo, no un pueblo, sino la humanidad entera!… ¡Que se enseñara a despreciar los instintos primordiales de la vida; que se inventara con mentiras un «alma», un «espíritu», para echar a perder el cuerpo; que se enseñe a sentir algo impuro en el presupuesto mismo de la vida, la sexualidad; que se busque el principio malo en la necesidad más profunda para florecer, en el estricto egoísmo (¡ya la palabra es calumniosa!); que, al revés, en el signo típico del declive y de la contradicción entre los instintos, en el «desprendimiento de sí», en perder el propio centro de gravedad, en la «despersonalización», en el «amor al prójimo» (¡manía del prójimo!), se vea el valor más alto, qué digo, el valor en sí!… ¿Cómo? ¿Estaría la humanidad misma en décadence? ¿Lo habría estado siempre? — Lo que es seguro es que lo único que se le ha enseñado como valores supremos son valores de décadence. La moral del desprendimiento del yo (<em>Entselbstung</em>) es la moral del declive por excelencia: el hecho «me hundo» traducido al imperativo «todos vosotros debéis hundiros», ¡y no solo al imperativo!… Esta única moral enseñada hasta hoy, la moral del desprendimiento del yo, delata una voluntad de acabar; en lo más hondo, niega la vida. — Aquí cabría todavía la posibilidad de que la degenerada no fuese la humanidad, sino únicamente esa clase parasitaria de hombre, la sacerdotal, que mediante la moral se ha encumbrado a fuerza de mentiras hasta convertirse en quien fija los valores de la humanidad; que adivinó que la moral cristiana era su medio de poder… Y así es, en efecto; esta es mi conclusión: los maestros, los conductores de la humanidad, teólogos todos, eran todos también <em>décadents</em>: de ahí que se transvaloraran todos los valores en sentido hostil a la vida, de ahí la moral… Definición de moral: moral es la idiosincrasia de los décadents con la segunda intención de vengarse de la vida, y lo consigue. Esta definición me importa. —</p>\n<h3>8</h3>\n<p>— ¿Me han entendido? — Ni una palabra he dicho que no hubiera dicho ya cinco años atrás por boca de Zaratustra. — <span class=\"pau\">[PAU 4 ▸]</span> Descubrir la moral cristiana es un acontecimiento sin igual, una auténtica catástrofe. Quien saca a la luz lo que ella es constituye una <em>force majeure</em> (una fuerza mayor), un destino: rompe la historia de la humanidad en dos pedazos. Hay un antes de él y un después de él… Lo que golpeó el rayo de la verdad fue justo lo que hasta hoy estaba más alto: quien comprenda lo que allí quedó destruido, que mire si aún le queda algo entre las manos. Todo lo que hasta hoy se llamaba «verdad» queda reconocido como la forma más dañina, más traicionera, más soterrada de la mentira; el pretexto sagrado de «mejorar» a la humanidad, como la artimaña para chuparle la sangre a la vida, para desangrarla. La moral como vampirismo… El que descubre la moral descubre a la vez que carecen de valor todos los valores que la gente cree o ha creído; ya no ve nada digno de veneración en los tipos humanos más venerados, ni siquiera en los que han sido declarados santos; ve en ellos la clase más funesta de engendros, funestos porque fascinaban… ¡El concepto «Dios», inventado como concepto opuesto a la vida; en él, todo lo dañino, lo que envenena, lo que calumnia, toda la enemistad a muerte contra la vida, reunidos en una unidad espantosa! ¡El concepto de «más allá», de «mundo verdadero», inventado para quitar valor al único mundo que hay, para no dejarle a nuestra realidad de la tierra ni una meta, ni una razón, ni una tarea! ¡El concepto de «alma», de «espíritu» y, al final, hasta de «alma inmortal», inventado con el fin de despreciar el cuerpo, de hacerlo enfermar —de hacerlo «santo»—, de tratar con una ligereza estremecedora todas las cosas que en la vida merecen seriedad: las cuestiones de la alimentación, la vivienda, la dieta del espíritu, el cuidado de los enfermos, la limpieza, el tiempo atmosférico! ¡La «salvación del alma» en vez de la salud, o sea, una <em>folie circulaire</em> (locura circular) entre los espasmos de la penitencia y la histeria de la redención! ¡El concepto «pecado», inventado junto con el instrumento de tortura que le corresponde, el concepto «libre albedrío», para confundir los instintos, para convertir en segunda naturaleza la desconfianza hacia los instintos! <span class=\"pau\">[◂ PAU 4]</span> ¡En el concepto de lo «desinteresado», del que «se niega a sí mismo», el auténtico emblema de la décadence —el dejarse seducir por lo dañino, el no-saber-ya-encontrar-el-propio-provecho, la autodestrucción— convertido en el signo de valor por antonomasia, en «deber», en «santidad», en lo «divino» del hombre! Por último —y es lo más espantoso—, en el concepto de hombre bueno, se ha tomado partido por todo lo débil, lo enfermo, lo malogrado, lo que sufre de sí mismo, por todo lo que debe perecer; se ha contrariado la ley de la selección; se ha hecho un ideal de la oposición al hombre orgulloso y bien logrado, al que dice sí, al que está seguro del futuro y responde de él: a este se le llama ahora el malvado… ¡Y todo eso se creyó como moral! — <em>Écrasez l’infâme!</em> («¡Aplastad lo infame!») —</p>\n<h3>9</h3>\n<p>— ¿Me han entendido? — <em>Dioniso contra el Crucificado…</em></p>\n<h2\nid=\"parte-1-guía-de-lectura-simplificada-ecce-homo-por-qué-soy-un-destino\">Part\n1. Simplified reading guide: <em>Ecce Homo</em>, “Why I Am a\nDestiny”</h2>\n<h3 id=\"hoja-para-el-alumnado\">Student sheet</h3>\n<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> <em>Ecce Homo</em> (“Behold the\nman”) is the intellectual autobiography that Friedrich Nietzsche\n(Röcken, 1844 - Weimar, 1900) wrote in 1888, a few months before his\nmental collapse. The title is a provocation: they are the words with\nwhich Pilate presented the scourged Jesus to the crowd. Nietzsche, who\ncalls himself “the Antichrist”, uses them ironically to present himself\nas a man of flesh and blood, not as a saint. “Why I Am a\nDestiny” is the last chapter: in it he presents himself as a turning\npoint that divides history in two.</p>\n<p><strong>How to use this guide.</strong> First read the fragment in the\ntext; then read “What it says” to check that you have understood;\nanswer the two questions in your notebook (one or two sentences each); and\ncopy down the vocabulary you did not know.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"1-yo-no-soy-un-hombre-soy-dinamita-fragmento-1-1\">1. “I am not\na man, I am dynamite” (fragment 1, §1)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> Nietzsche presents himself as “dynamite”: a\nforce that will blow up the foundations of Western civilisation. He makes clear that he is not the founder of any religion, that he does not want\n“believers” or to be declared a saint; he prefers to be a “buffoon”, because\nnothing has been more mendacious than saints. His truth is “terrible”:\nuntil now lies have been called truth. His formula is the\n“<strong>revaluation of all values</strong>”, an act of\n“supreme self-examination” (autognosis) on the part of humanity. He feels himself the\nfirst “decent” man, the first to have smelt the lie as a lie. And yet he presents himself as a “joyful messenger”: from\nhim on there are hopes again.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Nietzsche does not propose to reform\nculture, but to demolish it: the Platonic-Christian tradition has called\n“truth” a lie hostile to life. Revaluation means inverting\nthose values in order to affirm life.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why does Nietzsche prefer to be a “buffoon” rather than a\n“saint”?</li>\n<li>What does “revaluation of all values” mean?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Dynamite</em>: a metaphor for his historical function: blowing up\nthe pillars of civilisation.</li>\n<li><em>Revaluation</em> (Umwertung): the critique and replacement of\nvalues contrary to life with others that affirm it.</li>\n<li><em>Autognosis</em>: knowledge of oneself; here, the moment at\nwhich humanity recognises that its values are lies.</li>\n<li><em>Mendacity</em>: the habit of lying; the lie of millennia of\ntradition.</li>\n<li><em>Joyful messenger</em>: he who brings the good news that, after\nthe “death of God”, man is free.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"2-el-primer-inmoralista-y-zaratustra-fragmento-2-2-3\">2. The\nfirst immoralist and Zarathustra (fragment 2, §§2-3)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> Nietzsche calls himself “the most\nterrible man” and, at the same time, “the most beneficent”: he knows the pleasure of destroying\nto the degree that corresponds to his strength, and in both he obeys his\n“Dionysian nature”, which does not separate saying yes from saying no. He\ndefines himself as “the first <strong>immoralist</strong>”, the annihilator\npar excellence. To explain this he turns to <strong>Zarathustra</strong>, the\nPersian sage who was the first to see in the struggle of good and evil the\nwheel that moves things: he created morality, that “fatal error”. For that reason\nZarathustra must also be the first to recognise it. His supreme virtue\nis <strong>truthfulness</strong>: the opposite of the cowardice of the\n“idealist”, who flees from reality by inventing perfect worlds.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Being an “immoralist” does not mean defending\nwickedness, but placing oneself outside the morality that has dominated the West. The\ndestruction of that morality is necessary in order to be able to affirm life (to say\nyes).</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What does it mean that Nietzsche calls himself “the first immoralist”?</li>\n<li>What role does Zarathustra play in the text and why must he be the\nfirst to “recognise” his error?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Immoralist</em>: someone who places himself outside traditional morality\n(not someone who lacks values).</li>\n<li><em>Dionysian nature</em>: a vital impulse that accepts the whole of\nlife, with its pain and its destruction.</li>\n<li><em>Zarathustra</em>: the Persian sage who, according to Nietzsche, created\nmorality; a symbol of the one who must overcome it.</li>\n<li><em>Truthfulness</em>: the radical honesty of looking at reality\nsquarely, without consolations.</li>\n<li><em>Idealist</em>: someone who invents an ideal world to flee\nreality; a symptom of cowardice.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4\nid=\"3-las-dos-negaciones-y-la-psicología-del-hombre-bueno-fragmento-3-4\">3.\nThe two negations and the psychology of the “good man” (fragment 3,\n§4)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> The word “immoralist” contains two\nnegations. First: it denies a type of man considered supreme, the\n“good”, the “benevolent”, the “beneficent”. Second: it denies a morality that\nhas come to count as valid in itself, the morality of <em>décadence</em>, Christian\nmorality. The overestimation of goodness is a symptom of\nweakness, incompatible with an ascending life: “<strong>negating and\nannihilating are conditions of saying yes</strong>”. To estimate the value\nof a type of man one must calculate the price of its preservation: the\ncondition of existence of the “good” is the lie, the refusal to see\nhow reality is constituted. To regard danger as something that\nmust be eliminated is the greatest stupidity, almost as stupid as wanting to\neliminate bad weather out of compassion for the poor.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Traditional goodness is not virtue, but\nweakness in disguise: it is born of fear of the harshness of life. Affirming\nlife demands accepting its risk and its pain, not eliminating them.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What are the two negations contained in the word\n“immoralist”?</li>\n<li>Why does Nietzsche say that the condition of existence of the\n“good” is the lie?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Décadence</em>: decadence; a state of culture in which the weak and the sick are\npreferred to the vital.</li>\n<li><em>Ascending life</em>: that which expands, overcomes itself and affirms its\ninstincts (will to power).</li>\n<li><em>Niaiserie</em>: silliness, stupidity (in French); wanting to eliminate\npain and danger from life.</li>\n<li><em>Saying yes</em>: the affirmation of life; its condition is the\nnegation of the values that deny it.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"4-la-crítica-de-la-moral-cristiana-fragmento-4-8\">4. The critique\nof Christian morality (fragment 4, §8)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> The discovery of Christian morality is\n“a veritable catastrophe”, a destiny that divides history in two.\nEverything that has been called “truth” is the most harmful form of lie; the\npretext of “improving” humanity is a ruse for “sucking the blood”\nof life: morality as <strong>vampirism</strong>. Nietzsche criticises\nthe fundamental concepts of metaphysics and religion:\n“<strong>God</strong>”, invented as the antithetical concept to life;\nthe “<strong>beyond</strong>” and the “true world”, invented to\ndevalue the only world that exists; the “<strong>soul</strong>” and the\n“immortal soul”, invented to despise the body; the\n“<strong>sin</strong>” and “free will”, invented to\nconfuse the instincts. In place of health, the “salvation of the soul”.\nAnd in the concept of the “good man”, the defence of everything weak and\nsick. He ends with the cry taken from Voltaire: “Crush the\ninfamous thing!”.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Religion and metaphysics are not\ninnocent errors: they are a “deadly hostility to life”,\nwhich despises the body and the real world in the name of a fictitious beyond.\nThe “death of God” is the condition for life to become\npossible again.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Which four concepts does Nietzsche criticise and for what purpose does he say they were\n“invented”?</li>\n<li>What does it mean that morality is a “vampirism”?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Vampirism</em>: a metaphor for morality, which sucks the energy of\nlife to feed fictitious ideals.</li>\n<li><em>Antithetical</em>: the opposite; God is antithetical to life.</li>\n<li><em>True world</em>: the metaphysical beyond, invented to\ndevalue the real world.</li>\n<li><em>Folie circulaire</em>: “circular madness”; the cycle of guilt\n(sin) and relief (redemption).</li>\n<li><em>Free will</em>: for Nietzsche, an “instrument of torture”\nfor making man guilty for his instincts.</li>\n<li><em>Ecrasez l'infâme!</em>: “Crush the infamous thing!”, a cry against\nthe Church (taken from Voltaire).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-2-los-cuatro-fragmentos-de-la-selección-pau\">Part 2. The\nfour fragments of the PAU selection</h2>\n<p>Traducción propia (2026), del alemán. Licencia de la web: <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.es\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"fragmento-1--1-yo-no-soy-un-hombre-soy-dinamita\">Fragment 1 ·\n§1: “I am not a man, I am dynamite”</h3>\n<p>No soy un hombre, soy dinamita. — Pese a todo ello, no tengo nada de fundador de religiones: las religiones son cosa de la plebe; después de tocar a personas religiosas necesito lavarme las manos… No quiero «creyentes»; pienso que tengo demasiada malicia incluso para creer en mí mismo; jamás hablo a las masas… Me da un miedo atroz que un día me declaren santo: ya se adivinará por qué saco este libro antes; ha de impedir que se hagan tropelías conmigo… No quiero ser un santo; antes prefiero ser un bufón… Quizá soy un bufón… Y aun así —mejor dicho, no «aun así», porque hasta hoy nada ha habido más embustero que los santos— es la verdad la que habla por mi boca. — Pero mi verdad es terrible: porque hasta hoy a la mentira se la llamaba verdad. — Transvaloración de todos los valores (<em>Umwertung aller Werte</em>): esa es mi fórmula para un acto en el que la humanidad toma conciencia de sí misma en el grado más alto, y que en mi persona se hizo carne y genio. Es mi suerte la que dispone que yo haya de ser el primer hombre decente, que me sepa enfrentado a la mendacidad de milenios… Yo he sido el primero en descubrir la verdad, porque fui el primero en percibir la mentira como mentira: en olerla… Mi genio está en mi olfato… Contradigo como nadie ha contradicho nunca y, sin embargo, soy lo opuesto de un espíritu que dice no. Soy un mensajero de buenas noticias como no ha existido otro; conozco tareas de tal altura que hasta hoy no había ni siquiera un concepto para ellas; solo a partir de mí vuelve a haber esperanzas.</p>\n<h3 id=\"fragmento-2--2-3-el-primer-inmoralista-y-zaratustra\">Fragment 2\n· §§2-3: the first immoralist and Zarathustra</h3>\n<p>Soy, con muchísima diferencia, el hombre más temible que haya habido hasta hoy; esto no quita que vaya a ser el más bienhechor. Conozco el gusto por destruir en un grado que se corresponde con mi fuerza para destruir; en ambas cosas obedezco a mi naturaleza dionisíaca, que no sabe separar el hacer que no del decir sí. Soy el primer inmoralista (<em>Immoralist</em>): con ello soy el destructor por excelencia. —</p>\n<h4>3</h4>\n<p>No me han preguntado, aunque deberían haberlo hecho, qué quiere decir justamente en mi boca —la del primer inmoralista— el nombre de Zaratustra: porque lo que hace de aquel persa algo tan inmensamente singular en la historia es exactamente lo contrario. Zaratustra fue el primero que vio en la lucha del bien y del mal la verdadera rueda que mueve el engranaje de las cosas; trasladar la moral al plano metafísico —como fuerza, causa y fin en sí— fue obra suya. Pero en el fondo la pregunta contendría ya la respuesta. Fue Zaratustra quien creó este error, el más funesto de todos, la moral: por consiguiente, tiene que ser también el primero en reconocerlo. Y no solo porque en este asunto tiene una experiencia más larga y más amplia que cualquier otro pensador —la historia entera no es sino la refutación experimental de la tesis del llamado «orden moral del mundo»—; lo más importante es que Zaratustra es más veraz que cualquier otro pensador. Su doctrina, y únicamente ella, tiene la veracidad (<em>Wahrhaftigkeit</em>) como virtud suprema; es decir, lo opuesto a la cobardía del «idealista», que ante la realidad se da a la fuga. Zaratustra tiene más valor en el cuerpo que todos los pensadores juntos.</p>\n<h3\nid=\"fragmento-3--4-las-dos-negaciones-y-la-psicología-del-hombre-bueno\">Fragment\n3 · §4: the two negations and the psychology of the “good man”</h3>\n<p>En el fondo, mi palabra «inmoralista» encierra dos negaciones. Niego, de un lado, un tipo de hombre que hasta hoy pasaba por el más alto: los buenos, los benévolos, los bienhechores; niego, por otro, cierta clase de moral que ha logrado vigencia y dominio como moral en sí, es decir, la moral de la <em>décadence</em> o, dicho de forma más tangible, la moral cristiana. Cabría considerar más decisiva la segunda contradicción, puesto que el sobreestimar la bondad y la benevolencia lo cuento ya, en líneas generales, entre las consecuencias de la décadence, como síntoma de debilidad, como algo inconciliable con una vida que asciende y dice sí: en el decir sí, negar y destruir son condición. — Por ahora me quedo con la psicología del hombre bueno. Si se quiere tasar cuánto vale un tipo de hombre, hay que calcular el precio que cuesta mantenerlo, hay que saber de qué condiciones depende su existencia. Lo que hace posible que existan los buenos es la mentira; con otras palabras: negarse a ver, a toda costa, cómo está hecha, en el fondo, la realidad: que no es, en efecto, de tal naturaleza que despierte a cada momento instintos de benevolencia, y mucho menos de tal naturaleza que tolere a cada momento la intromisión de unas manos bonachonas y miopes. Considerar las situaciones de penuria de todo tipo como una objeción, como algo que hay que abolir, es la <em>niaiserie</em> (la necedad) por excelencia; en conjunto, una auténtica calamidad por sus efectos, una fatalidad de estupidez, casi tan necio como lo sería querer abolir el mal tiempo, por compasión, pongamos, con los pobres…</p>\n<h3 id=\"fragmento-4--8-la-crítica-de-la-moral-cristiana\">Fragment 4 ·\n§8: the critique of Christian morality</h3>\n<p>Descubrir la moral cristiana es un acontecimiento sin igual, una auténtica catástrofe. Quien saca a la luz lo que ella es constituye una <em>force majeure</em> (una fuerza mayor), un destino: rompe la historia de la humanidad en dos pedazos. Hay un antes de él y un después de él… Lo que golpeó el rayo de la verdad fue justo lo que hasta hoy estaba más alto: quien comprenda lo que allí quedó destruido, que mire si aún le queda algo entre las manos. Todo lo que hasta hoy se llamaba «verdad» queda reconocido como la forma más dañina, más traicionera, más soterrada de la mentira; el pretexto sagrado de «mejorar» a la humanidad, como la artimaña para chuparle la sangre a la vida, para desangrarla. La moral como vampirismo… El que descubre la moral descubre a la vez que carecen de valor todos los valores que la gente cree o ha creído; ya no ve nada digno de veneración en los tipos humanos más venerados, ni siquiera en los que han sido declarados santos; ve en ellos la clase más funesta de engendros, funestos porque fascinaban… ¡El concepto «Dios», inventado como concepto opuesto a la vida; en él, todo lo dañino, lo que envenena, lo que calumnia, toda la enemistad a muerte contra la vida, reunidos en una unidad espantosa! ¡El concepto de «más allá», de «mundo verdadero», inventado para quitar valor al único mundo que hay, para no dejarle a nuestra realidad de la tierra ni una meta, ni una razón, ni una tarea! ¡El concepto de «alma», de «espíritu» y, al final, hasta de «alma inmortal», inventado con el fin de despreciar el cuerpo, de hacerlo enfermar —de hacerlo «santo»—, de tratar con una ligereza estremecedora todas las cosas que en la vida merecen seriedad: las cuestiones de la alimentación, la vivienda, la dieta del espíritu, el cuidado de los enfermos, la limpieza, el tiempo atmosférico! ¡La «salvación del alma» en vez de la salud, o sea, una <em>folie circulaire</em> (locura circular) entre los espasmos de la penitencia y la histeria de la redención! ¡El concepto «pecado», inventado junto con el instrumento de tortura que le corresponde, el concepto «libre albedrío», para confundir los instintos, para convertir en segunda naturaleza la desconfianza hacia los instintos!</p>\n<h2 id=\"parte-4-conceptos-clave-para-el-examen-diccionario\">Part 4.\nKey concepts for the exam (dictionary)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition for the exam</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Revaluation (Umwertung)</td>\n<td>The critique and replacement of values contrary to life with others\nthat affirm it, based on the will to power.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Immoralist</td>\n<td>Someone who places himself outside traditional morality (good/evil), not someone who\nlacks values; the destroyer of that morality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Décadence</td>\n<td>Decadence: a state of culture in which the weak, the\nsick and the fictitious are preferred to the vital.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Will to power</td>\n<td>A vital force that tends to expand and overcome itself; the principle of\nascending life.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slave morality / master morality</td>\n<td>The first is born of the resentment of the weak and inverts\nvalues; the second affirms life and strength.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Passive / active nihilism</td>\n<td>The passive is despair at the loss of values; the active,\nthe destruction of false values in order to create new ones.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Death of God</td>\n<td>The disappearance of the absolute foundation (God) that sustained\nvalues; it leaves man free to create his own.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>True world / apparent world</td>\n<td>A Platonic-Christian distinction that Nietzsche inverts: the “true\nworld” is a fable that devalues the only real world.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overman (Übermensch)</td>\n<td>The man who overcomes traditional morality and creates his own values\nby affirming life.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eternal recurrence</td>\n<td>The idea that life repeats itself eternally; the test of the total affirmation\nof life.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dionysian / Apollonian</td>\n<td>The Dionysian affirms life with its pain and its intoxication; the\nApollonian seeks order and form.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resentment</td>\n<td>The origin of slave morality: the hatred of the weak that inverts the\nvalues of the strong.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"
 },
 "hf-paquete-beauvoir": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 26",
  "title": "Beauvoir: The Second Sex, ‘Conclusion’",
  "html": "<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-0-el-texto-original-completo\">Esquema de la «Conclusión» de <em>El segundo sexo</em></h2>\n<p class=\"nota\">Simone de Beauvoir, <em>Le Deuxième Sexe</em>, t. II (1949), «Conclusion». Esquema de elaboración propia a partir del original francés: es un resumen por niveles, no una traducción. § = número de párrafo de la Conclusión (del epígrafe, § 1, a la frase final, § 21); entre corchetes, la página en la edición que usa la selección oficial de la PAU (Cátedra). Los recuadros F1-F4 señalan los fragmentos que tienes traducidos para comentar.</p>\n<h3>Tesis central</h3>\n<p>La guerra entre los sexos no es un destino biológico. Es el resultado de una situación histórica de opresión, que pervierte por igual a mujeres y hombres. Solo la liberación real de la mujer puede acabar con ella: económica, social, educativa y sexual. Esa liberación no borra la diferencia ni el deseo entre los sexos: los convierte en relaciones entre libertades que se reconocen como iguales, y abre la puerta a la fraternidad.</p>\n<h3>0. Planteamiento del problema (§ 1-2) [p. 529]</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Punto de partida: una cita de Laforgue y la opinión de muchos hombres, según la cual entre los sexos siempre habrá disputa y la fraternidad es imposible.</li>\n<li>Hecho: hoy ni unos ni otras están contentos con su relación.</li>\n<li>Pregunta: ¿es una condena originaria o una fase pasajera de la historia?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>I. Diagnóstico: de dónde viene el conflicto (§ 3-11) [pp. 529-534]</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I.1. No nace de la biología ni del sexo (§ 3).</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) La biología no impone a los sexos ninguna hostilidad; el caso de la mantis se explica por la especie.</li>\n<li>b) La humanidad no es solo especie: es historia, y se define por cómo asume lo natural.</li>\n<li>c) Psicoanálisis: la «envidia» femenina no es deseo de mutilar al varón, sino un deseo ambiguo de su trascendencia.</li>\n<li>d) Conclusión parcial: la sexualidad expresa una situación global y no explica por sí sola la conducta. La «lucha de sexos» no es un choque eterno entre esencias. <strong>[F1]</strong></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>I.2. Las dos formas históricas del combate (§ 4-6).</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) <em>Forma antigua (§ 4):</em> la mujer encerrada en la inmanencia intenta encerrar también al hombre. Se defiende de una inferioridad impuesta, no natural ni culpable. Principio: toda opresión engendra guerra, y quien es tratado como inesencial busca recuperar su soberanía. <strong>[F1]</strong></li>\n<li>b) <em>Forma actual (§ 5):</em> la mujer quiere salir hacia la trascendencia y el hombre se resiste a tratarla como igual. Ya no se enfrentan individuos, sino dos «castas»: dos libertades que, en vez de reconocerse, quieren dominarse.</li>\n<li>c) <em>Dos planos (§ 6):</em> en el sexual y en el espiritual, la mujer «femenina» intenta reducir al varón a la pasividad, y la «emancipada» o «moderna» reclama los valores activos y la igualdad.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>I.3. Juego sucio de ambos lados y círculo vicioso (§ 7-8).</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) La reivindicación concreta es legítima. Pero muchas mujeres juegan a dos barajas, con privilegios antiguos y derechos nuevos, y el hombre les niega las oportunidades que dice exigirles.</li>\n<li>b) Raíz: la mujer se vive a la vez como sujeto y como «otra». No calcula: su situación ambigua se lo dicta.</li>\n<li>c) La disputa durará mientras se mantenga «la feminidad» como tal. Repartir culpas no sirve: cada sexo es víctima del otro y de sí mismo, y cómplice de su adversario. Lo que cada uno odia en el otro es su propia mala fe.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>I.4. Por qué los hombres sostienen la sumisión (§ 9-10).</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Origen de la opresión: el existente quiere escapar de sí y se enajena en otro, al que somete. Cada varón busca en «su» mujer el mito de su propia virilidad.</li>\n<li>b) Precio para él: queda preso de esa imagen, con miedo y desgaste. Se liberaría liberándola, pero lo teme.</li>\n<li>c) Las mentiras que disfrazan la opresión: «protección» en lugar de tutela, el hogar como felicidad, la «feminidad» como tesoro y el supuesto privilegio de la irresponsabilidad. Beauvoir lo ironiza comparándolo con otros grupos oprimidos.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>I.5. Complicidad de la mujer y papel de la educación (§ 11) [p. 534].</strong> <strong>[F3]</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) La mujer colabora más de lo habitual en un oprimido, y los hombres lo usan, de mala fe, para decir que ella eligió su destino.</li>\n<li>b) Pero su educación entera le cierra la rebeldía y le vende la renuncia como vocación. Ejemplo: no se puede culpar de ignorante a quien se educó en la pereza.</li>\n<li>c) Ella cede a la tentación, pero la tentación viene de él. Los reproches mutuos no absuelven a ninguno.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>II. La relación concreta en la desigualdad (§ 12) [pp. 534-537]</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>II.1. El equívoco de la «igualdad en la desigualdad».</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Ella reclama la igualdad abstracta que le prometen; él se atiene a la desigualdad real.</li>\n<li>b) Disputa sobre «dar» y «tomar»: el intercambio no es entre iguales. Ella cree valer muchísimo; para él es algo accesorio.</li>\n<li>c) El tiempo compartido no vale lo mismo para los dos: para él es riqueza, para ella una carga. El equilibrio es inestable: ella siente que se vende barata y él que paga caro.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>II.2. No hay justicia dentro de una injusticia.</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) El resentimiento de la mujer funciona como defensa; él se siente chantajeado y ella estafada.</li>\n<li>b) Analogía: ni el colono ni el general pueden portarse bien dentro de su sistema. El hombre es culpable sin haber cometido la falta; ella es víctima y verdugo a su pesar.</li>\n<li>c) Hay casos cotidianos sin salida buena, y quien tiene buena voluntad, sea él o ella, queda desgarrado.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>II.3. El problema es la situación, no las personas.</strong> La dependencia económica convierte a la mujer en una «parásita». Si se le da autonomía para ganarse la vida, acaba su dependencia y también la del hombre.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>III. El mundo de la igualdad y sus condiciones (§ 13-15) [pp. 537-540]</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>III.1. Un modelo imaginable (§ 13).</strong> La igualdad que prometió la revolución soviética:\n<ul>\n<li>misma educación, trabajo y salario;</li>\n<li>libertad erótica sin comercio sexual;</li>\n<li>matrimonio libre y disoluble;</li>\n<li>maternidad libre;</li>\n<li>iguales derechos para todos los hijos, con la sociedad corriendo con las cargas de la crianza.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>III.2. ¿Basta con cambiar las leyes? (§ 14) [p. 538]</strong> <strong>[F2]</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Objeción de los escépticos: las mujeres «serán siempre mujeres».</li>\n<li>b) Respuesta: en lo humano no hay nada puramente «natural». La mujer es un producto de la civilización, hecho por la mirada ajena desde la infancia; por eso su destino no está fijado.</li>\n<li>c) Precisión: la economía es el factor primordial, pero no basta sin sus consecuencias morales, sociales y culturales, que hoy no se dan en ningún país. Por eso la mujer actual vive partida entre lo que fue y lo que aún no es.</li>\n<li>d) La transformación tiene que ser colectiva: un educador aislado no puede crear a la «mujer nueva», porque la niña educada como un chico se siente una excepción. Hay que plantar el bosque entero.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>III.3. Una educación igualitaria (§ 15) [pp. 538-540].</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Con las mismas exigencias y la misma consideración desde la infancia, los «complejos» del psicoanálisis cambiarían de sentido: ni inferioridad de ella ni superioridad de él.</li>\n<li>b) La niña se interesaría por lo que hace y no por lo que es; la pubertad y la iniciación sexual serían menos traumáticas.</li>\n<li>c) Educación mixta y educación sexual coherente, frente a tabúes que solo producen represión.</li>\n<li>d) Lo decisivo: que la niña vea en el varón un compañero y no un semidiós, que no se le pongan obstáculos gratuitos y que tenga recursos para superar lo penoso de la condición humana. <strong>[F4]</strong></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>IV. La sexualidad liberada y el reconocimiento mutuo (§ 16) [pp. 540-541]</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>a) No hay fatalidad: las particularidades femeninas valen por el sentido que les da el sistema. <strong>[F4]</strong></li>\n<li>b) La «pasividad» erótica de la mujer viene de cómo se concibe, y la agresividad viril es un privilegio que el sistema le asigna. Ya hay parejas que viven el sexo como intercambio y no como victoria y derrota. <strong>[F4]</strong></li>\n<li>c) Hombre y mujer son a la vez carne y libertad, pasividad y actividad. Cada uno proyecta en el otro la parte de sí que rechaza.</li>\n<li>d) Si asumieran con lucidez esa ambigüedad, se reconocerían como semejantes. Ser humano importa más que cualquier diferencia, y la «virtud» está en lo que depende de nosotros.</li>\n<li>e) Ambos comparten el mismo drama de la existencia y se necesitan; de ahí podría nacer la fraternidad.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>V. Objeciones y respuestas (§ 17-19) [pp. 541-544]</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V.1. «Es una utopía: un círculo vicioso» (§ 17).</strong> La historia no gira en círculo: dar libertad y responsabilidad capacita para ellas. Los opresores no cederán gratis, pero la rebelión de los oprimidos y la evolución de los privilegiados crean situaciones nuevas, y la igualdad económica y social llegará casi con seguridad.</li>\n<li><strong>V.2. «Se perderá la chispa, el encanto femenino» (§ 18).</strong> Es la nostalgia de quienes defienden el presente. Ese encanto es valioso, pero raro y fugaz, y si se paga con desgracia hay que sacrificarlo.</li>\n<li><strong>V.3. «La mujer dejará de ser el doble y la mediadora del hombre» (§ 19).</strong> Se perderán ciertas experiencias y facilidades para él. Pero no desaparecerán el amor, la felicidad ni la poesía: nacerán relaciones que aún no podemos imaginar, y algunas ya existen.</li>\n<li><strong>V.4. «El mundo de la igualdad será uniforme y aburrido» (§ 19).</strong> Siempre quedarán diferencias, «diferencias en la igualdad». La monotonía la crean las instituciones, no la libertad, y las tensiones de la existencia (carne y espíritu, instante y tiempo, placer y olvido) seguirán viviéndose en la sexualidad.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>VI. Conclusión (§ 19-21) [p. 544]</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Liberar a la mujer no es negar su relación con el hombre, sino dejar de encerrarla en ella. Reconocerse como sujetos mantiene la alteridad y el deseo.</li>\n<li>b) Abolida la esclavitud de media humanidad, la pareja humana encontrará su verdadero sentido. Apoyo (§ 20): Marx, para quien la relación hombre-mujer mide el grado de humanidad alcanzado.</li>\n<li>c) Tarea (§ 21): que la libertad acabe imponiéndose en el mundo dado. Para ello, mujeres y hombres deben afirmar su fraternidad por encima de sus diferencias naturales.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2\nid=\"parte-1-guía-de-lectura-simplificada-el-segundo-sexo-conclusión\">Part\n1. Simplified reading guide: <em>The Second Sex</em>,\n“Conclusion”</h2>\n<h3 id=\"hoja-para-el-alumnado\">Student sheet</h3>\n<p><strong>Before reading.</strong> <em>The Second Sex</em> (1949) is the\nwork in which Simone de Beauvoir (Paris, 1908-1986) analyses the\nsituation of woman. The “Conclusion” sums up her central thesis, which she had\nalready formulated at the beginning of the book: “One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman”. Beauvoir is an existentialist: for her, the human being has\nno fixed essence, but makes himself through his acts.\nShe applies that idea to woman: her condition is not a biological destiny,\nbut the result of a history and an upbringing.</p>\n<p><strong>How to use this guide.</strong> First read the fragment in the\ntext; then read “What it says” to check that you have understood;\nanswer the two questions in your notebook (one or two sentences each); and\ncopy down the vocabulary you did not know.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"1-la-lucha-de-sexos-no-es-natural-fragmento-1\">1. The struggle\nbetween the sexes is not natural (fragment 1)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> The conflict between men and women is not\nplayed out on a sexual plane: sexuality does not define a destiny, but\nexpresses a total “situation”. The struggle between the sexes is not deduced from\nanatomy. Those who explain it by biology take for granted that in “the timeless sky\nof Ideas” two eternal essences are fighting, the Feminine\nand the Masculine; but on earth that combat takes different historical\nforms. Woman, confined to <strong>immanence</strong>,\ntries to hold man too in that prison: the mother, the wife,\nthe mistress are “jailers”. But she does nothing but defend herself: neither an\nimmutable essence nor a culpable choice condemns her to\ninferiority; both have been imposed on her. “All oppression creates a state\nof war”: the being considered <strong>inessential</strong> wants to\nre-establish his sovereignty.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> The conflict between the sexes is neither\nbiological nor eternal, but historical and social: it is born of oppression. If\nthe situation changes, the conflict changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What does Beauvoir reject when she denies that the struggle between the sexes is deduced\nfrom anatomy?</li>\n<li>Why does she say that woman, in opposing man, “does nothing but\ndefend herself”?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Immanence</em>: a state of passivity and confinement, without projects\nof one’s own; what is imposed on woman.</li>\n<li><em>Transcendence</em>: the capacity to project oneself towards the future\nthrough freedom and action.</li>\n<li><em>Essence</em>: a fixed and immutable nature; Beauvoir denies it\nof the human being.</li>\n<li><em>Inessential</em>: one who lacks autonomy and lives for the\nprojects of another (woman as “the Other”).</li>\n<li><em>Sovereignty</em>: being master of one’s own existence and of one’s\nown decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"2-en-la-sociedad-humana-nada-es-natural-fragmento-2\">2. “In human\nsociety nothing is natural” (fragment 2)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> Is it enough to change laws and\ncustoms for men and women to be alike? The sceptics\nsay that “women will always be women”. But that is to admit that\ntoday’s woman is a creation of nature. It must be repeated that\n“<strong>in human society nothing is natural</strong>”: woman is a\nproduct of civilisation. She is not defined by hormones or\nmysterious instincts, but by the way she perceives her body through the\nconsciousness of others; the abyss that separates the adolescent girl from the\nadolescent boy has been widened since childhood. Her destiny is not\nfixed for eternity. But changing the economic condition is not enough:\nthat factor is the primordial one, but as long as it does not bring the moral, social and cultural\nconsequences, the “new woman” cannot emerge. Today\nwoman is divided between the past and the future.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> The feminine condition is a\nsocial construction, not a biological destiny. To change it, the\neconomy is not enough: culture and mentality must also be transformed.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What does it mean that “in human society nothing is natural”?</li>\n<li>Why does Beauvoir say that changing woman’s economic\ncondition is not enough?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Civilisation / culture</em>: the set of social interventions\nthat create woman’s identity.</li>\n<li><em>Economic condition</em>: the primordial factor of feminine\nevolution, but insufficient by itself.</li>\n<li><em>New woman</em>: one who has overcome the division between past\n(immanence) and future (freedom).</li>\n<li><em>Destiny</em>: an idea rejected by Beauvoir: life is not\nwritten in advance, it is constructed.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"3-la-conspiración-de-la-educación-y-la-mala-fe-fragmento-3\">3.\nThe conspiracy of education and bad faith (fragment 3)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> Men find in woman more\ncomplicity than the oppressor usually finds in the oppressed, and they use\nthis in bad faith to say that she wanted her destiny. But woman’s whole\neducation conspires to close the paths of rebellion to her:\nthe whole of society, beginning with parents, exalts the value of love,\nof self-denial, of the gift of oneself, and conceals from her that no one will be prepared\nto bear that burden. Woman accepts those lies because they invite her to\nease: it is the worst crime against her. She is educated without being taught to\nassume her existence; she lets herself be carried along, counting on the protection of others.\nShe is wrong to yield, but man cannot reproach her for it, because the\ntemptation comes from him. When the conflict breaks out, each blames the\nother, but “the faults of one do not absolve the other”.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Woman’s passivity is not a\nfree choice, but the result of an education that steers her towards\ndependence. “Bad faith” is accepting those lies in order not to face\nthe anguish of freedom.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What values does society exalt in woman and what does it conceal from her?</li>\n<li>Why does Beauvoir say that man cannot reproach woman\nfor her passivity?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Self-denial / gift of oneself</em>: values imposed on woman that\npush her to give up her projects.</li>\n<li><em>Bad faith</em>: fleeing from one’s own freedom by accepting imposed\nroles in order to avoid the anguish of deciding.</li>\n<li><em>Anguish</em>: the feeling that comes with awareness of one’s own\nfreedom and responsibility.</li>\n<li><em>Conspiracy</em>: a social process that “mutilates” woman’s capacity for\nrebellion.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4 id=\"4-hacia-un-amor-en-libertad-e-igualdad-fragmento-4\">4. Towards\nlove in freedom and equality (fragment 4)</h4>\n<p><strong>What it says.</strong> What is most useful for the girl is that she not seek\nin man a demigod, but a companion, a friend, a partner: thus\nnothing would prevent her from assuming her existence. “Eroticism and love would have\nthe character of a free surpassing and not of surrender; she could live them\nas a relationship between equals”. It is not a matter of suppressing at a stroke\nall the difficulties: the most intelligent education cannot prevent\nthe young woman from having her own experience; what can be asked is that obstacles not be\npiled up without reason. Woman is not the victim of any\nfatality: her singularities get their importance from the meaning\nthat is given to them, and they can be overcome if they are grasped from a new\nperspective. Her ovaries do not condemn her to live on her knees: masculine\naggressiveness is a privilege of a system that asserts male\nsovereignty, and woman only feels passive because she conceives herself\nfrom the outset as such.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea.</strong> Beauvoir’s proposal is\n<strong>fraternity</strong>: a relationship between equals, in which\nlove and eroticism are a free surpassing and not a surrender.\nPassivity is not biological, but learned.</p>\n<p><strong>Comprehension questions.</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What model of relationship does Beauvoir propose in contrast to that of the\n“demigod”?</li>\n<li>Why does she say that woman “only feels passive because she conceives herself\nfrom the outset as such”?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Fraternity</em>: a bond of equality and mutual recognition\nbetween human beings.</li>\n<li><em>Free surpassing</em>: an act of transcendence in which the\nindividual projects himself without losing his autonomy.</li>\n<li><em>Companion / equal</em>: a model of relationship that replaces the\nhierarchy of “demigod” and “devotee”.</li>\n<li><em>Male sovereignty</em>: a system of privileges that places the\nmale as absolute subject.</li>\n<li><em>Passivity</em>: behaviour imposed by education, not a natural\ntrait.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"parte-2-los-cuatro-fragmentos-de-la-selección-pau\">Part 2. The\nfour fragments of the PAU selection</h2>\n<p class=\"nota\">Cuatro fragmentos de la «Conclusión» (<em>Le Deuxième Sexe</em>, t. II, París, Gallimard, 1949), en <strong>traducción propia con fines docentes</strong>, del original francés. Se reproducen como cita para su análisis y comentario en clase. Son los fragmentos que se trabajan para la PAU de la EHU: la coordinación de Historia de la Filosofía fijó la «Conclusión» como texto del examen (mensaje a los centros del 28-10-2025), y los exámenes de 2026 salieron de estos pasajes. El original se cita por párrafo (§), porque la edición electrónica de Gallimard no tiene páginas fijas; se indica también la página de la edición que usa la selección oficial (Cátedra) para localizarlos.</p>\n<h3 id=\"beauvoir-f1\">F1 · El conflicto no es biológico: sus dos formas históricas</h3>\n<p class=\"nota\">Texto de la PAU 2026, convocatoria extraordinaria (EHU).</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Esto significa que el drama no transcurre en el terreno de lo sexual; además, nunca hemos visto en la sexualidad algo que fije un destino ni que guarde, por sí sola, la llave del comportamiento humano, sino la expresión de una situación (<em>situation</em>) entera, que ella misma contribuye a definir. La anatomía del varón y la de la mujer no llevan consigo, sin más, la lucha entre los sexos. Lo cierto es que, al mencionarla, se supone sin discusión que en un cielo de Ideas ajeno al tiempo combaten entre sí unas esencias imprecisas: lo Eterno femenino, lo Eterno masculino; y pasa inadvertido que esta pelea de titanes toma aquí abajo dos formas muy diferentes, propias de dos épocas distintas de la historia.</p><p>Confinada en la inmanencia (<em>immanence</em>), la mujer trata de encerrar también al hombre en esa cárcel; así la cárcel se confundirá con el mundo, y ella ya no sufrirá por verse recluida allí: la madre, la esposa y la amante hacen de carceleras; la sociedad, cuyas reglas han dictado los varones, establece por decreto la inferioridad femenina, y la mujer únicamente puede suprimir esa inferioridad si acaba con la superioridad viril. Se aplica a mutilar al hombre, a someterlo; le contradice, le niega su verdad y sus valores. Pero así no hace sino defenderse: lo que la ha condenado a la inmanencia, a la inferioridad, no ha sido una esencia inmutable, como tampoco una elección culpable. Ambas le vienen impuestas. Cualquier opresión engendra un estado de guerra. Tampoco aquí hay excepción. El existente al que se tiene por inesencial (<em>inessentiel</em>) no puede sino aspirar a restablecer su soberanía.</p><span class=\"per\">— Simone de Beauvoir, <em>Le Deuxième Sexe</em>, t. II, «Conclusion», § 3 (final) y § 4 (Gallimard, 1949). Traducción propia con fines docentes (2026). En la edición de la selección oficial PAU (Cátedra): p. 530.</span></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"beauvoir-f2\">F2 · ¿Basta con cambiar las leyes? Nada es natural</h3>\n<p class=\"nota\">Texto del modelo de examen PAU 2026 (coordinación de la EHU).</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Pero ¿es suficiente con transformar leyes, instituciones, costumbres, opinión pública y el contexto social entero para que mujeres y hombres lleguen a ser realmente semejantes? «Las mujeres serán siempre mujeres», dicen los escépticos; y otros, metidos a videntes, profetizan que, al despojarse de su feminidad, no conseguirán convertirse en hombres y se volverán monstruos. Hablar así supone aceptar que la mujer actual la ha creado la naturaleza; conviene insistir de nuevo en que en la colectividad humana nada es natural y que, entre otras cosas, la mujer es un producto que la civilización ha ido elaborando; la intervención de los demás en su destino es originaria: si esa acción se encauzara de otro modo, conduciría a un resultado completamente distinto. Lo que define a la mujer no son sus hormonas ni unos instintos misteriosos, sino la manera en que, a través de conciencias ajenas, vuelve a hacer suyos su propio cuerpo y el vínculo que mantiene con el mundo; la sima que se abre entre la muchacha y el muchacho adolescentes se ha cavado de forma deliberada desde los primeros años de su infancia; más adelante, no habrá manera de evitar que la mujer sea aquello que <em>han hecho de ella</em>, y siempre arrastrará tras de sí ese pasado; si se mide lo que pesa, se ve con toda claridad que su destino no ha quedado fijado para siempre. Desde luego, no hay que creer que, con solo modificar su condición económica, la mujer quedará transformada: ese factor fue y continúa siendo el factor primordial en su evolución; pero, en tanto no acarree las consecuencias que anuncia y exige —morales, sociales, culturales, etc.—, la mujer nueva no podrá aparecer; a día de hoy, esas consecuencias no se han hecho realidad en ningún lugar, ni en la U.R.S.S. ni en Francia ni en los EE. UU.; y por eso la mujer de hoy vive desgarrada entre el pasado y el futuro; casi siempre se presenta como una «verdadera mujer» con disfraz de hombre, y se siente incómoda tanto en su carne femenina como en su ropa de hombre.</p><span class=\"per\">— Simone de Beauvoir, <em>Le Deuxième Sexe</em>, t. II, «Conclusion», § 14 (hasta «…habit masculin») (Gallimard, 1949). Traducción propia con fines docentes (2026). En la edición de la selección oficial PAU (Cátedra): p. 538.</span></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"beauvoir-f3\">F3 · Complicidad, mala fe y educación</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Lo cierto es que los hombres hallan en su compañera una complicidad mayor que la que, por lo común, halla el opresor en el oprimido; y, apoyándose en ello, sostienen de mala fe (<em>mauvaise foi</em>) que ese destino que le han impuesto lo ha <em>querido</em> ella. Ya se ha visto que, de hecho, su educación entera se confabula para impedirle el paso hacia la rebeldía y la aventura; toda la sociedad —y los primeros, sus padres, tan respetados— la engaña: le exalta lo mucho que valen el amor, la abnegación, la entrega de uno mismo, y le calla que ni amante, ni marido, ni hijos van a estar dispuestos a soportar la pesada carga que eso trae consigo. Ella se presta gustosa a esas mentiras, porque la animan a deslizarse cuesta abajo hacia lo más cómodo: y ahí está el peor crimen que se comete con ella. Desde niña y durante toda su vida la miman y la corrompen al señalarle como vocación propia esa dimisión que tienta a todo existente al que su libertad le angustia. Si a un niño se le empuja a la pereza teniéndolo distraído de la mañana a la noche, sin dejarle ocasión de estudiar ni hacerle ver de qué le serviría, nadie dirá, al hacerse hombre, que escogió ser incapaz e ignorante; pues así se cría a la mujer: nunca se le enseña que le es necesario asumir por sí misma su existencia. Ella se abandona de buena gana a contar con que otros la protejan, la amen, la socorran y la guíen; se deja seducir por la esperanza de lograr su ser sin <em>hacer</em> nada. Hace mal cediendo a la tentación; pero no le corresponde al hombre echárselo en cara, porque el tentador fue él. Cuando entre ambos estalle un conflicto, cada cual culpará al otro de la situación (<em>situation</em>): ella le echará en cara que fue él quien la creó —«Nadie me enseñó a razonar ni a ganarme la vida…»—; y él, que ella la aceptó —«Tú no sabes nada, eres una inútil…»—. Cada sexo se cree justificado al pasar al ataque; pero las culpas de uno no hacen inocente al otro.</p><span class=\"per\">— Simone de Beauvoir, <em>Le Deuxième Sexe</em>, t. II, «Conclusion», § 11 (Gallimard, 1949). Traducción propia con fines docentes (2026). En la edición de la selección oficial PAU (Cátedra): p. 534.</span></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"beauvoir-f4\">F4 · Educar para la igualdad; la sexualidad sin fatalidad</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Lo que, ante todo, sería de gran provecho para la muchacha es no ver en el varón a un semidiós, sino solamente a un camarada, a un amigo, a un compañero: así nada la desviaría de hacerse cargo ella misma de su existencia; el erotismo y el amor serían una libre superación, no una dimisión, y ella podría vivirlos en pie de igualdad. Claro está que no se pretende borrar de un plumazo cuantas dificultades ha de vencer el niño hasta hacerse adulto; ni la educación más inteligente y tolerante lo eximiría de vivir por su cuenta y riesgo su propia experiencia; lo que cabe pedir es que no se le pongan obstáculos gratuitos por el camino. Que ya no se cauterice con hierro candente a las niñas «viciosas» supone ya un avance; algo han aprendido los padres del psicoanálisis; aun así, las condiciones en que actualmente se forma la mujer y se inicia en la sexualidad son tan deplorables que ninguna objeción contra un cambio radical puede tenerse por válida. Lo que se busca no es abolir en ella las contingencias y miserias de la condición humana, sino darle con qué superarlas.</p><p>Ninguna misteriosa fatalidad hace de la mujer una víctima; los rasgos singulares que la distinguen deben su peso al sentido que cobran, y podrán superarse en cuanto se los mire desde perspectivas nuevas; así, ya vimos cómo, en su experiencia erótica, la mujer vive —y a menudo aborrece— el dominio masculino: pero no por eso hay que concluir que sus ovarios la obliguen a vivir de rodillas para siempre. La agresividad viril solo aparece como un privilegio señorial dentro de un sistema que conspira por entero a afirmar la soberanía del varón; y si la mujer se <em>siente</em> tan hondamente pasiva en el acto amoroso, es porque ya de antemano se <em>piensa</em> como tal.</p><span class=\"per\">— Simone de Beauvoir, <em>Le Deuxième Sexe</em>, t. II, «Conclusion», § 15 (final) y § 16 (hasta «…comme telle») (Gallimard, 1949). Traducción propia con fines docentes (2026). En la edición de la selección oficial PAU (Cátedra): p. 540.</span></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"parte-4-conceptos-clave-para-el-examen-diccionario\">Part 4.\nKey concepts for the exam (dictionary)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition for the exam</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Existence / essence</td>\n<td>Existence precedes essence: the human being has no fixed\nnature, he makes himself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Immanence</td>\n<td>A state of passivity and confinement, without projects of one’s own; what\nsociety imposes on woman.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transcendence</td>\n<td>The capacity to project oneself towards the future through freedom and\naction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Situation</td>\n<td>The set of conditioning factors (biological, historical, economic,\nsocial) in which an individual lives; it is not a destiny.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject / the Other</td>\n<td>The subject is the one who has initiative and freedom (the essential); “the\nOther” is the inessential, defined in relation to the male.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Otherness</td>\n<td>The condition of being always defined in relation to the male, as the\nsecondary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bad faith</td>\n<td>Fleeing from one’s own freedom by accepting imposed roles in order to avoid the\nanguish of deciding.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anguish</td>\n<td>The feeling that comes with awareness of one’s own freedom and of the\nresponsibility of choosing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fraternity</td>\n<td>A bond of equality and mutual recognition that makes it possible to overcome the\nstruggle between the sexes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New woman</td>\n<td>One who has overcome the division between past (immanence) and future\n(freedom), without disguising herself as a man.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sovereignty</td>\n<td>Being master of one’s own existence and of one’s own decisions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman”</td>\n<td>Beauvoir’s central formula: femininity is a social construction,\nnot a biological destiny.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"
 },
 "hf-dualismo-monismo": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 14-15 (rationalism)",
  "title": "Descartes and monism: one substance or two?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 14-15 (rationalism). The mind-body problem: are we two substances (dualism) or only one (monism)?</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the debate ‘Descartes against monism’ in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>. Reading and commentary; the fragments can be rotated for a comprehension exercise.</p>\n<h2>1. Descartes: I am a thinking thing (Meditation II)</h2>\n<blockquote>‘What am I, then? A thing that thinks. And what is a thing that thinks? It is a thing that doubts, understands, affirms, denies, wills, does not will, and also imagines and senses.’</blockquote>\n<p>After doubting everything, the one thing he cannot deny is that, while he thinks, he exists. The mind is known before and better than the body: in the example of the wax, what remains when the wax melts is not grasped by the senses, but by the understanding.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Descartes: the real distinction between mind and body (Meditation VI)</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Since I can conceive clearly and distinctly one thing without the other, that is enough to be sure that they are distinct. I have, on the one hand, a clear idea of myself as a thinking, non-extended thing; on the other, a distinct idea of the body as an extended thing that does not think. It is certain, then, that I am really distinct from my body and that I can exist without it.’</blockquote>\n<p>This is the dualism of substances: mind (<em>res cogitans</em>) and body (<em>res extensa</em>). But Descartes qualifies that he is not in his body ‘like a pilot in his ship’: he forms with it a very close union.</p>\n\n<h2>3. The objection of Elisabeth of Bohemia (1643)</h2>\n<blockquote>‘I beg you to tell me how the soul of man, being only a thinking substance, can determine the spirits of the body to produce voluntary actions.’</blockquote>\n<p>If the mind is not extended, how does it move a body that is? Pushing and moving imply contact, and contact implies extension. It is the harshest objection to dualism. Descartes replies by appealing to a ‘primitive notion’ of the union of soul and body (and, in other texts, to the pineal gland as a meeting point), but he admits that this union is better understood by living it than by reasoning about it.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Spinoza’s monism: a single substance</h2>\n<blockquote>‘By substance I understand that which is in itself and is conceived through itself. Only a single substance can be given or conceived: God, or Nature (<em>Deus sive Natura</em>).’</blockquote>\n<p>For Spinoza, thought and extension are not two substances, but two attributes of one and the same. Mind and body are ‘one and the same thing’, conceived now under the attribute of thought, now under that of extension: they do not interact, they correspond (parallelism). Thus Elisabeth’s problem dissolves… but at the price of denying that the soul is a separate thing.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Materialist monism: La Mettrie</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Man is a machine so complex that it is impossible at first to form a clear idea of it. The soul is no more than a principle of movement, a material part of the brain.’</blockquote>\n<p>There is no need for a separate thinking substance: the mental is a function of the body. Materialist monism: there is only body.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>You play as Descartes, defending that mind and body are really distinct. The monist opponent pushes with Elisabeth’s objection (if the soul is not extended, it cannot move the body) and with the razor of Spinoza and La Mettrie (one substance is superfluous). Traps to watch for: the false dilemma ‘either an immortal soul or a machine without freedom’, the straw man (‘according to you the body does not matter at all’) and the personal attack. Your strong card: what I know with most certainty is that I think, and thinking is not a property of pure extension.</p>"
 },
 "hf-hegel-kant": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 21 (German idealism)",
  "title": "Hegel against Kant: the limits of reason or reason without limits?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 18 (Kant) and 21 (German idealism, Hegel). The scope of reason: does knowing have insurmountable limits, or can reason think everything?</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the debate ‘Hegel against Kant’ in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>.</p>\n<h2>1. Kant: phenomenon and thing in itself</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Thoughts without content are empty; intuitions without concepts are blind.’</blockquote>\n<p>We only know <em>phenomena</em>: things as they appear to us, ordered by space, time and the categories of the understanding. We never know the <em>thing in itself</em> (the noumenon), what the thing is apart from our way of perceiving and thinking it.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Kant: the antinomies</h2>\n<p>When reason goes beyond possible experience (God, the soul, the world as a totality), it can prove with equal force a thesis and its opposite: that the world has a beginning in time and that it does not; that there is freedom and that everything is determined. For Kant, that contradiction is the sign that we have overstepped the limits of knowledge: we must stop and keep silent about what does not fit within experience.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Hegel: you cannot examine knowing before you know</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Wanting to know before knowing is as absurd as the plan of that scholastic who wanted to learn to swim before getting into the water.’</blockquote>\n<p>Hegel reproaches Kant for setting up the critique of the faculty of knowledge as a wall in advance, as if we could review knowledge from outside knowledge. An instrument can only be tested by using it.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Hegel: the thing in itself is an empty abstraction</h2>\n<p>Kant’s ‘thing in itself’ —what would remain of an object if we took away everything we think about it— is not a deep mystery but the poorest of abstractions: a void. For Hegel, positing an unknowable beyond amounts to giving up thinking precisely where we ought to begin.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Hegel: contradiction and the whole</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The true is the whole.’</blockquote>\n<p>Where Kant sees in contradiction (the antinomies) the limit of reason, Hegel sees its engine: reality advances through contradictions that are overcome in a higher truth (the dialectic). Truth is not an isolated result but the whole process that leads up to it.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>The computer defends Kant: we must recognise the limits of reason and stay silent about the thing in itself. You play as Hegel: those limits are a prejudice, the thing in itself is a void, and contradiction does not halt thought, it drives it. Traps to watch for: the false dilemma ‘either Kant’s limits or metaphysical delirium’, the straw man (painting Kant as a sceptic who denies science) and appeal to authority (‘Kant said it, end of discussion’). Note: calling Kant ‘analytic’ is imprecise; what is under discussion is his critical method, which <em>separates</em> (phenomenon/noumenon, subject/object), as opposed to Hegel’s dialectic, which thinks their union.</p>"
 },
 "hf-gorgias": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 5 (the Sophists and Socrates)",
  "title": "Socrates against Callicles (Gorgias): the law of nature or justice?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (the Sophists and Socrates). The confrontation in the <em>Gorgias</em>: does the law of nature (the strong) rule, or is there a justice that holds for everyone?</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the ‘Socrates against Callicles’ debate in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>.</p>\n<h2>1. Callicles: the law of nature</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Nature itself shows that it is just for the one who is worth more to have more than the one who is worth less, and the stronger more than the weaker.’</blockquote>\n<p>Callicles distinguishes between law (<em>nómos</em>) and nature (<em>physis</em>). The laws that call equality just were made by the weak many to bind the strong out of fear; but by nature, he says, the superior should rule and have more.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Callicles: let your desires grow</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Whoever wants to live rightly must let his desires be as great as possible and not restrain them, and be able to satisfy them with courage.’</blockquote>\n<p>The good life, for Callicles, is that of the person who has great appetites and the power to sate them. Conventional moderation and justice are, in his view, a trap to tame the strong.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Callicles: mocking philosophy</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Philosophy is charming if one touches it in moderation in youth; but whoever persists in it beyond that age is ruined. If they drag you into a court, you will stand there gaping, and anyone will be able to slap you with impunity.’</blockquote>\n<p>Callicles does not merely argue: he ridicules Socrates as a man who has wasted his time on childish things and does not know how to defend himself in the real world.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Socrates: your own law contradicts you</h2>\n<p>Socrates takes Callicles’ distinction and turns it back on him: if ‘the stronger’ is the one who has most power, and the many together have more power than one, then the laws of the many <em>are</em> those of nature. Callicles has to specify what he means by ‘better’ and ‘stronger’, because his argument plays on the ambiguity of those words. (It is worth noting that here too Socrates takes advantage of that ambiguity: it is a good point to discuss in class whether his move is entirely fair.)</p>\n\n<h2>5. Socrates: the leaky jar</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The soul of the foolish is like a leaky jar: however much is poured in, it never fills up.’</blockquote>\n<p>A life of immoderate appetites is not happiness but an endless lack: filling and filling a broken vessel. Satisfying one desire after another is like scratching an itch without stopping.</p>\n\n<h2>6. Socrates: pleasure is not the good</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Good and evil are not had at the same time; pleasure and pain are: the thirsty man drinks with pleasure while still suffering thirst. So pleasure is not the same as the good.’</blockquote>\n<p>Hence his central thesis, the one that scandalises Callicles: <strong>it is worse to commit an injustice than to suffer one</strong>, because injustice harms what is most valuable, the soul of the one who commits it.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>You play as Socrates against Callicles. Callicles pushes with strong, legitimate cards (the law of nature, the example of the strong who impose themselves) that must be refuted, not dismissed as traps. His traps: the personal attack (philosophy is for children, they will slap you), fear (they will take you to court), the false dilemma (either you rule or you are ruled) and the straw man (that you preach letting yourself be trampled on). Your best cards: turning his own distinction back on him, the leaky jar and the point that pleasure is not the good. Temptation to avoid: answering his contempt with another ad hominem.</p>"
 },
 "hf-eutifron": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 5 (the Sophists and Socrates)",
  "title": "Socrates against Euthyphro: what is piety?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (the Sophists and Socrates). The <em>Euthyphro</em>: Socrates asks what ‘the pious’ is and dismantles, one after another, the definitions of a man who thinks himself an expert in religion.</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the ‘Socrates against Euthyphro’ debate in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>. A key piece for the Socratic method (asking for the definition, not examples) and for the <em>Euthyphro dilemma</em>, which is still alive in ethics (is the good good because God commands it, or does God command it because it is good?).</p>\n<h2>The situation</h2>\n<p>In front of the court, Socrates meets Euthyphro, a soothsayer who is about to prosecute his own father for the death of a labourer. Euthyphro is sure he is acting piously; Socrates, accused precisely of impiety, asks him to teach him what ‘the pious’ is.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Euthyphro: an example instead of a definition</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The pious is what I am doing now: prosecuting the one who commits a crime, whoever he may be, even if he is my father.’</blockquote>\n<p>Socrates objects that this is <em>an example</em> of a pious action, not piety itself. He asks for the common feature ‘by which all pious things are pious’, a model against which to measure any action. (Giving a case is not defining: it is the underlying trap of the whole dialogue.)</p>\n\n<h2>2. Euthyphro: ‘the gods do it too’</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Zeus himself bound his father Cronus for devouring his children; if the gods do such things, why should I not accuse my father?’</blockquote>\n<p>Appealing to ‘the gods do it’ does not prove that it is just: it is a <strong>false authority</strong> (that someone is powerful or revered does not make them an expert on the good). Moreover, the stories of gods who quarrel are about to turn against Euthyphro.</p>\n\n<h2>3. First definition: ‘the pious is what the gods love’</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The pious is what is loved by the gods, and the impious what is hateful to them.’</blockquote>\n<p>Socrates accepts it in order to examine it and finds the crack: if the gods quarrel among themselves —as the poets tell— the same action will be loved by some and hated by others, and then it would be <strong>pious and impious at once</strong>. The definition contradicts itself.</p>\n\n<h2>4. The patch: ‘what ALL the gods love’</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Well, the pious will be what all the gods love without exception; none who is truly a god would love a crime.’</blockquote>\n<p>Euthyphro patches up his definition to dodge the counterexample. It is the ‘no true scotsman’ move: narrowing the rule to suit oneself so that the awkward case does not count. Socrates lets it pass… and raises the decisive question.</p>\n\n<h2>5. The Euthyphro dilemma</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Is the pious loved by the gods BECAUSE it is pious, or is it pious BECAUSE the gods love it?’</blockquote>\n<p>If the gods love it <em>because</em> it is already pious, then being loved is not what makes it pious: piety has its own reason for being, prior to the gods’ love. ‘Loved by the gods’ only says what <em>happens to</em> the pious (an effect), not what it <em>is</em> (its essence). The definition does not define. <em>(This is the dilemma that, transposed to ‘is the good good because God commands it, or does God command it because it is good?’, is still debated today in ethics.)</em></p>\n\n<h2>6. The final circle: ‘trade’ with the gods</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Piety is a kind of science of giving to and asking from the gods: offering them worship in exchange for their favours.’</blockquote>\n<p>Socrates shows that ‘pleasing the gods’ is nothing but ‘being loved by them’: we are back at the starting point, already refuted. The argument goes round in a circle. Cornered, Euthyphro claims he is in a hurry and leaves, with the question unanswered.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>You play as Socrates. Euthyphro is not an aggressor like Callicles: he is a self-assured man who keeps producing definitions that collapse. Your job is not to insult him but to ask for the <em>definition</em> and show where each attempt fails. Note his nameable traps: false authority (‘the gods do it’), the ‘no true scotsman’ patch (‘none who is truly a god…’) and the ad populum (‘all Athens knows it, only you doubt it’). Your best cards: demanding the definition, the contradiction of the quarrelling gods, the <strong>dilemma</strong> and the circle of trade. Temptations to avoid: attacking him for prosecuting his father (ad hominem) or boxing him into a false dilemma (‘either you define it or you are impious’).</p>"
 },
 "hf-trasimaco": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 5 (the Sophists and Socrates)",
  "title": "Socrates against Thrasymachus: is justice the advantage of the stronger?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (the Sophists and Socrates). The clash in Book I of the <em>Republic</em>: is justice something good in itself, or just the name that power gives to what suits it?</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the ‘Socrates vs. Thrasymachus’ debate in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>.</p>\n<h2>1. Thrasymachus: justice is what is to the advantage of the strong</h2>\n<blockquote>‘I say that the just is nothing other than what is to the advantage of the stronger.’</blockquote>\n<p>Thrasymachus bursts in ‘like a wild beast’ and defines: in each city, whoever holds power rules and makes laws for his own benefit; calling obedience to those laws ‘just’ is therefore serving the interest of the ruler. Justice would be the disguise of the powerful man’s advantage.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Socrates: but rulers make mistakes</h2>\n<p>Socrates objects: rulers sometimes deceive themselves and make laws that harm them. Then, by your own definition, being just would be obeying something that is <em>not</em> to the advantage of the strong. The definition contradicts itself.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Thrasymachus shields himself: ‘the ruler, insofar as he rules, does not err’</h2>\n<blockquote>‘When a doctor makes a mistake, at that very moment he is not acting as a doctor. The same goes for the ruler: strictly speaking, whoever rules does not make mistakes.’</blockquote>\n<p>To save his thesis, Thrasymachus narrows the definition: error does not count because ‘the one who truly rules’ does not err. It is the ‘no true scotsman’ move: redefining to suit oneself so that the counterexample does not apply.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Socrates: every craft serves its object, not itself</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The doctor, as a doctor, does not seek his own good but that of the patient; and the shepherd looks after the sheep, not himself.’</blockquote>\n<p>Socrates takes that figure of the expert ‘in the strict sense’ seriously and turns it around: every craft, practised as such, seeks the good of <em>that which it is concerned with</em>. So whoever rules well, as a ruler, seeks the good of the ruled, not his own. The advantage of the strong is not the purpose of ruling.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Thrasymachus: but injustice is more profitable and happier</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Look at who triumphs: tyrants and cheats. The wrongdoer on a grand scale is not a criminal, he is a happy man; injustice is the true strength.’</blockquote>\n<p>Thrasymachus changes tack: the unjust life, he says, is more profitable than the just one; the just man always gets less. Here he mixes reasons with traps: generalising from a few successful tyrants and posing a false dilemma (‘either an exploited sucker or a clever one who exploits’).</p>\n\n<h2>6. Socrates: without some justice nothing gets done</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Even a band of thieves needs not to rob one another in order to get its loot; injustice sows discord and makes them unable to act together.’</blockquote>\n<p>Total injustice does not give strength but paralysis: it sets men against one another and each against himself. And with the argument from <em>function</em> (ergon): if the soul’s own task is to live, and its virtue is justice, the just soul lives well, and living well is being happy. The just life is, therefore, the happiest.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>You play as Socrates against Thrasymachus, who barges in roughly and mixes strong cards with traps. His nameable traps: the ‘no true scotsman’ (whoever rules ‘in the strict sense’ does not err), the ad hominem (you only ask out of vanity), hasty generalisation (a few happy tyrants) and the false dilemma (either you exploit or you are exploited). Your best cards: the band of thieves that needs justice, the craft that serves its object (the doctor, the shepherd) and the function of the soul. Temptation to avoid: answering his contempt with another insult.</p>"
 },
 "hf-atheismusstreit": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 20-21 (German idealism)",
  "title": "The Atheismusstreit (1798): is it atheist to identify God with the moral order?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 20-21 (German idealism). The ‘atheism dispute’ that cost Fichte his chair at Jena: is thinking of God as the living moral order of the world believing in God or denying him?</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the ‘Atheismusstreit’ debate in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>, where you play as Fichte against his accusers.</p>\n<h2>The situation</h2>\n<p>In 1798 Fichte publishes in his journal an essay on the basis of faith. An anonymous pamphlet denounces it as atheism; the government of Saxony threatens to confiscate the journal and punish its readers. Fichte replies. The controversy ends with his departure from the University of Jena.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Fichte: God is the living moral order of the world</h2>\n<blockquote>‘That living and active moral order is itself God; we neither need nor can conceive of any other.’</blockquote>\n<p>The divine, for Fichte, is not a thing or an object that exists ‘out there’ but the moral order in which we trust when we act well: that which gives meaning and effectiveness to duty. Believing in God is living and acting within that order.</p>\n\n<h2>2. The objection: then, how does that differ from having no God?</h2>\n<blockquote>‘A God who is not a substance, nor a person, nor a creator, but only “the moral order” —how does that differ from having no God?’</blockquote>\n<p>This is the opponent’s legitimate objection, and it deserves an answer, not contempt: if everything the tradition attributes to God (personal being, creator, substance) is taken away, it seems nothing is left to call ‘God’.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Fichte: a God-thing would be an idol</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Making God a substance or a person, one being more among beings, lowers him to an idol; denying that idol is not denying God.’</blockquote>\n<p>Fichte turns the accusation around: turning God into a finite object —however great— is already a form of idolatry. He does not deny God: he denies a representation unworthy of God. The moral order is not ‘an idea of mine’; it is the most real thing, that on which all obligation rests.</p>\n\n<h2>4. The traps in the accusation</h2>\n<p>The accusers hardly argue: they distort his thesis (‘he says God is only an idea of ours’ = straw man), they frighten (‘without a personal God, morality, oaths and the State collapse’), they attack the person (‘he is a troublemaking Jacobin’), they appeal to tradition (‘the catechism of all Christendom says otherwise’) and to numbers (‘all decent people are scandalised’). And, above all, they use force: the anonymous pamphlet and the threat of censorship.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Fichte: judge my reasons, not my politics or my label</h2>\n<blockquote>‘If I am wrong, prove it; do not gag me. Punishing an idea with censorship does not refute it.’</blockquote>\n<p>The fundamental reply: an idea can only be defeated by another idea. Calling Fichte ‘atheist’ or ‘Jacobin’ does not decide whether he is right; and silencing a journal does not prove that its content is false.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>You play as Fichte against his accusers. Careful: not everything they say is a trap. The question ‘how does that differ from having no God?’ is legitimate and must be answered (the moral order is the most real thing; a God-thing would be an idol), not dismissed as a fallacy. Their nameable traps: the straw man (God is ‘only an idea’), fear (the State will collapse), the ad hominem (Jacobin), the appeal to tradition (the catechism) and the ad populum (everyone is scandalised); plus force: the anonymous pamphlet and censorship. Temptations to avoid: replying with another ad hominem (‘an ignorant priest’) or with a false dilemma (‘either my whole idealism or you are fanatics’).</p>"
 },
 "hf-eutidemo": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 5 (the Sophists and Socrates)",
  "title": "Socrates against Euthydemus: the art of winning with words",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (the Sophists and Socrates). The <em>Euthydemus</em> is Plato’s great catalogue of verbal traps: two eristic brothers boast of refuting any statement, whatever the other person says, by playing on the double meaning of words.</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the ‘Socrates against Euthydemus and Dionysodorus’ debate in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>. An ideal piece for teaching <strong>equivocation</strong> (amphiboly): refuting a word is not refuting the thing.</p>\n<h2>The situation</h2>\n<p>Two brothers, now elderly, Euthydemus and Dionysodorus, boast of mastering a new art: refuting anyone on anything. They show it off on the young Clinias: whatever his answers, they make him look ridiculous. Socrates watches and, in the end, contrasts these pirouettes with what a true education would be.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Do those who already know learn, or those who do not know?</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Those who learn, are they the wise or the ignorant?’</blockquote>\n<p>Whatever Clinias says, he falls: if ‘the wise’, it seems absurd (they learn what they already know); if ‘the ignorant’, it is too (they learn without understanding). The trick is that ‘to learn’ means both <em>to acquire new knowledge</em> and <em>to understand what one has</em>. Once the double meaning is undone, the paradox disappears.</p>\n\n<h2>2. If you know something, you know everything</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Do you know something? Then you are wise; and whoever is wise, knows. So you know everything.’</blockquote>\n<p>The argument moves from ‘knowing one thing’ to just ‘knowing’, and from there to ‘knowing everything’. Once again it is the same word used in two senses as if it were one.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Your dog is your father</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Do you have a dog, and is he a father of puppies? Then he is a father and he is yours: he is your father. And you are the brother of the puppies.’</blockquote>\n<p>‘He is a father’ (of his puppies) and ‘he is mine’ (as an animal) are true separately, but they cannot be added together into ‘he is my father’. Two senses of the verb ‘to be’ and of the relation ‘father of’ are being confused.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Wanting him to improve is wanting him to die</h2>\n<blockquote>‘You want the young man to become wise, that is, to stop being what he is; you want him to stop being: so you want him to perish!’</blockquote>\n<p>‘Ceasing to be ignorant’ is not ‘ceasing to be’. Wanting someone to become wise is wanting them to go on being themselves and also to know. The equivocation plays on ‘to be’ and ‘to cease to be’.</p>\n\n<h2>5. The siege: answer only ‘yes’ or ‘no’</h2>\n<p>So that the victim cannot distinguish senses, the brothers force them to answer with a flat ‘yes’ or ‘no’ and run them over in turn. Denying nuance is part of the trap: hardly any important word has just one meaning. And they threaten that, whatever answer is given, the victim will fall all the same (false: only those who agree to play without making distinctions fall).</p>\n\n<h2>6. Socrates: you refute the word, not the thing</h2>\n<p>Socrates’ key is not to shout louder but to <em>distinguish</em>. In each trap he shows that the word has been used in two senses; once the senses are separated, the argument collapses by itself. That is why he contrasts <em>eristic</em> (winning the dispute at any price) with <em>dialectic</em> (seeking the truth together): one entertains for a while; the other teaches.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>You play as Socrates against the two brothers. Almost all their cards are the same trick in different disguises: <strong>equivocation</strong> (playing on the double meaning of ‘to learn’, ‘to know’, ‘to be’, ‘father’). Your weapon is not to insult them but to <em>distinguish the two senses</em> of the word in play: once that is done, the trap falls. Also watch for their siege traps: the false dilemma of ‘answer only yes or no’ and the ‘you contradict yourself’ that distorts what you said. Temptations to avoid: calling them ‘fairground swindlers’ (ad hominem) or boxing them into an ‘either wordplay or frauds’ (false dilemma): they would be right that you are falling into the very thing you reproach them for.</p>"
 },
 "hf-criton": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 5 (the Sophists and Socrates)",
  "title": "Socrates against Crito: should one escape an unjust sentence?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (the Sophists and Socrates). The <em>Crito</em>: Socrates, condemned to death, refuses to flee from prison although he could. Is it permissible to disobey a law when the sentence is unjust?</p>\n<p><strong>Use</strong>: source text for the ‘Socrates against Crito’ debate in the game <em>Duel of Reasons</em>. A key piece for political obligation (the ‘contract’ with the laws) and for the principle of not returning injustice for injustice.</p>\n<h2>The situation</h2>\n<p>At dawn, in prison, Crito visits Socrates: the ship whose arrival marks the execution is about to arrive, and he brings money, contacts and a plan to get him out of Athens. Socrates, serene, agrees to examine whether he should flee, but only with reasons.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Crito: think about what people will say</h2>\n<blockquote>‘If you do not escape, people will think that we, your friends, were stingy and cowardly and did not want to spend money to save you.’</blockquote>\n<p>Crito’s first push is the <strong>opinion of the majority</strong> (‘what will people say’) and fear of reproach. He also appeals to the children (you will leave them orphans) and suggests that staying is comfortable and cowardly.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Socrates: not the majority, but whoever knows</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Do we listen to just anyone, or only to the one who understands? The man in training does not follow the praise of the crowd, but the doctor or the trainer.’</blockquote>\n<p>In what concerns the body we follow the expert, not the mass; in what concerns the just and the unjust —which is more important— we do the same. The opinion of the many does not decide what is just; it can kill, but it has no authority over the truth.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Socrates: one must never commit injustice</h2>\n<blockquote>‘In no way must one commit injustice, not even return injustice for injustice, nor evil for evil, even if one has received it.’</blockquote>\n<p>The question is not what people will say or what suits us, but whether fleeing is just. And since one must never do wrong, we must see whether escaping harms someone who does not deserve it.</p>\n\n<h2>4. The Laws of Athens speak</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Do you think a city can survive in which the sentences passed have no force, but private individuals annul them?’</blockquote>\n<p>Socrates imagines the Laws addressing him: fleeing would be destroying them. He was born, raised and lived under them; he could have left if he did not like them, and he did not: by that he accepted a <em>pact</em> to obey them. The Laws are like parents: one does not repay them with an injury. Breaking the pact now, only because the sentence harms him, would be to prove right those who say he is a destroyer of laws.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Children and friends</h2>\n<p>As for the children, the Laws say, they will be raised better by someone who leaves them a good example and good friends than by a father who flees, breaking his pacts. And escaping would not save friendship: it would put his friends in danger and stain his memory. Socrates stays.</p>\n\n<h2>For the debate (Duel of Reasons)</h2>\n<p>You play as Socrates against Crito, who is not an enemy but a friend who wants to save you: he mixes legitimate offers (there are means, friends are willing, your death benefits your enemies) with fallacious pushes. His nameable traps: the ad populum (‘people will say’), the appeal to pity (the orphaned children) and the false dilemma (either you flee or you hand them victory). Your best cards: listening to whoever knows and not the majority, the principle of not returning injustice for injustice, and the pact with the Laws. Temptations to avoid: calling Crito a coward (ad hominem) or posing a false dilemma yourself; a friend is answered with reasons, not contempt.</p>"
 },
 "hf-lec-agustin-vida-feliz": {
  "subject": "hf",
  "tema": "History of Philosophy · Topic 12 (faith and reason)",
  "title": "Saint Augustine, On the Happy Life (ch. 2, §8, 10 and 11)",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 12 (faith and reason). Saint Augustine: happiness lies not in the goods of fortune but in possessing God.</p>\n<h2>To set the scene</h2>\n<p>Augustine wrote <em>On the Happy Life</em> in the autumn of 386, in a country house near Milan, shortly before his baptism. It is a dialogue set during his birthday, in the form of a banquet: Augustine, his mother Monica and the young Trygetius and Licentius speak. Sections 8, 10 and 11 of chapter 2 are included here (9 is left out); the numbers in square brackets order the paragraphs of each section.</p>\n\n<h2>Section 8</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[1] —And the soul, what do you say? —I asked them—. Will it not also have its food? Do you not think that knowledge is its main nourishment?</p>\n<p>—Yes, of course —said my mother—, because the soul is fed by nothing other than the understanding and knowledge of things.</p>\n<p>[2] As Trygetius was not entirely convinced by that statement, my mother said to him:</p>\n<p>—And haven’t you yourself explained today how and from where the soul is nourished? Shortly after you sat down to eat you said that you had not noticed the cup we were using, because your head was lost in other matters; and yet neither your hands nor your mouth stopped. Where was your mind, your soul, then, while you ate without paying attention? Believe me: at that moment too the soul was feeding on its own food —its imaginings and thoughts—, eager to grasp something.</p>\n<p>[3] These words set off a discussion, and I said to them:</p>\n<p>—Will you not grant me that the souls of great scholars and students are, in their own order, much richer and broader than those of people who lack knowledge?</p>\n<p>—That is clear —they all answered at once.</p>\n<p>—With good reason, then, we can say that the souls of people without discipline or sound learning are hungry and empty.</p>\n<p>—I think —said Trygetius— that their souls are full, but of evils and wickedness.</p>\n<p>[4] —Exactly that —I said to him—. Do not doubt it: that is sterility and hunger of the soul. For just as bodies deprived of food often fall ill and fill with ulcers as a result of hunger, in the same way their souls are full of diseases, which are the sign of their fasting. Indeed, the ancients considered nequitia, or wickedness, the mother of all vices, because it is something that is nothing. And the virtue opposed to that vice is called frugality (<em>frugalitas</em>). Just as that word comes from <em>frux</em>, that is, ‘fruit’, to express a kind of spiritual fertility, the other, nequitia, comes from sterility, from nothingness, because nothingness is what flows away, dissolves, liquefies, and always dies and is lost. That is why we also call such people ‘lost’. On the other hand, what is truly something remains, stands firm, is always the same, like virtue, whose principal and noblest part is frugality and temperance. But if what I have said seems incomprehensible to you, you will surely grant me that, if the ignorant have their souls full, then for both bodies and souls there are two kinds of food: some healthy and beneficial, and others deadly and harmful.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Section 10</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[1] Then, taking up my discourse again, I continued:</p>\n<p>—Do we all want to be happy?</p>\n<p>As soon as I had finished the sentence, they all said yes with one voice.</p>\n<p>—And is a person happy when they do not have what they desire?</p>\n<p>—No —they all replied.</p>\n<p>—And will the person who has everything they want be happy?</p>\n<p>Then my mother replied:</p>\n<p>—If they want good things and get them, yes; but if they want bad things, even if they get them, they will be wretched.</p>\n<p>[2] Smiling and glad, I said to her:</p>\n<p>—Mother, you have conquered the fortress of philosophy! Cicero, in the book entitled <em>Hortensius</em>, made the defence and praise of philosophy, and you only lack his words to express it as he did. Here is what everyone says —without being philosophers— and are even ready to argue for: that those who live as they wish are happy. A grave error! For the height of wretchedness is to have an unsuitable desire. To obtain something unsuitable is far more unfortunate than not obtaining something one desires. For a twisted will brings many more misfortunes than good luck brings benefits.</p>\n<p>[3] My mother approved these words with lively exclamations, and for a moment we forgot that she was a woman, convinced that we were before a great sage. I, as far as my strength allowed, wondered from what divine source she drank such profound truths.</p>\n<p>—Tell us now —said Licentius— what the person who seeks happiness should desire and in what things they should find their satisfaction.</p>\n<p>—On your birthday you will invite me, and I will accept with great affection everything you offer me. In the same way I want you today to sit at the banquet in my house, without asking for what has not been prepared.</p>\n<p>[4] He was left ashamed and repentant, and I added:</p>\n<p>—On one thing we agree: a person cannot be happy who lacks what they want, but neither is one simply happy who has everything they want. Is that not so?</p>\n<p>They all accepted it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Section 11</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[1] —Answer me now: whoever is not happy, is that person wretched?</p>\n<p>They all agreed without hesitation.</p>\n<p>—Therefore, everyone who does not have what they want is wretched.</p>\n<p>They all accepted it.</p>\n<p>—What, then, should the human being seek in order to attain happiness? This dish too will not be lacking at our banquet to satisfy Licentius’s hunger, for, in my opinion, it must be something that it is enough to want in order to obtain it.</p>\n<p>They all saw it clearly.</p>\n<p>[2] —Therefore —I said—, it must be something permanent and secure, which does not depend on fortune nor is subject to the ups and downs of life. For what is passing and mortal cannot be had as one wishes, nor at the moment one wishes.</p>\n<p>They all accepted it, but Trygetius said:</p>\n<p>—Fortunately, there are those who possess in abundance passing and pleasant things that fortune has given them and that satisfy all their desires.</p>\n<p>—And what do you say —I asked him— of someone who fears losing something? Are they happy?</p>\n<p>—Not at all.</p>\n<p>—And can someone live without fear who can lose what they love?</p>\n<p>—No, they cannot —he replied.</p>\n<p>—The things that fortune gives can be lost; therefore, those who possess and love them cannot be happy.</p>\n<p>[3] Trygetius admitted it. Then my mother said:</p>\n<p>—Even if they were sure of not losing those goods, those goods still cannot give them true satiety; and the more needy they are, the more wretched they will be.</p>\n<p>I replied to him:</p>\n<p>—And what do you say of someone who lives surrounded by those goods, but has set a limit and measure to their desire and lives with temperance? Does that person not seem happy to you?</p>\n<p>—They will not be happy because of those goods —said my mother—, but because they use them with moderation.</p>\n<p>[4] —Very good —I said to her—; my question admitted no other answer, and you could not have answered otherwise. Let us conclude, then, that whoever wants to be happy must seek permanent goods, which no blow of fortune can snatch away.</p>\n<p>—We have long possessed that truth —said Trygetius.</p>\n<p>—Do you think that God is eternal and permanent?</p>\n<p>—That is so evident —said Licentius— that it would not even be necessary to ask.</p>\n<p>All the others also accepted it with devotion.</p>\n<p>—Therefore, whoever possesses God is happy.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<h2>Logical analysis of some sentences</h2>\n<p>In the Logic Corner (Classic Arguments tab) there is an analysis of several sentences from this text: their logical form, their Euler diagram and their truth table.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/clasicos/monica\">Monica (section 10): ‘If someone desires good things and has them, they are happy’</a>: a sufficient condition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/clasicos/agustin\">Augustine (section 11): ‘Only the person who desires what they cannot lose can attain happiness’</a>: a necessary condition, and the trap that ‘only’ is not ‘all’.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/clasicos/agustin2\">Variant (sections 10 and 11): ‘…which is the end that we all necessarily seek’</a>: the literal reading and that of ‘whoever possesses God is happy’, with the circle of the unhappy.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/ejercicios/contrafactico\">A classroom sentence that argues with the text: ‘…they would be happy if they could not stop desiring it’</a>: one can lose the desire, not just the object.</li>\n</ul>"
 }
};
